window.GUIDE_HTML={"fbs-physiology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Homeostasis &amp; control systems</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Homeostasis</b> = keeping the <b>internal environment (ECF)</b> constant (Claude Bernard’s “milieu intérieur”).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Negative feedback</span> = response <i>opposes</i> the change → stability. Most body systems (BP via baroreceptors, temperature, blood glucose, PTH–Ca²⁺, ADH–osmolarity).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Positive feedback</span> = response <i>amplifies</i> the change. Physiologic examples: <span class=\"hy\">LH surge (estrogen), oxytocin in labor (Ferguson reflex), clotting cascade, action potential upstroke (Na⁺ channel opening), lactation</span>. Pathologic: “vicious cycle” in hemorrhagic shock (cardiac failure → ↓ perfusion → worse failure).</li>\n<li><b>Feedforward</b> = anticipatory response before the variable changes (e.g., ↑ HR before exercise, cephalic phase of digestion).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gain = correction ÷ remaining error</span>. Higher gain = better control. Example: BP pushed +100, baroreflex returns to +25 → correction 75 / error 25 → gain = −3 (negative = negative feedback). Temperature control has very high gain (~−33).</li>\n<li><b>Baroreceptor reflex</b>: carotid sinus (CN IX) &amp; aortic arch (CN X) stretch → ↑ BP ⇒ ↑ firing ⇒ ↑ vagal, ↓ sympathetic ⇒ ↓ HR, vasodilation. <span class=\"hy\">Fast, short-term BP control; resets in chronic HTN.</span> Carotid massage ↑ firing ⇒ ↓ HR.</li>\n<li>Chemoreceptors (carotid/aortic bodies) sense ↓ PO₂, ↑ PCO₂, ↓ pH.</li>\n<li>Kidneys = long-term BP control (pressure natriuresis, RAAS); infinite gain over time.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Body fluid compartments</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Compartment</th><th>% body weight</th><th>Fraction</th><th>Measured with</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Total body water (TBW)</b></td><td class=\"hy\">≈ 60% (60–40–20 rule)</td><td>—</td><td>D₂O, tritiated water, antipyrine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ICF</td><td>40%</td><td class=\"hy\">2/3 of TBW</td><td>TBW − ECF</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ECF</td><td>20%</td><td class=\"hy\">1/3 of TBW</td><td>Inulin, mannitol, ²²Na</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interstitial fluid</td><td>15%</td><td class=\"hy\">3/4 of ECF</td><td>ECF − plasma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plasma</td><td>5%</td><td class=\"hy\">1/4 of ECF</td><td>Evans blue, ¹²⁵I-albumin</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood volume = plasma volume ÷ (1 − Hct). TBW is <b>lower in women, obese, elderly</b> (fat has little water); higher in infants (~75%).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Main ions: ECF = Na⁺, Cl⁻, HCO₃⁻. ICF = K⁺, Mg²⁺, phosphates, proteins.</span> Plasma has more protein than interstitial fluid (Gibbs–Donnan effect: slightly more cations in plasma).</li>\n<li><b>Osmolarity</b> (osm/L solution) vs <b>osmolality</b> (osm/kg water); normal ≈ <span class=\"hy\">285–295 mOsm/kg</span>. Plasma Osm ≈ 2[Na⁺] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8.</li>\n<li><b>Tonicity</b> = effective osmolarity (only <i>non-penetrating</i> solutes count). <span class=\"hy\">Urea is an ineffective osmole</span> (crosses membranes) → isosmotic urea solution is <i>hypotonic</i> → cells swell.</li>\n<li>Cell in <b>hypotonic</b> solution → water in → swells/lyses. <b>Hypertonic</b> → water out → shrinks (crenation). 0.9% NaCl &amp; 5% dextrose (initially) are isotonic.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Water always moves toward higher osmolarity; disturbances start in the ECF, and the ICF changes only secondarily by osmosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Darrow–Yannet diagrams (volume/osmolarity shifts) — MUST KNOW</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Example</th><th>ECF vol</th><th>ICF vol</th><th>Osmolarity</th><th>Hct / plasma protein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Isosmotic expansion</td><td>Isotonic saline infusion</td><td>↑</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>↓ / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Isosmotic contraction</td><td>Diarrhea, burns, vomiting</td><td>↓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>↑ / ↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hyperosmotic expansion</td><td>High NaCl intake, hypertonic saline</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↓ / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyperosmotic contraction</td><td>Sweating, fever, diabetes insipidus</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>— / ↑</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyposmotic expansion</td><td>SIADH, water intoxication</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>— / ↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyposmotic contraction</td><td>Adrenal insufficiency (↓ aldosterone)</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td><td>↑ / ↑</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Hct logic:</span> Hct = RBC volume ÷ (RBC + plasma). Losing plasma alone (diarrhea) ↑ Hct. RBCs act like ICF: they shrink when ECF osmolarity ↑ and swell when it ↓, which cancels or adds to the plasma change. Acute hemorrhage (whole blood lost) → ECF ↓ but Hct unchanged at first.</p>\n<ul><li>Heavy sweating → <b>ADH ↑</b>, urine output drops (≈1,500 → 500 mL/day), thirst ↑.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyponatremia</span> = too much water relative to Na⁺ (dilutional), e.g., SIADH → brain swelling (confusion, seizures). Correct slowly (osmotic demyelination).</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Starling forces &amp; edema</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Net filtration = Kf [(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</span>. Pc pushes out; πc (plasma oncotic, mainly <b>albumin</b>) pulls in.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Edema causes: ↑ Pc (heart failure, venous obstruction, DVT), ↓ πc (nephrotic syndrome, liver cirrhosis, malnutrition/kwashiorkor), ↑ Kf/permeability (burns, inflammation, sepsis), lymphatic obstruction (filariasis, cancer, node dissection → non-pitting).</span></li>\n<li>Safety factors against edema: low interstitial compliance, ↑ lymph flow (up to 10–50×), washout of interstitial protein.</li>\n<li>Lymph returns ~2–4 L/day of filtered fluid and protein; thoracic duct → left subclavian/IJ junction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Membrane transport</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Energy</th><th>Carrier?</th><th>Saturable?</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Simple diffusion</td><td>None (down gradient)</td><td>No</td><td>No (linear)</td><td>O₂, CO₂, steroids, ethanol, urea (some)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Facilitated diffusion</td><td>None</td><td>Yes</td><td class=\"hy\">Yes (Tm)</td><td class=\"hy\">GLUT1–5 (GLUT4 insulin-dependent: muscle, fat)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Channels</td><td>None</td><td>Pore</td><td>Can saturate at very high conc.</td><td>Na⁺/K⁺/Ca²⁺ channels, aquaporins</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Primary active</td><td>ATP directly</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Ca²⁺-ATPase (SERCA, PMCA), H⁺/K⁺-ATPase</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Secondary active</td><td>Na⁺ gradient (ATP indirectly)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>SGLT (symport), Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger NCX &amp; Na⁺/H⁺ exchanger NHE (antiport)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fick’s law</b>: diffusion rate ∝ (area × concentration gradient × permeability)/thickness. Lipid solubility ↑ permeability; size and charge ↓ it. <span class=\"hy\">Ions can’t cross the bilayer because they are charged/hydrated, not lipid-soluble.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase: 3 Na⁺ OUT, 2 K⁺ IN per ATP</span> <span class=\"mn\">(NOKIA: Na⁺ Out, K⁺ In, ATPase)</span>. Basolateral in epithelia. Electrogenic (net +1 out) → contributes a few mV to RMP. Inhibited by <span class=\"hy\">digoxin/ouabain</span>.</li>\n<li><b>Symporter</b> = same direction (SGLT: Na⁺ + glucose IN). <b>Antiporter</b> = opposite directions (NCX: 3 Na⁺ in, 1 Ca²⁺ out; NHE: Na⁺ in, H⁺ out).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Proximal tubule glucose reabsorption order: (1) basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pumps Na⁺ out → (2) low intracellular Na⁺ creates gradient → (3) apical SGLT2 drags glucose in against its gradient → (4) GLUT2 lets glucose out basolaterally.</span></li>\n<li>Glucose transport saturates: renal threshold ≈ <b>200 mg/dL</b>, Tm ≈ 375 mg/min → glucosuria in diabetes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">SGLT2 inhibitors (-gliflozins)</span> → glucosuria; side effects UTIs, genital yeast infection, euglycemic DKA, volume depletion.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Blocking the Na⁺/K⁺ pump (or ischemia/no ATP) → pump fails FIRST, secondary transport (SGLT) fails second</span>, cells swell (Na⁺ + water in).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Digitalis (digoxin) cascade — classic NBME:</b> blocks Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase → ↑ intracellular Na⁺ → weaker Na⁺ gradient → NCX removes less Ca²⁺ → ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ → more SR Ca²⁺ → <b>↑ contractility (positive inotropy)</b>. Also ↑ vagal tone (slows AV node). <b>Hypokalemia potentiates toxicity</b> (K⁺ competes for the same site). Toxicity: nausea, yellow-green vision, arrhythmias; antidote = digoxin Fab.</div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Osmosis</b>: water through aquaporins toward higher osmolarity. Osmotic pressure π = g·C·σ·RT. Reflection coefficient σ: 1 = impermeant (albumin), 0 = freely permeant (urea).</li>\n<li><b>Vesicular transport</b>: endocytosis (phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor-mediated via clathrin — LDL), exocytosis (Ca²⁺-triggered, SNAREs), transcytosis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Resting membrane potential (RMP)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">At rest the membrane is most permeable to K⁺ (K⁺ leak channels) → RMP ≈ −70 to −90 mV, close to E<sub>K</sub>.</span> The Na⁺/K⁺ pump <i>maintains</i> gradients (and adds ~−4 mV) but is not what directly sets RMP.</li>\n<li><b>Nernst equation</b> (single ion at 37 °C): E = (61.5/z) log([out]/[in]).</li>\n<li><b>Goldman (GHK) equation</b>: RMP = weighted average of E for each ion by its permeability → moves toward the ion with highest permeability.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Ion</th><th>[ECF]</th><th>[ICF]</th><th>Equilibrium potential (deck values)</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>K⁺</td><td>4–5 mM</td><td>140–150 mM</td><td>≈ −95 mV</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Na⁺</td><td>140–145 mM</td><td>10–15 mM</td><td>≈ +65 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cl⁻</td><td>~105–110 mM</td><td>~5–10 mM</td><td>≈ −90 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺</td><td>~2.5 mM</td><td>~0.0001 mM</td><td>≈ +120 mV</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyperkalemia → smaller K⁺ gradient → less K⁺ efflux → depolarized RMP (closer to threshold)</span>. Initially excitable, but sustained depolarization inactivates Na⁺ channels → weakness, <b>peaked T waves, wide QRS, arrhythmias</b>. Treat: <b>IV calcium gluconate</b> (stabilizes membrane), insulin + glucose, β₂-agonists (shift K⁺ in), binders, dialysis.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hypokalemia → hyperpolarized RMP (further from threshold)</span> → muscle weakness, cramps, ileus, flat T waves, <b>U waves</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Calcium sets the THRESHOLD (stabilizes Na⁺ channels).</span> <b>Hypocalcemia</b> → Na⁺ channels open with less depolarization → threshold drops toward RMP → spontaneous firing → <span class=\"hy\">tetany, paresthesias, Chvostek &amp; Trousseau signs</span> (e.g., after thyroidectomy/hypoparathyroidism). <b>Hypercalcemia</b> → threshold ↑ → ↓ excitability, <b>sluggish reflexes</b>, weakness <span class=\"mn\">(stones, bones, groans, thrones, psychiatric overtones)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Action potentials &amp; conduction</h2>\n<h3>Phases (nerve / skeletal muscle)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Threshold</b> (~−55 mV) → voltage-gated Na⁺ channel <b>activation (m) gates</b> open (positive feedback) → <span class=\"hy\">upstroke = Na⁺ influx</span>; overshoot to ~+35 mV.</li>\n<li>At peak, <span class=\"hy\">Na⁺ inactivation (h) gates close</span> → Na⁺ entry stops; they stay closed until repolarization → <b>absolute refractory period</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Repolarization = K⁺ efflux</span> through delayed-rectifier voltage-gated K⁺ channels; slow closure → <b>after-hyperpolarization</b> (undershoot toward E<sub>K</sub>).</li>\n<li><b>Relative refractory period</b>: some Na⁺ channels recovered; a stronger-than-normal stimulus can fire.</li>\n<li><b>All-or-none</b>; stimulus intensity is coded by <b>frequency</b> of APs, not amplitude.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Accommodation</span>: slow depolarization (e.g., hyperkalemia) inactivates Na⁺ channels before threshold → no AP even though Vm passes threshold.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Toxin / drug</th><th>Target</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tetrodotoxin (pufferfish), saxitoxin (red tide)</td><td>Voltage-gated Na⁺ channel (from outside)</td><td>No upstroke → no AP, paralysis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tetraethylammonium (TEA)</td><td>Voltage-gated K⁺ channels (from inside)</td><td>Impaired repolarization → prolonged AP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Local anesthetics (lidocaine)</td><td>Na⁺ channel (from inside, use-dependent)</td><td>Block conduction; small, myelinated pain fibers first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ciguatoxin / batrachotoxin</td><td>Hold Na⁺ channels open</td><td>Persistent depolarization</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Conduction velocity</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">↑ velocity with ↑ diameter (↓ internal resistance) and myelination.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Myelin ↓ membrane capacitance and ↑ membrane resistance</span> → less charge needed to change Vm, current spreads farther → <b>saltatory conduction</b> node to node (Na⁺ channels concentrated at nodes of Ranvier).</li>\n<li>Length constant λ = √(Rm/Ri): large λ = signal spreads farther. Time constant τ = Rm × Cm: small τ = faster depolarization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Demyelination (MS — CNS oligodendrocytes; Guillain–Barré — PNS Schwann cells) ↑ capacitance, current leaks → slowed/blocked conduction.</span></li>\n<li>Fiber types: Aα (largest, fastest: proprioception, motor) &gt; Aβ (touch) &gt; Aγ &gt; Aδ (fast pain, cold) &gt; B (preganglionic autonomic) &gt; C (unmyelinated, slow pain, postganglionic).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Graded vs action potentials</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Graded (local) potential</th><th>Action potential</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Size ∝ stimulus; summate (spatial/temporal)</td><td>All-or-none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Decay with distance; no refractory period</td><td>Propagated without decrement; refractory period</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EPSP/IPSP, receptor/end-plate potentials</td><td>Axon, muscle fibers</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Na⁺/K⁺ pump: 3 out, 2 in, primary active, basolateral; digoxin &amp; ouabain block it.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">SGLT = secondary active symport (apical); GLUT = facilitated diffusion, no ATP.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">RMP ≈ E<sub>K</sub> because K⁺ permeability dominates; ECF K⁺ is the main determinant of RMP stability.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">TTX blocks Na⁺ (no upstroke); TEA blocks K⁺ (no repolarization).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Diarrhea = isosmotic contraction (↑ Hct). SIADH = hyposmotic expansion. Adrenal insufficiency = hyposmotic contraction. Sweating = hyperosmotic contraction.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Nephrotic syndrome / cirrhosis → ↓ albumin → ↓ πc → edema. Heart failure → ↑ Pc → edema.</li>\n<li>ICF 2/3, ECF 1/3; interstitial 3/4 of ECF, plasma 1/4.</li>\n<li>Positive feedback examples: LH surge, oxytocin, clotting, Na⁺ channel opening in AP upstroke.</li>\n<li>Hypocalcemia → tetany (↓ threshold). Hypercalcemia → sluggish reflexes.</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-cellbio": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Organelles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cytosol</b> = fluid portion only; <b>cytoplasm</b> = cytosol + organelles + inclusions (everything inside the plasma membrane except the nucleus).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Nucleus</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Double membrane (outer continuous with RER), <b>nuclear pores</b> (bidirectional transport; mRNA export, protein import via NLS). Blocking pores → mRNA can’t reach ribosomes → no translation.</li>\n<li><b>Nuclear lamina</b> (lamins A, B, C — type V intermediate filaments) maintains shape; LINC complexes connect it to the cytoskeleton. <span class=\"hy\">Lamin A mutation → progeria (Hutchinson–Gilford).</span></li>\n<li><b>Nucleolus</b> = site of <span class=\"hy\">rRNA synthesis (RNA polymerase I)</span> and ribosomal subunit assembly. RNA Pol II = mRNA; Pol III = tRNA, 5S rRNA.</li>\n<li>mRNA path: transcription (nucleus) → capping, splicing, poly-A tail → export via pores → translation on ribosomes.</li>\n<li>Heterochromatin = condensed, inactive (Barr body); euchromatin = open, active.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ribosomes, RER, Golgi</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Eukaryotic 80S = 60S + 40S (prokaryotic 70S = 50S + 30S → antibiotic targets).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Signal sequence + SRP decide fate</span>: SRP binds the N-terminal signal peptide, pauses translation, docks the ribosome on the RER (SRP receptor) → protein enters ER lumen via translocon (Sec61).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">RER makes secretory, membrane and lysosomal proteins</span> (and N-linked glycosylation begins). <b>Free ribosomes</b> make cytosolic, nuclear, mitochondrial and <span class=\"hy\">peroxisomal</span> proteins.</li>\n<li>Cells rich in RER: plasma cells, pancreatic acinar cells, goblet cells; Nissl bodies in neurons.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">SER: steroid hormone synthesis (adrenal cortex, Leydig cells, gonads), lipid synthesis, detoxification (hepatocyte cytochrome P450), glycogen breakdown (glucose-6-phosphatase), Ca²⁺ storage (sarcoplasmic reticulum in muscle).</span></li>\n<li><b>Golgi</b>: cis (receives from ER) → trans (ships out). Modifies N-glycans, adds <b>O-linked sugars</b> (Ser/Thr), sulfation of GAGs, proteolytic processing, <span class=\"hy\">adds mannose-6-phosphate (M6P) to lysosomal enzymes</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Coat</th><th>Direction</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>COPII</td><td>Anterograde: ER → cis-Golgi <span class=\"mn\">(“2 = forward to Golgi”)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>COPI</td><td>Retrograde: Golgi → ER (and intra-Golgi), KDEL retrieval</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Clathrin</td><td>trans-Golgi → lysosomes; plasma membrane → endosomes (receptor-mediated endocytosis, e.g., LDL)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Insulin processing (Anki):</b> preproinsulin (RER, signal peptide cleaved) → proinsulin (folds, 3 disulfides) → Golgi/secretory granules: prohormone convertases cut out <b>C-peptide</b> → insulin + C-peptide secreted 1:1. <b>C-peptide</b> distinguishes endogenous insulin (insulinoma, sulfonylureas: ↑ C-peptide) from exogenous insulin (factitious: ↓ C-peptide). ↑ proinsulin → defective convertase processing.</div>\n<h3>Lysosomes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Acid hydrolases (pH ≈ 4.5–5, H⁺-ATPase); digest by endocytosis, phagocytosis, autophagy.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Trafficking: RER synthesis → cis-Golgi GlcNAc-phosphotransferase adds M6P → M6P receptor in trans-Golgi → clathrin vesicle → late endosome → lysosome.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">I-cell disease (mucolipidosis II)</span>: defective GlcNAc-phosphotransferase → no M6P tag → enzymes secreted → <span class=\"hy\">↑ plasma lysosomal enzymes</span>, inclusion bodies, coarse facies, gingival hyperplasia, restricted joints, clouded corneas; AR, fatal in childhood.</li>\n<li>Classic storage diseases (Tay–Sachs, Gaucher, Niemann–Pick, Fabry, Hurler, Hunter, metachromatic leukodystrophy) = a <b>single enzyme</b> deficient; I-cell = <b>all</b> enzymes mis-sorted.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Enzyme</th><th>Key feature</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tay–Sachs</td><td>Hexosaminidase A (GM2)</td><td>Cherry-red macula, no hepatosplenomegaly, onion-skin lysosomes</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Gaucher</td><td>Glucocerebrosidase</td><td>Most common; crumpled tissue-paper macrophages, bone crises, HSM</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Niemann–Pick</td><td>Sphingomyelinase</td><td>Cherry-red macula + HSM, foamy cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fabry (XR)</td><td>α-galactosidase A</td><td>Neuropathic pain, angiokeratomas, renal/cardiac disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hurler / Hunter (XR)</td><td>α-L-iduronidase / iduronate sulfatase</td><td>GAG accumulation; corneal clouding in Hurler only <span class=\"mn\">(Hunters aim for the X)</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</td><td>Arylsulfatase A</td><td>Demyelination</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Mitochondria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Double membrane; inner membrane folds = <b>cristae</b> (↑ surface for ETC/ATP synthase). Matrix: TCA cycle, β-oxidation, own circular DNA.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">mtDNA: ~37 genes, maternal inheritance, heteroplasmy</span> (mixed normal/mutant copies) → variable severity between siblings and tissues; threshold effect.</li>\n<li>Endosymbiotic evidence: own circular DNA, 70S-like ribosomes, binary fission, double membrane, cardiolipin.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Mitochondrial myopathy: ragged-red fibers</span> (Gomori trichrome); lactic acidosis; tissues with high ATP demand (brain, muscle, eye) affected.</li>\n<li>MELAS (encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, stroke-like episodes), MERRF (myoclonic epilepsy, ragged-red fibers), <span class=\"hy\">LHON (Leber hereditary optic neuropathy — sudden bilateral vision loss in young men; affected males cannot pass it on)</span>.</li>\n<li>Apoptosis: <span class=\"hy\">cytochrome c release</span> (outer membrane permeabilized by Bax/Bak) → apoptosome.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Peroxisomes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Single membrane; proteins made on <b>free ribosomes</b>, imported via PTS1 (C-terminal SKL) and <b>peroxins (PEX)</b>; replicate by fission; no DNA.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Functions: β-oxidation of very-long-chain fatty acids (VLCFA) and branched-chain (α-oxidation of phytanic acid), plasmalogen synthesis (myelin), bile acid synthesis, H₂O₂ production + catalase breakdown.</span></li>\n<li>Peroxisomal β-oxidation makes heat + H₂O₂ (no ATP); shortened chains go to mitochondria to finish.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Zellweger syndrome</span>: PEX gene defect → no functional peroxisomes; hypotonia, seizures, hepatomegaly, craniofacial anomalies, early death.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy</span>: ABCD1 transporter defect → VLCFA buildup → adrenal insufficiency + progressive demyelination in boys.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Refsum disease</span>: phytanic acid accumulation (α-oxidation defect) → ataxia, retinitis pigmentosa, ichthyosis, neuropathy; restrict dietary phytanic acid (dairy, ruminant fat).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Plasma membrane</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Functions: barrier, transport, signaling, cell recognition/adhesion. Basic structure = <b>phospholipid bilayer</b> (amphipathic: polar heads, nonpolar tails; held by hydrophobic interactions).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Outer leaflet: phosphatidylcholine, sphingomyelin, glycolipids. Inner leaflet: phosphatidylserine (PS), phosphatidylethanolamine, phosphatidylinositol.</span> PS flipping to the outside = “eat me” signal in apoptosis (annexin V detects it).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cholesterol = fluidity buffer</span>: at high temperature ↓ fluidity (restrains tails); at low temperature ↑ fluidity (prevents packing).</li>\n<li>Unsaturated (kinked) fatty acids &amp; short chains ↑ fluidity; saturated &amp; long chains and cold ↓ fluidity (hypothermia → stiff membranes).</li>\n<li><b>Glycolipids</b>: sugars face outside; recognition. <span class=\"hy\">Cholera toxin binds ganglioside GM1</span> → ↑ cAMP → secretory diarrhea.</li>\n<li><b>Proteins</b>: integral (transmembrane α-helices or β-barrels; channels, carriers, receptors), peripheral (non-covalent, e.g., spectrin, cytochrome c), lipid-anchored (Ras via prenylation; GPI-anchored proteins).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ABO blood type</span>: glycosyltransferases add sugars to H antigen — A adds GalNAc, B adds galactose, <b>O has no active transferase</b> (bare H antigen). Bombay phenotype lacks H.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ABC transporters (P-glycoprotein/MDR1)</span> pump drugs out using ATP → chemotherapy resistance; at the blood–brain barrier they keep many drugs out of the CNS.</li>\n<li><b>Glycocalyx</b>: carbohydrate coat — protection, recognition, endothelial anti-adhesion, enterocyte brush-border enzymes.</li>\n<li><b>Lipid rafts</b>: cholesterol + sphingolipid-rich, less fluid microdomains for signaling. <b>Caveolae</b> (caveolin) = flask-shaped rafts; endocytosis/transcytosis, abundant in endothelium, adipocytes, smooth muscle; absent in neurons/RBCs.</li>\n<li><b>Polarity</b>: apical vs basolateral domains separated by <span class=\"hy\">tight junctions (claudins, occludin)</span>; classic example = intestinal/renal epithelium.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Cytoskeleton</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Microfilaments (actin)</th><th>Intermediate filaments</th><th>Microtubules</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Diameter</td><td>~7 nm (smallest)</td><td>~10 nm</td><td>~25 nm (largest, hollow)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subunit</td><td>G-actin → F-actin</td><td>Tissue-specific proteins</td><td>α/β-tubulin dimers, 13 protofilaments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td class=\"hy\">ATP; treadmilling</td><td>None; very stable</td><td class=\"hy\">GTP; dynamic instability</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Polarity</td><td>Yes (+ barbed)</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (− at centrosome, + at periphery)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Functions</td><td>Microvilli, contractile ring, cell motility, adherens junctions</td><td class=\"hy\">Tensile strength; tumor markers</td><td>Mitotic spindle, cilia/flagella, axonal transport</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Motors</td><td>Myosin</td><td>—</td><td class=\"hy\">Kinesin (+ end, anterograde), dynein (− end, retrograde)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Actin drugs</b>: cytochalasin (blocks polymerization), phalloidin (stabilizes).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Spectrin–ankyrin–band 3–protein 4.1</span> = RBC membrane skeleton. <span class=\"hy\">Hereditary spherocytosis</span> (usually AD): spherocytes without central pallor, ↑ MCHC, splenomegaly, hemolytic anemia, gallstones; <b>osmotic fragility / EMA binding test</b>; splenectomy.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Dystrophin</span> links actin to the dystroglycan complex/ECM in muscle. <span class=\"hy\">Duchenne</span> (X-linked, frameshift deletion → no dystrophin): proximal weakness by age ~5, <b>Gowers sign</b>, calf pseudohypertrophy, ↑ CK, dilated cardiomyopathy. <b>Becker</b>: in-frame deletion → truncated dystrophin, milder, later onset.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Intermediate filament</th><th>Tissue / tumor marker</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Cytokeratin</td><td>Epithelium → carcinomas</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Vimentin</td><td>Mesenchyme (fibroblasts, endothelium) → sarcomas</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Desmin</td><td>Muscle → rhabdo/leiomyosarcoma</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>GFAP</td><td>Astrocytes/glia → gliomas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neurofilaments</td><td>Neurons → neuronal tumors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lamins</td><td>Nuclear envelope (all cells)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Microtubule drugs</span>: <b>vincristine/vinblastine</b> block polymerization; <b>paclitaxel (taxanes)</b> stabilize (block depolymerization) <span class=\"mn\">(“Taxes stabilize society”)</span>; <b>colchicine</b> blocks polymerization (gout, ↓ neutrophil migration); griseofulvin; mebendazole. All arrest mitosis (M phase). <span class=\"hy\">Vincristine → peripheral neuropathy</span> (axonal transport depends on microtubules); vinblastine → bone-marrow suppression.</li>\n<li>Kinesin/dynein: rabies, HSV, polio and tetanus toxin travel <b>retrograde via dynein</b> to the cell body.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cilia: 9+2 axoneme</span> (motile: airway, fallopian tube, ependyma) with <b>dynein arms</b>; basal body = 9 triplets; primary (nonmotile, 9+0) cilia sense (kidney tubule flow, photoreceptors, embryonic node). Ciliopathies: polycystic kidney disease, Bardet–Biedl.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Kartagener syndrome (primary ciliary dyskinesia)</span>: AR dynein arm defect → infertility (immotile sperm; ↑ ectopic pregnancy), recurrent sinusitis, bronchiectasis, <b>situs inversus</b> (~50%); ↓ nasal NO.</li>\n<li><b>Centrosome</b> (pair of centrioles, γ-tubulin) = MTOC; organizes spindle. Kinetochore microtubules attach to chromosomes; polar/interpolar push poles apart; astral anchor to cortex.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Cell cycle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Phases: <b>G1</b> (growth; variable length) → <b>S</b> (DNA replication: DNA content doubles 2c → 4c, still 2n) → <b>G2</b> (prep, check DNA) → <b>M</b> (prophase, prometaphase, metaphase, anaphase, telophase) → cytokinesis.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">G0</span> = quiescent, out of cycle. Cell types: <b>labile</b> (never G0: skin, gut epithelium, bone marrow, hair follicles), <b>stable/quiescent</b> (G0 but can re-enter: hepatocytes, kidney tubule cells, fibroblasts), <span class=\"hy\">permanent</span> (cannot divide: neurons, skeletal &amp; cardiac muscle, RBCs).</li>\n<li><b>Regulation</b>: <span class=\"hy\">cyclin–CDK complexes</span> drive transitions (cyclin D–CDK4/6 in G1, cyclin E–CDK2 G1/S, cyclin A S, cyclin B–CDK1 = MPF for G2/M). CDKs are constitutive; cyclins rise and fall (ubiquitin–proteasome degradation; APC/C at metaphase→anaphase).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rb</span> (tumor suppressor) binds and inhibits <b>E2F</b>; cyclin D–CDK4 <b>phosphorylates Rb</b> → releases E2F → G1→S. <span class=\"hy\">HPV E7 binds Rb; E6 degrades p53.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">p53 (“guardian of the genome”)</span>: DNA damage → p53 → <b>p21</b> (CDK inhibitor) → G1 arrest for repair; if irreparable → apoptosis (↑ Bax/PUMA/NOXA). Mutated in &gt;50% of cancers; Li–Fraumeni syndrome (germline).</li>\n<li>Checkpoints: G1/S (DNA damage), G2/M (replication complete, DNA intact), <b>spindle (M) checkpoint</b> (all kinetochores attached) — failure → aneuploidy.</li>\n<li><b>Mitosis</b>: 1 diploid → 2 identical diploid cells. <b>Meiosis</b>: 1 diploid → 4 haploid; <span class=\"hy\">meiosis I separates homologs (reductional), crossing over in prophase I; meiosis II separates sister chromatids</span>. Nondisjunction → trisomy (e.g., Down; risk ↑ with maternal age, mostly meiosis I).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Apoptosis</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Apoptosis</th><th>Necrosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">ATP-dependent, programmed; single cells</td><td>Passive; groups of cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">Cell shrinks; pyknosis → karyorrhexis → karyolysis; eosinophilic cytoplasm; apoptotic bodies</td><td>Cell swells, membrane ruptures, contents leak</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"hy\">NO inflammation (membrane intact; PS “eat-me” → phagocytosis)</td><td>Inflammation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>DNA laddering (internucleosomal cleavage, ~180 bp)</td><td>Random DNA degradation (smear)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Physiologic roles</b>: embryogenesis (interdigital webs, lumen formation), hormone withdrawal (endometrium, menstrual shedding), <span class=\"hy\">thymic negative selection</span>, immune cell deletion, neutrophil turnover, cytotoxic T-cell killing, keeping tissue size constant.</li>\n<li><b>Intrinsic (mitochondrial) pathway</b>: DNA damage, growth-factor withdrawal, stress → p53 → <span class=\"hy\">pro-apoptotic Bax/Bak</span> form pores (BH3-only proteins Bim, Bad, PUMA, NOXA activate) → <span class=\"hy\">cytochrome c release</span> → binds Apaf-1 → apoptosome → <span class=\"hy\">caspase 9 (initiator)</span> → caspase 3/6/7 (executioners). <span class=\"hy\">Bcl-2 and Bcl-xL are anti-apoptotic</span> (keep the outer membrane closed).</li>\n<li><b>Extrinsic (death receptor) pathway</b>: <span class=\"hy\">Fas ligand–Fas (CD95)</span> or TNF–TNFR1 → FADD adaptor → <span class=\"hy\">caspase 8 (initiator)</span> → executioner caspases. Cytotoxic T cells also use <b>perforin + granzyme B</b> (activates caspases directly).</li>\n<li>IAPs/<b>survivin</b> inhibit caspases (overexpressed in tumors). Released Smac/DIABLO blocks IAPs.</li>\n<li>Defective Fas/FasL → <span class=\"hy\">autoimmune lymphoproliferative syndrome</span> (failure to delete autoreactive lymphocytes).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">BCL-2 overexpression: follicular lymphoma, t(14;18)</span> (BCL-2 moved next to Ig heavy-chain promoter) → cells don’t die. CLL also overexpresses BCL-2 (venetoclax).</li>\n<li><b>Detection</b>: <span class=\"hy\">annexin V (binds externalized PS — early)</span>, TUNEL (DNA fragments), DNA laddering on gel, caspase assays, cytochrome c release.</li>\n<li>Too little apoptosis → cancer, autoimmunity. Too much → neurodegeneration (Alzheimer, Parkinson, ALS), ischemic injury, AIDS (CD4 loss).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">I-cell disease = no M6P tag (GlcNAc-phosphotransferase) → enzymes in plasma.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Zellweger (no peroxisomes), X-ALD (VLCFA, adrenal + demyelination), Refsum (phytanic acid).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Ragged-red fibers + maternal inheritance + lactic acidosis = mitochondrial disease.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Vincristine/vinblastine block assembly; paclitaxel blocks disassembly; colchicine for gout.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Kartagener: dynein arm defect, situs inversus, infertility, bronchiectasis.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Cytokeratin carcinoma, vimentin sarcoma, desmin muscle, GFAP glia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Intrinsic = cytochrome c / caspase 9; extrinsic = Fas / caspase 8; BCL-2 t(14;18) follicular lymphoma; annexin V detects PS.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">p53 → p21 → G1 arrest; Rb holds E2F; HPV E6 (p53), E7 (Rb).</li>\n<li>COPII forward (ER → Golgi), COPI backward, clathrin to lysosomes/endocytosis.</li>\n<li>Cholesterol buffers fluidity; PS inner leaflet; cholera toxin binds GM1.</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-biochem": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Carbohydrates</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Classified by carbon number (triose, pentose, hexose) and carbonyl: <b>aldose</b> (aldehyde: glucose, galactose, ribose) vs <b>ketose</b> (ketone: fructose).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">D/L = position of –OH on the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl</span> (right = D). Natural sugars are mostly D; natural amino acids are L.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epimers differ at ONE carbon: glucose/galactose (C4), glucose/mannose (C2).</span> Enantiomers = mirror images. Anomers = differ at the anomeric carbon (α vs β) after ring closure; interconvert by mutarotation.</li>\n<li><b>Glycosidic bonds</b>: O-glycosidic (sugar–OH; disaccharides) vs N-glycosidic (sugar–N; nucleosides, N-linked glycoproteins).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Reducing sugar = free anomeric carbon</span> (Benedict’s/Fehling’s positive): glucose, fructose, galactose, maltose, lactose. <span class=\"hy\">Sucrose is NON-reducing</span> (both anomeric carbons used). Trehalose also non-reducing.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disaccharide</th><th>Units &amp; bond</th><th>Enzyme</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Sucrose</td><td>Glucose α1→β2 fructose (non-reducing)</td><td>Sucrase</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lactose</td><td>Galactose β1→4 glucose</td><td>Lactase (β-galactosidase) — deficiency → bloating, diarrhea, + hydrogen breath test</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maltose</td><td>Glucose α1→4 glucose</td><td>Maltase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trehalose</td><td>Glucose α1→1 glucose</td><td>Trehalase</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Polysaccharides</b>: <span class=\"hy\">glycogen &amp; amylopectin branch with α(1→6)</span> on α(1→4) chains (glycogen every 8–10 residues — more branched); amylose = unbranched α(1→4); <span class=\"hy\">cellulose = β(1→4), humans can’t digest (fiber)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Liver glycogen maintains blood glucose (has glucose-6-phosphatase); muscle glycogen fuels muscle only.</span> Liver stores last ~12–24 h of fasting.</li>\n<li><b>GAGs</b>: long unbranched repeating disaccharides (amino sugar + uronic acid), highly <b>negatively charged</b> (sulfate, carboxyl) → bind Na⁺ and water → gels that cushion/lubricate. Bound to core proteins as <b>proteoglycans</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>GAG</th><th>Key location / fact</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyaluronic acid</td><td>Only non-sulfated GAG, not attached to core protein; synovial fluid, vitreous, cartilage aggregates</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Heparin</td><td>Only intracellular GAG (mast cell granules); most negatively charged; activates antithrombin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chondroitin sulfate</td><td>Most abundant; cartilage (aggrecan), bone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Keratan sulfate</td><td>Cornea, cartilage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dermatan sulfate</td><td>Skin, vessels, heart valves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heparan sulfate</td><td>Basement membranes, cell surfaces (glomerular charge barrier)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Glycosylation (Anki)</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>N-linked</th><th>O-linked</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Residue</td><td class=\"hy\">Asparagine (amide N), consensus Asn-X-Ser/Thr (X ≠ Pro)</td><td class=\"hy\">Serine/threonine –OH (hydroxylysine in collagen); no strict consensus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Begins co-translationally in ER, modified in Golgi</td><td>Golgi</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How</td><td class=\"hy\">En bloc: 14-sugar Glc₃Man₉GlcNAc₂ built on dolichol phosphate, transferred by oligosaccharyltransferase (OST)</td><td>One sugar at a time by glycosyltransferases (mucin-type starts with GalNAc)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Quality control</b>: <span class=\"hy\">calnexin/calreticulin</span> retain misfolded glycoproteins in the ER (glucose trimming/re-adding); persistent misfolding → ERAD → ubiquitin–proteasome.</li>\n<li><b>Tunicamycin</b> blocks dolichol-linked oligosaccharide synthesis → no N-glycosylation.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Congenital disorders of glycosylation</span>: multisystem — hypotonia, developmental delay, abnormal fat pads, inverted nipples, coagulopathy; screen with transferrin isoforms.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Amino acids</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>All proteins use <span class=\"hy\">L-amino acids</span>; <span class=\"hy\">glycine is the only achiral amino acid</span> (R = H; smallest, flexible; every 3rd residue in collagen). D-amino acids in bacterial walls (D-Ala-D-Ala, vancomycin target).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Proline</span>: R group bonds its own amino N (imino acid) → rigid, no backbone H-bond donor → <span class=\"hy\">α-helix breaker</span>, found at turns; hydroxyproline in collagen. Cis peptide bonds mainly occur before proline.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Essential amino acids: PVT TIM HALL</span> — Phe, Val, Thr, Trp, Ile, Met, His, Arg (conditionally), Leu, Lys.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Purely ketogenic: Leucine and Lysine</span>. Both glucogenic + ketogenic: Phe, Tyr, Trp, Ile, Thr. Ketogenic → acetyl-CoA/acetoacetate; cannot make net glucose (PDH irreversible).</li>\n<li><b>Aromatic</b> (Phe, Tyr, Trp) <span class=\"hy\">absorb UV at 280 nm</span> (protein assay); nucleic acids at 260 nm; NADH 340 nm.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cysteine</span> –SH forms <b>disulfide bonds</b> (secreted proteins, insulin, antibodies); part of glutathione. Met &amp; Cys contain sulfur.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Histidine</span>: imidazole pKa ≈ 6 (near physiologic pH) → buffering; <b>proximal His</b> binds heme Fe²⁺, <b>distal His</b> stabilizes O₂ and sterically limits CO binding (CO affinity ~200–250× O₂). Bohr effect: H⁺ protonates His → T state → O₂ unloading.</li>\n<li>Acidic: Asp, Glu (negative at pH 7). Basic: Lys, Arg (positive), His (partly). Polar uncharged: Ser, Thr, Asn, Gln, Cys, Tyr.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sickle cell: Glu → Val at β6 (HbS)</span>; deoxy-HbS polymerizes (hydrophobic Val). HbC: Glu → Lys (milder, target cells).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Acid–base properties &amp; pI</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])</span>. At pH = pKa → 50:50; best buffering within ±1 pH unit of pKa.</li>\n<li>Zwitterion at physiologic pH (–NH₃⁺, –COO⁻). <span class=\"hy\">pI = pH with net charge 0</span>: neutral AA = average of the two pKas (~6); acidic AA = average of the two acidic pKas (low pI); basic AA = average of the two basic pKas (high pI).</li>\n<li>pH &lt; pI → net positive (moves to cathode). pH &gt; pI → net negative (moves to anode). Proteins are least soluble at their pI (precipitate).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Proteins: structure, folding &amp; misfolding</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Level</th><th>Defined by</th><th>Bonds</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Primary</td><td>Amino-acid sequence (N → C)</td><td class=\"hy\">Peptide (covalent amide) bonds — planar, rigid, partial double-bond, usually trans</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Secondary</td><td>α-helix, β-sheet, turns</td><td class=\"hy\">Backbone H-bonds (C=O to N–H)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tertiary</td><td>3D fold of one chain (domains)</td><td>Hydrophobic interactions (main), ionic, H-bonds, disulfides</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Quaternary</td><td>Multiple subunits (e.g., Hb α₂β₂)</td><td>Same non-covalent ± disulfides</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Insulin: two chains, <b>3 disulfides</b> (2 interchain, 1 intrachain on A chain).</li>\n<li>Classification: fibrous (collagen, keratin, elastin — structural, insoluble) vs globular (enzymes, Hb, antibodies — soluble); simple vs conjugated (glyco-, lipo-, metallo-, hemoproteins).</li>\n<li><b>Denaturation</b> (heat, pH, urea, detergents, heavy metals) disrupts 2°–4° structure; primary intact. <b>Anfinsen</b>: sequence determines fold (ribonuclease refolds).</li>\n<li><b>Chaperones</b> (Hsp70, Hsp90, chaperonins Hsp60) assist folding; misfolded proteins → ubiquitin → proteasome.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Misfolding diseases</span>: amyloidosis (β-pleated sheet, Congo red → apple-green birefringence), <span class=\"hy\">Alzheimer (Aβ plaques, tau tangles)</span>, <span class=\"hy\">prion diseases (PrPᶜ α-helix → PrPˢᶜ β-sheet; CJD, kuru, mad cow; spongiform, protease-resistant)</span>, Huntington, Parkinson (α-synuclein), cystic fibrosis ΔF508 (misfolded CFTR degraded), α₁-antitrypsin deficiency (PiZ retained in hepatocyte ER → cirrhosis + emphysema).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Enzymes (Anki)</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Class</th><th>Reaction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1 Oxidoreductases</td><td>Electron transfer (dehydrogenases, oxidases)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2 Transferases</td><td>Move a group (kinases transfer phosphate, transaminases)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>3 Hydrolases</td><td>Break bonds by adding water (proteases, lipases, phosphatases)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 Lyases</td><td>Break bonds without water/oxidation (decarboxylases, aldolase)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5 Isomerases</td><td>Rearrange within a molecule (mutases, epimerases)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>6 Ligases</td><td>Join two molecules using ATP (synthetases, carboxylases)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Km = [S] at ½ Vmax (inverse of affinity); Vmax ∝ enzyme amount.</span> Lineweaver–Burk: x-intercept = −1/Km, y-intercept = 1/Vmax.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Inhibitor</th><th>Binds</th><th>Km</th><th>Vmax</th><th>L–B plot</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Competitive (reversible)</td><td>Active site (free E)</td><td>↑</td><td>—</td><td>Lines cross on y-axis; overcome by ↑ [S]</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Noncompetitive</td><td>Allosteric site (E and ES)</td><td>—</td><td>↓</td><td>Lines cross on x-axis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uncompetitive</td><td>ES complex only</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td><td>Parallel lines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Irreversible</td><td>Covalent (aspirin–COX, organophosphates)</td><td>—</td><td>↓</td><td>Like noncompetitive</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Feedback inhibition</b>: end product inhibits an early enzyme. <b>Feedforward activation</b>: early metabolite activates a later enzyme (F-1,6-BP → pyruvate kinase).</li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Vitamins (water-soluble)</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Vitamin</th><th>Coenzyme</th><th>Function / key enzymes</th><th>Deficiency</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B1 Thiamine</td><td>TPP</td><td>Oxidative decarboxylation &amp; transketolase: PDH, α-KG DH, BCKDH, transketolase <span class=\"mn\">(Be APT)</span></td><td>Wet beriberi (high-output HF), dry beriberi (neuropathy), Wernicke (confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, ataxia), Korsakoff (amnesia, confabulation)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B2 Riboflavin</td><td>FAD, FMN</td><td>Redox; succinate DH (Complex II)</td><td>Cheilosis, magenta tongue, corneal vascularization <span class=\"mn\">(2 C’s of B2)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B3 Niacin</td><td>NAD⁺, NADP⁺</td><td>Redox; NADPH for reductive biosynthesis; made from Trp (needs B2, B6)</td><td>Pellagra: Dermatitis (Casal necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia (± Death)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>B5 Pantothenate</td><td>CoA</td><td>Acyl transfer; fatty acid synthase</td><td>Rare: dermatitis, alopecia, adrenal insufficiency, burning feet</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B6 Pyridoxine</td><td>PLP</td><td>Transamination (ALT, AST), decarboxylation, ALA synthase (heme), glutamate → GABA, glycogen phosphorylase</td><td>Convulsions, peripheral neuropathy, sideroblastic anemia, cheilosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B7 Biotin</td><td>Biotin itself</td><td>Carboxylation: pyruvate carboxylase, acetyl-CoA carboxylase, propionyl-CoA carboxylase</td><td>Dermatitis, alopecia, enteritis, lactic acidosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B9 Folate</td><td>THF</td><td>One-carbon transfer: purines, thymidine (dTMP)</td><td>Megaloblastic anemia, hypersegmented PMNs, ↑ homocysteine, <b>normal MMA</b>, <b>no neuro signs</b>; neural tube defects</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>B12 Cobalamin</td><td>Methyl- &amp; adenosyl-cobalamin</td><td>Methionine synthase; methylmalonyl-CoA mutase</td><td>Megaloblastic anemia + ↑ homocysteine + <b>↑ MMA</b> + subacute combined degeneration (dorsal columns, lateral corticospinal, spinocerebellar)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>C Ascorbate</td><td>—</td><td>Hydroxylation of Pro/Lys (collagen), dopamine β-hydroxylase, Fe³⁺→Fe²⁺ absorption, antioxidant</td><td>Scurvy: bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Thiamine before glucose</span> in alcoholics (glucose metabolism consumes TPP → precipitates Wernicke). Dx thiamine: ↑ RBC transketolase activity after adding TPP. Brain &amp; heart (aerobic) suffer first.</li>\n<li>B1 causes: alcoholism (most common US), malnutrition, hyperemesis, bariatric surgery.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pellagra causes beyond diet: Hartnup disease (↓ Trp absorption), carcinoid syndrome (Trp → serotonin), isoniazid (↓ B6).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Niacin drug</span>: best ↑ HDL, ↓ LDL/TG; side effects flushing (prostaglandin; pretreat with aspirin), hyperglycemia, hyperuricemia (gout).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Isoniazid → B6 deficiency → neuropathy; give pyridoxine.</span> Also penicillamine, OCPs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Biotin: raw egg whites (avidin) or long antibiotics.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Folate deficiency causes: alcoholism, pregnancy, methotrexate, trimethoprim, phenytoin.</span> Stores last <b>months</b>; give before conception (0.4 mg; 4 mg if prior NTD).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">B12 causes: pernicious anemia (anti-IF/parietal cell antibodies), gastrectomy, terminal ileum disease/resection (Crohn), Diphyllobothrium latum, vegan diet, metformin.</span> Absorbed with intrinsic factor (parietal cells) in the <b>terminal ileum</b>; stores last <b>years</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Giving folate alone in B12 deficiency corrects the anemia but NOT the neurologic damage.</span></li>\n<li>Humans can’t make vitamin C (lack L-gulonolactone oxidase). Fat-soluble A, D, E, K are stored (toxicity possible) and need fat absorption.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Redox vitamins = B2 + B3; decarboxylation = B1 + B6; carboxylation = biotin; one-carbon = folate (+B12); acyl transfer = B5; hydroxylation = vitamin C.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Glucose vs galactose = C4 epimers; sucrose non-reducing; glycogen branches α(1→6).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Hyaluronic acid only non-sulfated; heparin only intracellular GAG.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">N-linked = Asn-X-Ser/Thr (ER, dolichol, en bloc); O-linked = Ser/Thr (Golgi, one at a time).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Proline breaks helices; glycine achiral; leucine &amp; lysine purely ketogenic; aromatics absorb 280 nm.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Competitive: Km ↑, Vmax same. Noncompetitive: Vmax ↓, Km same.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Wernicke triad + alcoholic → thiamine (before glucose). ↑ MMA → B12, not folate.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Prions = PrP α→β conversion; amyloid stains Congo red (apple-green).</li>\n</ul>", "fbs-embryology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Meiosis &amp; chromosome counting</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Ploidy (n)</th><th>DNA (c)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Spermatogonium / oogonium (before S)</td><td>2n</td><td>2c</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Primary spermatocyte / oocyte (after S)</td><td>2n (46)</td><td>4c</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Secondary spermatocyte / oocyte (after meiosis I)</td><td>1n (23)</td><td>2c</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spermatid / ovum (after meiosis II)</td><td>1n</td><td>1c</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Meiosis I = reductional (homologs separate; crossing over in prophase I). Meiosis II = equational (sister chromatids separate).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Nondisjunction (mostly maternal meiosis I) ↑ with maternal age → trisomy 21, 18, 13; Klinefelter (47,XXY), Turner (45,X).</span></li>\n<li>Primordial germ cells arise in the <b>yolk sac wall (epiblast origin)</b> and migrate to the gonadal ridge by week 5–6; lost PGCs → germ cell tumors (sacrococcygeal teratoma).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Spermatogenesis &amp; the male tract</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Begins at puberty, in seminiferous tubules; ~64–74 days. Spermatogonia (mitosis) → primary spermatocyte → secondary spermatocyte → spermatids → <b>spermiogenesis</b> (shape change only, no division).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Spermiogenesis: acrosome from Golgi (hyaluronidase, acrosin), flagellum from centriole, mitochondria form midpiece sheath, cytoplasm shed.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sertoli cells (FSH)</span>: blood–testis barrier (tight junctions), androgen-binding protein, <b>inhibin B</b> (→ ↓ FSH), <b>Müllerian-inhibiting factor/AMH</b> (Müllerian ducts regress), support spermatogenesis. <span class=\"hy\">Leydig cells (LH)</span>: interstitial, make <b>testosterone</b> <span class=\"mn\">(L for LH, L for Leydig)</span>.</li>\n<li>Sperm path: <span class=\"mn\">SEVEN UP</span> — Seminiferous tubules → (rete testis, efferent ductules) → Epididymis (maturation, motility, storage) → Vas deferens → Ejaculatory duct → (Nothing) → Urethra → Penis.</li>\n<li><b>Capacitation</b> (in female tract; removal of cholesterol/glycoproteins) → <b>acrosome reaction</b> on binding ZP3 of the zona pellucida.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Oogenesis &amp; the ovary</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Oogonia multiply only before birth (months 3–7)</span>; primary oocytes <span class=\"hy\">arrest in prophase I (diplotene) until puberty</span>; most undergo <b>atresia</b> (~7 million fetal → 1–2 million at birth → ~400,000 at puberty → ~400 ovulated).</li>\n<li>Each cycle: LH surge → meiosis I completes → secondary oocyte + 1st polar body → <span class=\"hy\">secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase II until fertilization</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Ovulated, waiting to Meet a sperm”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Follicle stages: primordial (flat cells) → <b>primary</b> (single cuboidal layer; zona pellucida forms) → secondary (2+ layers, antrum begins) → tertiary/<b>Graafian</b> (large antrum, cumulus oophorus).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Two-cell model: theca cells (LH) make androgens → diffuse to granulosa cells (FSH) → aromatase → estrogen.</span> <i>Your deck:</i> theca interna = vascular, hormone-secreting layer (estrogen); theca externa = fibrous.</li>\n<li>Granulosa = female counterpart of Sertoli; theca = counterpart of Leydig.</li>\n<li><b>Corpus luteum</b> (from ruptured follicle) → <span class=\"hy\">progesterone</span> (+ estrogen). Degenerates after ~14 days → corpus albicans, unless <span class=\"hy\">hCG</span> from the syncytiotrophoblast rescues it (until placenta takes over ~weeks 8–10).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Menstrual cycle &amp; hormones</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Days</th><th>Ovary</th><th>Endometrium</th><th>Hormones</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Menstrual</td><td>1–5</td><td>Early follicle recruitment</td><td>Functional layer shed</td><td>↓ estrogen &amp; progesterone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Follicular / proliferative</td><td>~6–14</td><td>Dominant follicle grows</td><td>Proliferates (estrogen)</td><td>FSH ↑ early, estrogen rises</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ovulation</td><td>~14</td><td>Graafian follicle ruptures 24–36 h after LH surge</td><td>—</td><td>Estrogen peak → positive feedback → <b>LH surge</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Luteal / secretory</td><td>15–28 (fixed 14 days)</td><td>Corpus luteum</td><td>Glands secrete glycogen; receptive</td><td>Progesterone ↑ (basal body temp ↑ ~0.5 °F)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Variation in cycle length comes from the follicular phase; luteal phase is fixed ~14 days.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Menses occurs because the corpus luteum degenerates → progesterone falls → spiral arteries constrict → ischemia → shedding.</span></li>\n<li>Estrogen: endometrial proliferation, thin stretchy cervical mucus (spinnbarkeit). Progesterone: secretory endometrium, thick mucus, maintains pregnancy, ↑ temperature.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Combined OCPs</span>: negative feedback ↓ FSH/LH → no follicle maturation, no LH surge (no ovulation); thick cervical mucus; thin endometrium.</li>\n<li>Endometrium layers: functional (shed) vs basal (regenerates). <b>Decidua</b> = pregnancy endometrium: basalis (maternal placenta), capsularis, parietalis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Fertilization, cleavage &amp; implantation (week 1)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fertilization in the ampulla of the uterine tube, within ~24 h of ovulation.</span></li>\n<li>Sperm penetrate corona radiata → <b>zona pellucida</b> (ZP3 binding, acrosome reaction) → fusion. <span class=\"hy\">Cortical reaction / zona reaction blocks polyspermy</span> (Ca²⁺ wave; ZP changes). Polyspermy → triploidy (partial mole 69,XXY).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Results of fertilization: meiosis II completed, diploidy restored, sex determined (X or Y sperm), cleavage initiated.</span></li>\n<li>Cleavage: mitosis without growth → blastomeres → <span class=\"hy\">morula (16 cells, ~day 3)</span> → enters uterus → <span class=\"hy\">blastocyst (~day 4–5)</span>: outer cells pump Na⁺ in, water follows → blastocyst cavity. <b>Trophoblast</b> (→ placenta) + <b>inner cell mass/embryoblast</b> (→ embryo).</li>\n<li>Hatches from zona → <span class=\"hy\">implantation begins ~day 6</span> in the posterior-superior uterine wall (endometrium in secretory/luteal phase). hCG detectable in blood ~day 8, urine ~day 10–14.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Ectopic pregnancy</span>: most often <b>ampulla</b>; risk ↑ with PID/salpingitis, prior ectopic, surgery, IUD, Kartagener. Abdominal pain + vaginal bleeding + ↑ hCG, empty uterus on US. Placenta previa = implantation near/over the internal os (painless bleeding).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Week 2: “week of twos”</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">2 layers of trophoblast: cytotrophoblast (inner, mitotic) + syncytiotrophoblast (outer, invasive, makes hCG).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">2 layers of embryoblast (bilaminar disc): epiblast (→ amniotic cavity; later gives all 3 germ layers) + hypoblast (→ primary yolk sac).</span> <i>Your deck</i> pairs epiblast ≈ ectoderm, hypoblast ≈ endoderm.</li>\n<li>2 cavities: amniotic cavity &amp; yolk sac (+ chorionic cavity from splitting of extraembryonic mesoderm). Connecting stalk → umbilical cord.</li>\n<li><b>Villi</b>: primary (cytotrophoblast core + syncytio) → secondary (+ extraembryonic mesoderm core) → <span class=\"hy\">tertiary (+ fetal blood vessels, end of week 3)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">hCG</span> (α subunit like LH/FSH/TSH) maintains the corpus luteum; very high in moles/choriocarcinoma (can cause hyperthyroidism, hyperemesis).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hydatidiform mole</span>: complete = 46,XX (empty egg + paternal duplication), no fetus, “snowstorm”/grape-like, very ↑ hCG, ↑ choriocarcinoma risk. Partial = 69,XXY (2 sperm), fetal parts present, lower risk.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Yolk sac, primitive gut &amp; umbilical cord</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Yolk sac: early blood formation (blood islands, week 3), primordial germ cells, contributes to primitive gut (folding incorporates it).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Vitelline duct normally obliterates; persistence → Meckel diverticulum</span> (rule of 2s: 2% of people, 2 feet from ileocecal valve, 2 inches long, 2 types of ectopic tissue — gastric/pancreatic, presents by age 2; painless bleeding) or vitelline fistula (meconium from umbilicus).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Umbilical cord: 2 arteries (deoxygenated blood to placenta) + 1 vein (oxygenated to fetus), Wharton jelly.</span> Single umbilical artery → associated with renal and cardiac anomalies.</li>\n<li><b>Allantois</b> → urachus (median umbilical ligament); patent urachus → urine from umbilicus.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Week 3: gastrulation &amp; germ layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gastrulation = formation of 3 germ layers from epiblast via the primitive streak (begins with primitive node/streak at the caudal end).</span> Epiblast cells invaginate: first replace hypoblast → <b>endoderm</b>; then between → <b>mesoderm</b>; remaining epiblast → <b>ectoderm</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Notochord</b> (from primitive node) → induces neural plate; adult remnant = <span class=\"hy\">nucleus pulposus</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sacrococcygeal teratoma</span> = remnant of primitive streak (most common tumor in newborns). <span class=\"hy\">Caudal regression syndrome</span> (sirenomelia) — maternal diabetes.</li>\n<li>Oropharyngeal (buccopharyngeal) and cloacal membranes: ectoderm + endoderm, no mesoderm.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Week 3–8 = embryonic period: most susceptible to teratogens (organogenesis).</span> Weeks 1–2: “all-or-none”.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Germ layer</th><th>Gives rise to</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface ectoderm</td><td>Epidermis, hair, nails, lens, anterior pituitary (Rathke pouch), enamel, mammary/sweat glands, inner ear</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neuroectoderm</td><td>Brain, spinal cord, retina, posterior pituitary, pineal, oligodendrocytes, astrocytes</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neural crest</td><td>PNS ganglia, Schwann cells, melanocytes, adrenal medulla (chromaffin), odontoblasts, parafollicular C cells, pia/arachnoid, aorticopulmonary septum, craniofacial bones <span class=\"mn\">(MOTEL PASS)</span></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Mesoderm</td><td>Muscle, bone, connective tissue, heart, blood/lymph vessels, kidneys, gonads, dermis, spleen, adrenal cortex, serous linings</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Endoderm</td><td>Gut epithelium, liver, pancreas, lungs, thyroid follicular cells, thymus, parathyroids, bladder/urethra epithelium</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Mesoderm, body cavities &amp; neurulation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesoderm divisions: <b>paraxial</b> → somites (sclerotome = vertebrae/ribs; myotome = skeletal muscle; dermatome = dermis); <b>intermediate</b> → urogenital system (kidneys, gonads); <b>lateral plate</b> → somatic (parietal) layer (body wall, limb bones/CT, parietal serosa) &amp; splanchnic (visceral) layer (gut wall muscle, heart, visceral serosa).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intraembryonic coelom</span> (space within lateral plate) → pericardial, pleural and peritoneal cavities; septum transversum → central tendon of diaphragm, part of liver.</li>\n<li><b>Neurulation (weeks 3–4)</b>: notochord induces neural plate → neural folds → neural tube; <span class=\"hy\">anterior neuropore closes ~day 25, posterior ~day 27–28</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Neural tube defects</span>: anencephaly (anterior neuropore; polyhydramnios — can’t swallow), spina bifida occulta (no herniation, tuft of hair/dimple), meningocele, myelomeningocele (posterior neuropore). <span class=\"hy\">↑ AFP in maternal serum &amp; amniotic fluid, ↑ AChE in amniotic fluid</span>. Prevent: <b>folic acid</b>. Risk: valproate, carbamazepine, maternal diabetes, folate deficiency.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Placenta: structure, variants &amp; pathology</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Fetal part = <b>chorion frondosum</b> (villous chorion); maternal part = <b>decidua basalis</b>. Exchange across the <b>placental membrane</b> (syncytiotrophoblast, cytotrophoblast — thins later — villous connective tissue, fetal endothelium). Maternal blood fills intervillous spaces from spiral arteries.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Syncytiotrophoblast makes hCG, hPL (human placental lactogen → insulin resistance), estrogen (estriol), progesterone.</span></li>\n<li>Crosses: O₂, CO₂, glucose, <span class=\"hy\">IgG (only immunoglobulin)</span>, lipid-soluble drugs, alcohol, many viruses (TORCH), anti-D antibodies (Rh disease). Does not cross: IgM, heparin, insulin (largely).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Placenta accreta / increta / percreta</span>: defective decidua basalis → villi attach to / invade / penetrate myometrium; risk prior C-section; postpartum hemorrhage.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Placental abruption</span>: premature separation → <b>painful</b> bleeding (cocaine, smoking, HTN, trauma); <b>Placenta previa</b> → <b>painless</b> bleeding.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Preeclampsia</span>: abnormal spiral artery remodeling → HTN + proteinuria after 20 weeks; eclampsia = + seizures (Mg sulfate); HELLP.</li>\n<li>Variants: succenturiate (accessory) lobe, velamentous cord insertion (vasa previa risk), battledore (marginal), circumvallate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Prenatal testing, amniotic fluid &amp; twins</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Amniotic fluid</span>: mostly fetal urine after ~week 16; removed by fetal swallowing. Normal ~1 L at term.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Polyhydramnios (&gt;2 L)</span>: can’t swallow — esophageal/duodenal atresia, anencephaly; maternal diabetes, twins. <span class=\"hy\">Oligohydramnios (&lt;0.5 L)</span>: can’t make/excrete urine — bilateral renal agenesis, posterior urethral valves, placental insufficiency, ROM → <b>Potter sequence</b> (pulmonary hypoplasia, flat face, limb deformities, clubfoot).</li>\n<li><b>Screening</b>: quad screen (~15–20 weeks): AFP, hCG, estriol, inhibin A. <span class=\"hy\">Down syndrome: ↓ AFP, ↑ hCG, ↓ estriol, ↑ inhibin A</span>. Trisomy 18: all ↓ (inhibin normal). NTD/abdominal wall defect/twins: ↑ AFP. First-trimester: ↑ nuchal translucency, ↓ PAPP-A, ↑ β-hCG (Down). Cell-free fetal DNA from ~10 weeks.</li>\n<li><b>Diagnostic</b>: <span class=\"hy\">chorionic villus sampling (~10–13 weeks)</span>; <span class=\"hy\">amniocentesis (~15–20 weeks)</span>; percutaneous umbilical blood sampling (cordocentesis). Lecithin/sphingomyelin ratio &gt;2 = fetal lung maturity.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Twins</span>: dizygotic (fraternal, 2/3; two eggs) → always dichorionic diamniotic. <span class=\"hy\">Monozygotic (identical) — timing of split decides membranes</span>:</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Split (days)</th><th>Stage</th><th>Result</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–4</td><td>Morula (2-cell to morula)</td><td>Dichorionic, diamniotic (2 placentas)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>4–8</td><td>Blastocyst (inner cell mass splits)</td><td>Monochorionic, diamniotic (most common MZ) — twin–twin transfusion risk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8–12</td><td>Bilaminar disc</td><td>Monochorionic, monoamniotic (cord entanglement)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>&gt;13</td><td>Embryonic disc</td><td>Conjoined twins</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g12\">12. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Primary oocyte: prophase I arrest until puberty. Secondary oocyte: metaphase II arrest until fertilization.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">LH → Leydig (testosterone); FSH → Sertoli (ABP, inhibin, AMH). Theca androgens → granulosa estrogen.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Fertilization in ampulla; implantation day 6; ectopic usually ampulla.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Week 2 = 2s (cyto/syncytio; epi/hypoblast; amnion/yolk sac). Week 3 = 3 germ layers (gastrulation), notochord → nucleus pulposus.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">NTD: ↑ AFP + ↑ AChE; prevent with folate. Down: ↓ AFP, ↑ hCG, ↓ estriol, ↑ inhibin.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Complete mole 46,XX paternal, no fetus; partial 69,XXY with fetal parts.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Monochorionic diamniotic = split days 4–8 (most common MZ twins).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Oligohydramnios → Potter sequence (renal agenesis); polyhydramnios → GI atresia/anencephaly.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-histology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Muscle</h2>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Cardiac</th><th>Smooth</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nuclei</td><td class=\"hy\">Many, peripheral</td><td class=\"hy\">1–2, central</td><td>1, central (cigar/corkscrew when contracted)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Special</td><td>Triads (T-tubule at <b>A–I junction</b> + 2 terminal cisternae)</td><td class=\"hy\">Intercalated discs (fascia adherens, desmosomes, gap junctions); dyads at <b>Z-line</b>; branched</td><td>Dense bodies (α-actinin), caveolae, gap junctions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regeneration</td><td>Satellite cells (limited)</td><td class=\"hy\">None → fibrous scar</td><td>Good (mitosis, pericytes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ binds</td><td>Troponin C</td><td>Troponin C</td><td class=\"hy\">Calmodulin → MLCK</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Organization: epimysium (whole muscle) → perimysium (fascicles) → <b>endomysium</b> (each fiber; reticular fibers) → fiber → myofibrils → sarcomeres.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sarcomere = Z to Z line. A band (myosin, entire length) stays constant. I band (actin only), H zone (myosin only) shorten during contraction. M line = middle of H zone.</span> <span class=\"mn\">(HIZ shrinkage; A is Always the same)</span></li>\n<li>Thin filament = actin + tropomyosin + troponin (T binds tropomyosin, I inhibits, C binds Ca²⁺). Thick = myosin II.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Accessory proteins</span>: <b>titin</b> (Z to M; elasticity, centers myosin; largest protein), <b>nebulin</b> (molecular ruler for actin length), <b>α-actinin</b> (anchors actin at Z), <b>desmin</b> (links Z discs to each other and sarcolemma), <b>dystrophin</b> (actin → dystroglycan → laminin; Duchenne/Becker), myomesin (M line), CapZ (+ end), tropomodulin (− end).</li>\n<li>Fiber types: <span class=\"hy\">type I = slow oxidative, red (myoglobin, mitochondria), posture, marathon</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“one slow red ox”)</span>; type IIa fast oxidative-glycolytic; <b>type IIb/x = fast glycolytic, white, sprint</b>.</li>\n<li>Muscle spindle (intrafusal: nuclear bag/chain; γ motor; senses length) vs Golgi tendon organ (tendon; senses tension; Ib).</li>\n<li><b>Satellite cells</b>: muscle stem cells under the basal lamina; hypertrophy (not hyperplasia) after exercise.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Cell junctions &amp; cytoskeleton</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Junction</th><th>Proteins</th><th>Cytoskeleton</th><th>Function / disease</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tight (zonula occludens)</td><td>Claudins, occludin</td><td>Actin</td><td>Seal paracellular space; apical/basolateral polarity; C. perfringens toxin targets claudins</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Adherens (zonula adherens)</td><td>E-cadherin (Ca²⁺-dependent), catenins</td><td>Actin</td><td>Belt; loss of E-cadherin → metastasis, diffuse gastric/lobular breast cancer</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Desmosome (macula adherens)</td><td>Desmogleins, desmocollins (cadherins), plakoglobin, desmoplakin</td><td>Intermediate (keratin)</td><td>Spot weld; <b>pemphigus vulgaris</b> = anti-desmoglein 1/3 → intraepidermal, flaccid bullae, + Nikolsky</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gap junction</td><td>Connexins (connexon)</td><td>—</td><td>Electrical/metabolic coupling (cardiac, smooth muscle); Charcot–Marie–Tooth X-linked (connexin 32)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hemidesmosome</td><td>Integrins (α6β4), BP180/BP230</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Cell → basement membrane; <b>bullous pemphigoid</b> = anti-hemidesmosome → subepidermal tense bullae, Nikolsky −</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Focal adhesion</td><td>Integrins → fibronectin</td><td>Actin</td><td>Cell–ECM; migration signaling</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intermediate filaments = tumor markers: cytokeratin (epithelium/carcinoma), vimentin (mesenchyme/sarcoma), desmin (muscle), GFAP (glia), neurofilament (neurons), lamins (nucleus).</span></li>\n<li>Microvilli = actin core (absorptive surface); stereocilia = long actin microvilli (epididymis, inner ear hair cells); cilia = microtubules 9+2 with dynein (Kartagener).</li>\n<li>Basement membrane = basal lamina (type IV collagen, laminin, heparan sulfate, nidogen) + reticular lamina (type III).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Cartilage</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Collagen</th><th>Perichondrium</th><th>Where</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Hyaline</td><td>Type II</td><td>Yes (except articular)</td><td>Articular surfaces, costal, trachea, larynx (most), nose, <b>fetal skeleton &amp; epiphyseal plate</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Elastic</td><td>Type II + elastic fibers</td><td>Yes</td><td>External ear, epiglottis, auditory tube</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Fibrocartilage</td><td><b>Type I</b> + II</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>Intervertebral disc (annulus), pubic symphysis, menisci, TMJ, tendon insertions</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cartilage is avascular</span> (nutrients diffuse from perichondrium/synovial fluid) → poor healing.</li>\n<li>Chondrocytes in lacunae; <b>isogenous groups</b> (from one cell). Matrix: aggrecan (chondroitin + keratan sulfate on hyaluronan) → water binding → compression resistance.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth: appositional (from perichondrium chondroblasts) vs interstitial (chondrocytes dividing inside; growth plate).</span></li>\n<li>Osteoarthritis = articular cartilage degeneration (no perichondrium to repair).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Bone</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Matrix: <span class=\"hy\">type I collagen</span> (osteoid) + hydroxyapatite. Cells:\n<ul><li><b>Osteoprogenitor</b> (mesenchymal, RUNX2) → <span class=\"hy\">osteoblasts</span> (cuboidal, on surfaces; make osteoid, <b>alkaline phosphatase</b>, osteocalcin; express <b>RANKL</b> and OPG; PTH receptors).</li>\n<li><b>Osteocytes</b> (trapped in lacunae, connected by canaliculi via gap junctions; mechanosensors; sclerostin).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Osteoclasts</span>: multinucleated, from <b>monocyte/macrophage lineage</b>; ruffled border + sealing zone (integrins) form <b>Howship lacuna</b>; secrete H⁺ (carbonic anhydrase II) and cathepsin K; <b>tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP)</b>. Activated by <span class=\"hy\">RANKL–RANK</span> + M-CSF; OPG = decoy receptor; denosumab = anti-RANKL. Calcitonin inhibits.</li></ul></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PTH acts on osteoblasts (receptors) → ↑ RANKL → osteoclast activation.</span> Intermittent PTH (teriparatide) builds bone.</li>\n<li><b>Compact (cortical) bone</b>: osteons/Haversian systems — concentric lamellae around a Haversian canal; <b>Volkmann canals</b> run perpendicular, connecting them; interstitial and circumferential lamellae. <b>Spongy (cancellous)</b>: trabeculae, red marrow, no osteons.</li>\n<li>Woven (primary, immature; random collagen; fracture callus, Paget) vs lamellar (mature).</li>\n<li>Periosteum (outer fibrous + inner osteogenic layer; Sharpey fibers) vs endosteum.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Ossification: intramembranous (flat skull bones, clavicle, mandible — mesenchyme → bone directly) vs endochondral (long bones, vertebrae, base of skull — hyaline cartilage model replaced).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth plate zones (epiphysis → diaphysis): reserve (resting) → proliferation (stacked coins; achondroplasia FGFR3) → hypertrophy (chondrocytes swell, Ca²⁺ matrix; vitamin D deficiency/rickets here) → calcification → ossification.</span></li>\n<li>Fracture repair: hematoma → soft callus (fibrocartilage) → hard callus (woven bone) → remodeling (lamellar).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Disorder</th><th>Defect</th><th>Labs / clue</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteoporosis</td><td>↓ bone mass, normal mineralization</td><td>Normal Ca, PO₄, ALP, PTH; vertebral crush, hip, Colles fx</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteopetrosis</td><td>Osteoclast failure (carbonic anhydrase II)</td><td>Dense brittle bone, marrow failure, Erlenmeyer flask</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteomalacia / rickets</td><td>Defective mineralization (vit D)</td><td>↓ Ca, ↓ PO₄, ↑ ALP, ↑ PTH; rachitic rosary, bowed legs</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Paget disease</td><td>Excess osteoclast then blast activity</td><td>Isolated ↑↑ ALP, mosaic woven bone, ↑ hat size, hearing loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta</td><td>Type I collagen</td><td>Blue sclerae, multiple fractures, hearing loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Achondroplasia</td><td>FGFR3 gain (AD) — ↓ proliferation zone</td><td>Short limbs, normal trunk; ↑ paternal age</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Connective tissue &amp; ECM</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Cells: fibroblasts (main), macrophages, mast cells (heparin, histamine; IgE), plasma cells, adipocytes. Fibers: collagen, reticular (type III), elastic. Ground substance: GAGs, proteoglycans, glycoproteins (fibronectin, laminin).</li>\n<li>Loose (areolar, under epithelia) vs dense irregular (dermis, capsules) vs <span class=\"hy\">dense regular (tendons, ligaments — parallel type I, few fibroblasts, poor blood supply)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Collagen</th><th>Location</th><th>Disease</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>I (most abundant)</td><td><b>B</b>one, sk<b>in</b>, tendon, dentin, fascia, cornea, late wound repair</td><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta, Ehlers–Danlos (some)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>II</td><td>Cartilage, vitreous, nucleus pulposus</td><td>Achondrogenesis/Stickler</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>III (reticulin)</td><td>Skin, vessels, uterus, granulation tissue, lymphoid organs</td><td>Vascular Ehlers–Danlos</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>IV</td><td>Basement membrane</td><td>Alport (hearing, eye, kidney), Goodpasture (anti-α3 IV)</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“Be (So Totally) Cool, Read Books”: type I Bone; II Cartilage; III Reticulin; IV Basement membrane.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Elastin: rich in glycine, proline, <b>desmosine</b> cross-links (lysyl oxidase), scaffold = <b>fibrillin-1</b>. <span class=\"hy\">Marfan = fibrillin-1 (FBN1)</span>: tall, arachnodactyly, lens subluxation upward, aortic root dilation/dissection, MVP. α₁-antitrypsin deficiency → elastase destroys lung elastin (panacinar emphysema).</li>\n<li>Collagen synthesis &amp; disease details → MSK Biochemistry guide.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Skin</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epidermis layers (deep → superficial): stratum basale (stem cells, melanocytes) → spinosum (desmosomes “spines”, Langerhans cells) → granulosum (keratohyalin, lamellar bodies) → lucidum (thick skin only) → corneum (dead keratinized)</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Californians Like Girls in String Bikinis”: corneum, lucidum, granulosum, spinosum, basale)</span>.</li>\n<li>Cells: keratinocytes (~90%), <span class=\"hy\">melanocytes (neural crest; melanin to keratinocytes via melanosomes; albinism = normal number, ↓ tyrosinase; vitiligo = ↓ number)</span>, <span class=\"hy\">Langerhans cells (antigen-presenting; Birbeck “tennis racket” granules; CD1a, S-100; bone marrow origin)</span>, <b>Merkel cells</b> (touch, basale).</li>\n<li>Dermis: papillary (loose CT, Meissner corpuscles) and reticular (dense irregular, Pacinian corpuscles deeper, glands). Hypodermis = adipose.</li>\n<li>Thick skin (palms/soles): stratum lucidum, no hair. Thin skin: hair follicles, sebaceous glands.</li>\n<li>Glands: <span class=\"hy\">eccrine sweat (merocrine; everywhere; sympathetic <b>cholinergic</b>)</span>, apocrine (axilla, groin; open into follicles; after puberty), sebaceous (holocrine; acne).</li>\n<li>Receptors: Meissner (light touch, papillary dermis, glabrous), Pacinian (vibration/pressure, deep, onion layers), Merkel (sustained pressure), Ruffini (stretch), free nerve endings (pain, temperature).</li>\n<li>Psoriasis: thickened spinosum (acanthosis), parakeratosis, Munro microabscesses. Burns: 1st degree epidermis; 2nd into dermis (blisters); 3rd full thickness (painless).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Slide identification tips</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Peripheral multiple nuclei + striations = skeletal; central nucleus + branching + intercalated discs = cardiac; spindle cells with central nucleus, no striations = smooth.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Concentric lamellae around a central canal = osteon (compact bone). Lacunae with isogenous groups = cartilage (glassy = hyaline; dark fibers = elastic; rows of cells between collagen = fibrocartilage).</li>\n<li>Large multinucleated cell in a Howship lacuna = osteoclast; cuboidal row on osteoid = osteoblasts.</li>\n<li>Parallel collagen with flattened nuclei in rows = tendon (dense regular).</li>\n<li>Keratinized stratified squamous with thick corneum and lucidum = thick skin.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-physiology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Neuromuscular junction (NMJ)</h2>\n<ol style=\"padding-left:16px;margin:2px 0 4px\">\n<li>AP reaches motor nerve terminal → <span class=\"hy\">voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels (P/Q type) open → Ca²⁺ influx</span>.</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ triggers <b>SNARE</b>-mediated exocytosis of ACh vesicles (synaptobrevin, syntaxin, SNAP-25).</li>\n<li>ACh binds <span class=\"hy\">nicotinic receptors (N<sub>M</sub>, ligand-gated cation channels; Na⁺ in &gt; K⁺ out)</span> on the motor end plate (junctional folds).</li>\n<li>End-plate potential (graded) → always reaches threshold (high safety factor) → muscle AP.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Acetylcholinesterase</span> in the synaptic cleft hydrolyzes ACh → acetate + choline (choline recycled).</li>\n</ol>\n<table><tr><th>Agent / disease</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Botulinum toxin</td><td>Cleaves SNAREs → blocks ACh release</td><td>Descending flaccid paralysis; infant botulism (honey); cosmetic/dystonia use</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Myasthenia gravis</td><td>Antibodies vs postsynaptic nicotinic ACh receptors</td><td>Fatigable weakness, ptosis, diplopia, <b>worse with use</b>; thymoma; edrophonium/AChE inhibitor test; treat pyridostigmine</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lambert–Eaton</td><td>Antibodies vs presynaptic voltage-gated Ca²⁺ channels</td><td>Proximal weakness <b>improves with repeated use</b>, autonomic sx, ↓ reflexes; small cell lung cancer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curare / d-tubocurarine, rocuronium</td><td>Competitive nicotinic antagonists (nondepolarizing)</td><td>Paralysis; reversed by AChE inhibitors (neostigmine) / sugammadex</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Succinylcholine</td><td>Depolarizing agonist; persistent depolarization inactivates Na⁺ channels</td><td>Fasciculations then flaccid paralysis; malignant hyperthermia, hyperkalemia</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Organophosphates / neostigmine</td><td>Inhibit AChE</td><td>↑ ACh; organophosphate poisoning = DUMBBELSS; atropine + pralidoxime</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hemicholinium</td><td>Blocks choline reuptake</td><td>↓ ACh synthesis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>α-bungarotoxin</td><td>Irreversibly binds nicotinic receptor</td><td>Paralysis</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Skeletal muscle structure &amp; excitation–contraction coupling</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Sarcomere (Z to Z): <span class=\"hy\">A band constant; I band and H zone shorten</span> (sliding filament). Titin = elasticity/centering; nebulin = actin length ruler.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">E–C coupling (skeletal): muscle AP spreads down T-tubules → DHPR (L-type Ca²⁺ channel acting as a voltage sensor) is <b>mechanically coupled</b> to RyR1 on the SR → Ca²⁺ release → binds troponin C → tropomyosin moves off myosin-binding sites → cross-bridge cycling.</span> Extracellular Ca²⁺ is NOT needed in skeletal muscle.</li>\n<li><b>Cardiac</b>: Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release — Ca²⁺ entering through DHPR triggers RyR2 (extracellular Ca²⁺ required).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Relaxation: SERCA pumps Ca²⁺ back into the SR (uses ATP)</span>; phospholamban regulates SERCA in heart. Calsequestrin stores Ca²⁺ in SR.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Malignant hyperthermia</span>: RyR1 mutation (AD); triggered by volatile anesthetics/succinylcholine → uncontrolled Ca²⁺ release → rigidity, hyperthermia, hypercarbia, hyperkalemia, ↑ CK → <b>dantrolene</b> (blocks RyR1).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Cross-bridge cycle (ATP roles):</b> (1) Rigor: myosin bound to actin, no ATP. (2) <b>ATP binds myosin → myosin releases actin.</b> (3) ATP hydrolysis (ADP + Pi stay bound) → myosin head “cocked”. (4) Head binds actin (once Ca²⁺ exposes sites). (5) <b>Pi release → power stroke</b>; ADP released. <span class=\"hy\">No ATP → rigor mortis (heads stuck to actin).</span> ATP is also used by SERCA (relaxation) and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.</div>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Contraction mechanics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Twitch</b>: single AP → single contraction (latent, contraction, relaxation). AP lasts ~2–5 ms, twitch much longer → summation possible.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Summation &amp; tetanus</span>: ↑ stimulation frequency → Ca²⁺ stays elevated → summation → <b>fused tetanus</b> (max force). <b>Treppe</b> (staircase): slightly ↑ force with repeated twitches.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Recruitment (size principle)</span>: small motor units (type I, slow, fatigue-resistant) recruited first, large (type II) last. Force graded by recruitment + rate coding.</li>\n<li>Motor unit = one α motor neuron + all fibers it innervates; small units for fine control (eye muscles), large for gross (quadriceps).</li>\n<li><b>Isometric</b> (length constant, tension ↑) vs <b>isotonic</b> (constant load, length changes): concentric (shortening), <b>eccentric</b> (lengthening under load — most force, most DOMS/damage).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Length–tension relationship</span>: max active tension at optimal sarcomere length (~2.0–2.2 µm, maximal cross-bridge overlap). Too short (filaments overlap/collide) or too long (little overlap) → ↓ active tension. Passive tension (titin, CT) ↑ with stretch; total = active + passive.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Force–velocity relationship</span>: ↑ load → ↓ shortening velocity; Vmax at zero load; at max (isometric) load velocity = 0. Power max at ~1/3 Vmax.</li>\n<li>Preload = load before contraction (sets length); afterload = load the muscle works against.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Energetics &amp; fiber types</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>ATP stores last only seconds. Sources: <span class=\"hy\">phosphocreatine (creatine kinase) — fastest, ~10 s</span> → anaerobic glycolysis (→ lactate; ~1–2 min) → oxidative phosphorylation (sustained, needs O₂).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fenn effect</span>: muscle doing more work consumes more ATP/energy (energy liberated ∝ work performed).</li>\n<li>Efficiency ~20–25% (rest lost as heat).</li>\n<li>Fatigue: ↓ glycogen, ↑ Pi, ↑ H⁺, impaired Ca²⁺ release; central fatigue.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th class=\"hy\">Type I (slow oxidative)</th><th>Type IIa (fast oxidative-glycolytic)</th><th class=\"hy\">Type IIx/IIb (fast glycolytic)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Color</td><td>Red (myoglobin)</td><td>Pink/red</td><td>White</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria / capillaries</td><td>Many</td><td>Many</td><td>Few</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Myosin ATPase speed</td><td>Slow</td><td>Fast</td><td>Fastest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fatigue</td><td>Resistant</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Fast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fiber size / motor unit</td><td>Small</td><td>Medium</td><td>Large</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>Posture (soleus), endurance</td><td>—</td><td>Sprint, power, extraocular-like fast movement</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“One slow red ox”: type 1, slow, red, oxidative. Endurance training ↑ mitochondria/type I traits; resistance training → hypertrophy of type II.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Exercise physiology</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>↑ Cardiac output (HR and SV), ↓ TPR (local metabolic vasodilation: adenosine, K⁺, H⁺, CO₂, lactate, NO), ↑ systolic BP, diastolic BP ~same or ↓.</li>\n<li>Ventilation ↑ proportionally (PaO₂, PaCO₂ normal in moderate exercise); ↑ O₂ extraction (rightward shift of O₂–Hb curve: ↑ temperature, CO₂, H⁺, 2,3-BPG).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Oxygen debt (EPOC)</span>: extra O₂ after exercise to restore phosphocreatine, ATP, lactate clearance (Cori cycle), myoglobin O₂.</li>\n<li>Lactate threshold: point where lactate accumulates (anaerobic glycolysis predominates).</li>\n<li>Training: ↑ VO₂max, ↑ stroke volume, ↓ resting HR, ↑ capillary density, ↑ mitochondria.</li>\n<li>Rhabdomyolysis (crush, extreme exertion, statins): ↑ CK, myoglobinuria (urine + blood on dipstick, no RBCs) → AKI; hyperkalemia.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Smooth muscle</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>No sarcomeres/troponin; actin anchored to <b>dense bodies</b> (α-actinin) and membrane; intermediate filaments (desmin, vimentin). Little SR; caveolae.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Contraction: ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺ (from ECF via L-type channels, and SR via IP₃ receptors) → Ca²⁺–calmodulin → activates myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK) → phosphorylates myosin light chain → cross-bridge cycling.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Relaxation: myosin light-chain phosphatase (MLCP) dephosphorylates myosin.</span> NO → ↑ cGMP → PKG → activates MLCP → relaxation (nitrates, sildenafil). β₂ / cAMP → PKA → inhibits MLCK → relaxation (albuterol).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Latch state</span>: dephosphorylated myosin remains attached → sustained tension with little ATP (sphincters, vascular tone).</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ sources: voltage-gated (L-type; blocked by verapamil, nifedipine) = electromechanical; <b>pharmacomechanical</b> = receptor (α₁, M₃) → Gq → PLC → IP₃ → SR Ca²⁺ release (no AP needed). Rho kinase inhibits MLCP → Ca²⁺ sensitization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Single-unit (visceral)</span>: gap junctions, contract as a syncytium, pacemaker/slow waves, stretch-activated — GI tract, uterus, bladder, small vessels. <b>Multi-unit</b>: each fiber innervated separately, fine control — iris, ciliary muscle, vas deferens, piloerector, large arteries.</li>\n<li>Slow waves (GI, interstitial cells of Cajal) set rhythm; spike potentials (Ca²⁺) cause contraction.</li>\n<li>Slower, more economical, can contract over a wide length range (stress–relaxation/plasticity — bladder fills without pressure rise).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Smooth</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ sensor</td><td class=\"hy\">Troponin C</td><td class=\"hy\">Calmodulin → MLCK</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ca²⁺ source</td><td>SR only (mechanical DHPR–RyR)</td><td>ECF + SR (IP₃)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulation</td><td>Thin-filament</td><td>Thick-filament (myosin phosphorylation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed / ATP use</td><td>Fast / high</td><td>Slow / low (latch)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Myasthenia = postsynaptic AChR Ab (worse with use); Lambert–Eaton = presynaptic Ca²⁺ channel Ab (better with use, SCLC).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Botulinum blocks ACh release (SNAREs); organophosphates block AChE; succinylcholine depolarizing block.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Skeletal E–C: DHPR mechanically opens RyR1; cardiac: Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">ATP binding releases myosin from actin; no ATP = rigor. SERCA relaxes muscle.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Malignant hyperthermia: RyR1, volatile anesthetics/succinylcholine → dantrolene.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle: Ca²⁺–calmodulin–MLCK; relax via MLCP (NO/cGMP); latch state.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Size principle: small type I units first. A band constant.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-biochem": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Glucose uptake</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Transporter</th><th>Location</th><th>Note</th></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT1</td><td>RBCs, brain, BBB, placenta</td><td>Insulin-independent, basal uptake</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT2</td><td>Liver, pancreatic β cells, kidney &amp; intestine (basolateral)</td><td>High Km — glucose sensor, bidirectional</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT3</td><td>Neurons, placenta</td><td>High affinity</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>GLUT4</td><td>Skeletal muscle, adipose, heart</td><td>Insulin-dependent (vesicles move to membrane); exercise also translocates GLUT4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GLUT5</td><td>Intestine, sperm</td><td>Fructose</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Glycolysis (cytosol; all cells)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucose → 2 pyruvate; net <b>2 ATP + 2 NADH</b> (anaerobic: pyruvate → lactate via LDH regenerates NAD⁺; RBCs have no mitochondria — glycolysis only).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Irreversible, regulated steps: hexokinase/glucokinase, PFK-1 (rate-limiting), pyruvate kinase.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Enzyme</th><th>Activators</th><th>Inhibitors</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Hexokinase (most tissues; low Km, high affinity)</td><td>—</td><td class=\"hy\">Glucose-6-phosphate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Glucokinase (liver, β cells; high Km, high Vmax)</td><td>Insulin</td><td>F-6-P (via GK regulatory protein)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>PFK-1 (rate-limiting)</td><td>AMP, fructose-2,6-bisphosphate</td><td>ATP, citrate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pyruvate kinase</td><td class=\"hy\">Fructose-1,6-bisphosphate (feedforward)</td><td>ATP, alanine, glucagon (PKA phosphorylation in liver)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pyruvate dehydrogenase (mitochondria)</td><td>NAD⁺, ADP, Ca²⁺ (exercising muscle), insulin</td><td>Acetyl-CoA, NADH, ATP</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Fructose-2,6-BP (made by PFK-2)</span>: fed state/insulin ↑ PFK-2 activity → ↑ F-2,6-BP → ↑ glycolysis; fasting/glucagon → ↑ FBPase-2 → ↓ F-2,6-BP → gluconeogenesis.</li>\n<li>Substrate-level phosphorylation steps: <b>phosphoglycerate kinase</b> (1,3-BPG → 3-PG) and <b>pyruvate kinase</b> (PEP → pyruvate). NADH made at G3P dehydrogenase.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PDH complex cofactors: TPP (B1), lipoic acid, CoA (B5), FAD (B2), NAD⁺ (B3)</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Tender Loving Care For Nancy”)</span>. Arsenic inhibits lipoic acid. Same cofactors for α-KG DH.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pyruvate kinase deficiency</span>: most common glycolytic enzyme defect → hemolytic anemia (RBCs can’t make ATP), echinocytes; ↑ 2,3-BPG.</li>\n<li>2,3-BPG made in RBCs via the Rapoport–Luebering shunt → right shift of O₂ curve.</li>\n<li>Pyruvate fates: lactate (LDH; anaerobic), alanine (ALT), oxaloacetate (pyruvate carboxylase, biotin; gluconeogenesis), acetyl-CoA (PDH; TCA).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cori cycle</span>: muscle/RBC lactate → liver → glucose (costs 4 net ATP in liver). Glucose–alanine cycle carries N to liver.</li>\n<li><b>Arsenic</b> also inhibits glyceraldehyde-3-P dehydrogenase step (arsenate substitutes for Pi → no ATP from 1,3-BPG). <b>Fluoride</b> inhibits enolase (used in blood glucose tubes).</li>\n<li>Fructose: fructokinase → F-1-P → aldolase B (bypasses PFK-1 → fast metabolism). <span class=\"hy\">Hereditary fructose intolerance (aldolase B)</span>: hypoglycemia, vomiting, liver failure after fruit/sucrose. Essential fructosuria (fructokinase) benign. Galactose: <span class=\"hy\">classic galactosemia (GALT)</span> → cataracts, liver failure, E. coli sepsis; galactokinase deficiency → cataracts only.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Glycogen metabolism</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stored in liver (~10% weight; maintains blood glucose) and muscle (~1–2%; fuels muscle only). <span class=\"hy\">Muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase</span> → can’t release free glucose.</li>\n<li><b>Synthesis</b>: glucose-6-P → glucose-1-P (phosphoglucomutase) → <span class=\"hy\">UDP-glucose</span> (UDP-glucose pyrophosphorylase) → glycogenin primer → <span class=\"hy\">glycogen synthase (rate-limiting; α1→4)</span> → branching enzyme (α1→6, every ~8–10 residues).</li>\n<li><b>Breakdown</b>: <span class=\"hy\">glycogen phosphorylase (rate-limiting; needs PLP/B6)</span> releases glucose-1-P from α1→4 until 4 residues from a branch → <b>debranching enzyme</b> (4-α-glucanotransferase moves 3 residues; α-1,6-glucosidase releases the branch glucose as free glucose) → glucose-1-P → glucose-6-P → (liver) glucose-6-phosphatase → glucose.</li>\n<li>Lysosomal α-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase) degrades a small fraction of glycogen in lysosomes.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Insulin (fed)</td><td>Activates protein phosphatase 1 → <b>dephosphorylates</b>: synthase ON, phosphorylase OFF → glycogen synthesis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Glucagon (liver), epinephrine (liver + muscle)</td><td>cAMP → PKA → phosphorylase kinase → <b>phosphorylates</b>: phosphorylase ON, synthase OFF → glycogenolysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muscle contraction</td><td>Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates phosphorylase kinase; AMP allosterically activates muscle phosphorylase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Liver glucose</td><td>Glucose inhibits liver phosphorylase; G-6-P activates synthase</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Phosphorylation = breakdown mode (glycogenolysis); dephosphorylation = storage mode.</p>\n\n<h3>Glycogen storage diseases — MUST KNOW</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Enzyme</th><th>Key findings</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>I — Von Gierke</td><td>Glucose-6-phosphatase (Ia) / transporter (Ib)</td><td>Severe <b>fasting hypoglycemia</b>, ↑↑ glycogen in liver &amp; kidney (hepatomegaly, renomegaly), <b>lactic acidosis, hyperuricemia (gout), hyperlipidemia</b>, doll-like face; can’t respond to glucagon. Tx: frequent cornstarch feeds; avoid fructose/galactose.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>II — Pompe</td><td>Lysosomal acid α-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase)</td><td><b>Cardiomegaly</b>, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, hypotonia, macroglossia, early death (infantile); normal blood glucose. <span class=\"mn\">“Pompe trashes the Pump (heart, liver, muscle)”</span>. Enzyme replacement.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>III — Cori</td><td>Debranching enzyme (α-1,6-glucosidase)</td><td>Milder Von Gierke: hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, ± myopathy, <b>normal lactate</b>; short outer chains (limit dextrin) accumulate; gluconeogenesis intact.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IV — Andersen</td><td>Branching enzyme</td><td>Abnormal long unbranched glycogen → cirrhosis, liver failure, death in early childhood.</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>V — McArdle</td><td>Muscle glycogen phosphorylase (myophosphorylase)</td><td><b>Exercise intolerance, painful cramps, myoglobinuria</b> (red urine) after exercise, ↑ CK, <b>flat venous lactate curve</b> on ischemic forearm test, “second wind” phenomenon; normal blood glucose.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VI — Hers</td><td>Liver glycogen phosphorylase</td><td>Mild hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VII — Tarui</td><td>Muscle PFK-1</td><td>Like McArdle + hemolytic anemia.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">“Very Poor Carbohydrate Metabolism” → Von Gierke (I), Pompe (II), Cori (III), McArdle (V). All AR.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. GAGs, proteoglycans &amp; carbohydrates in MSK</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>GAGs = repeating disaccharides (amino sugar + uronic acid), <b>negatively charged</b> → attract Na⁺ and water → <span class=\"hy\">resist compression</span> (cartilage); proteoglycan = core protein + GAGs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Aggrecan</span> (cartilage): chondroitin sulfate + keratan sulfate on a core protein, attached by link protein to <b>hyaluronan</b> → huge aggregates.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hyaluronic acid = only non-sulfated GAG, not bound to protein; synovial fluid viscosity, vitreous.</span> Heparin = only intracellular GAG (mast cells). Chondroitin sulfate most abundant (cartilage, bone). Keratan sulfate (cornea, cartilage). Dermatan sulfate (skin, vessels, valves). Heparan sulfate (basement membranes).</li>\n<li>Synthesis: core protein in RER → GAG chains added and sulfated in Golgi (PAPS sulfate donor). Degradation in lysosomes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Mucopolysaccharidoses</span> (lysosomal GAG degradation defects; urinary GAGs, coarse facies, skeletal dysplasia): <b>Hurler</b> (α-L-iduronidase, AR) — corneal clouding, gargoylism, intellectual disability, early death; <b>Hunter</b> (iduronate sulfatase, X-linked) — no corneal clouding, aggressive behavior <span class=\"mn\">(“Hunters see clearly and aim for the X”)</span>; heparan + dermatan sulfate accumulate.</li>\n<li>Osteoarthritis: ↓ proteoglycans → cartilage loses water-holding capacity; glucosamine/chondroitin supplements.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Collagen</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Structure: <span class=\"hy\">triple helix of α chains with Gly-X-Y repeats (glycine every 3rd residue — only AA small enough for the center); X/Y often proline and hydroxyproline/hydroxylysine.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>Location</th><th>Detail / disease</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Translation of preprocollagen</td><td>RER</td><td>Signal peptide cleaved</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>2. Hydroxylation of Pro &amp; Lys</td><td>RER</td><td>Prolyl &amp; lysyl hydroxylase need <b>vitamin C</b> (+ Fe²⁺, O₂, α-KG) → <b>scurvy</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Glycosylation of hydroxylysine</td><td>RER</td><td>Galactose/glucose added</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>4. Triple helix (procollagen) via disulfides at C-propeptides</td><td>RER</td><td><b>Osteogenesis imperfecta</b> — can’t form triple helix (Gly substitutions)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5. Secretion</td><td>Golgi → exocytosis</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>6. Propeptide cleavage → tropocollagen</td><td>Extracellular</td><td>Procollagen peptidases; defect → <b>Ehlers–Danlos (dermatosparaxis)</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>7. Cross-linking → fibrils</td><td>Extracellular</td><td><b>Lysyl oxidase (copper)</b> covalent cross-links; <b>Menkes disease</b> (ATP7A, ↓ Cu: kinky brittle hair, hypotonia, growth delay); Ehlers–Danlos; lathyrism</td></tr></table>\n<table><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Defect</th><th>Features</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Scurvy</td><td>Vitamin C → ↓ hydroxylation</td><td>Swollen bleeding gums, perifollicular hemorrhage, corkscrew hairs, poor wound healing, hemarthrosis</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Osteogenesis imperfecta</td><td>Type I collagen (COL1A1/2), AD</td><td>Multiple fractures with minimal trauma (mimics child abuse), <b>blue sclerae</b>, hearing loss (otosclerosis), dentinogenesis imperfecta</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ehlers–Danlos</td><td>Collagen processing (classic: type V; vascular: type III)</td><td>Hyperextensible skin, hypermobile joints, easy bruising; vascular type → arterial/organ rupture, berry aneurysms</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Menkes</td><td>Copper transport (ATP7A), XR</td><td>↓ lysyl oxidase → brittle kinky hair, neurodegeneration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alport</td><td>Type IV collagen</td><td>Hematuria/nephritis, sensorineural deafness, lens/eye defects <span class=\"mn\">(“can’t see, can’t pee, can’t hear”)</span></td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Collagen types: I Bone/skin/tendon; II Cartilage; III Reticulin (vessels, granulation); IV Basement membrane.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Elastin</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Stretchy protein in lungs, large arteries, elastic ligaments (ligamentum flavum), skin, vocal cords.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rich in nonhydroxylated proline, glycine and lysine; lysyl oxidase forms desmosine cross-links</span>; assembled on a <b>fibrillin</b> scaffold. Broken down by elastase (inhibited by α₁-antitrypsin).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Marfan syndrome</span>: FBN1 (fibrillin-1), AD — tall, long limbs, arachnodactyly, pectus, <b>upward lens subluxation</b>, MVP, <b>aortic root dilation → dissection</b>, hypermobility. (Homocystinuria: downward lens dislocation, thrombosis, intellectual disability.)</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">α₁-antitrypsin deficiency</span>: unopposed elastase → panacinar emphysema (lower lobes, young, nonsmoker) + cirrhosis (misfolded PiZ retained in hepatocyte ER, PAS+ globules).</li>\n<li>Wrinkles of aging: ↓ collagen &amp; elastin synthesis, UV damage (solar elastosis).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">PFK-1 rate-limiting (activated by AMP, F-2,6-BP; inhibited by ATP, citrate); pyruvate kinase deficiency → hemolysis.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Glycogen synthase / phosphorylase are rate-limiting; insulin dephosphorylates (store), glucagon/epi phosphorylate (break down).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Von Gierke: hypoglycemia + lactic acidosis + gout + hepatomegaly. Pompe: cardiomegaly (lysosomal). Cori: Von Gierke-like but normal lactate. McArdle: cramps, myoglobinuria, flat lactate.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Vitamin C → hydroxylation (scurvy); copper → lysyl oxidase cross-links (Menkes); OI = type I collagen, blue sclerae.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Marfan = fibrillin-1 (upward lens, aortic root); hyaluronic acid non-sulfated; Hunter = X-linked, no corneal clouding.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-anatomy": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Back, shoulder &amp; scapular region</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Action / lesion</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Trapezius</td><td>Spinal accessory (CN XI)</td><td>Elevates/retracts/rotates scapula up; CN XI injury (posterior triangle surgery/node biopsy) → shoulder droop, can’t shrug or abduct above 90°</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Latissimus dorsi</td><td>Thoracodorsal (C6–8)</td><td>Extends, adducts, medially rotates arm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Levator scapulae, rhomboids</td><td>Dorsal scapular (C5)</td><td>Elevate/retract scapula; lesion → mild lateral scapular winging</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Serratus anterior</td><td>Long thoracic (C5–7)</td><td>Protracts, upward rotates scapula (abduction &gt;90°); lesion (mastectomy/axillary dissection, stab) → <b>medial winged scapula</b> pushing against wall</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Deltoid</td><td>Axillary (C5–6)</td><td>Abducts 15–90°; lesion → flat shoulder, ↓ abduction, numb “regimental badge” area</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Supraspinatus</td><td>Suprascapular</td><td>Initiates abduction (0–15°); <b>most commonly torn rotator cuff</b>; impingement under acromion; empty-can test, painful arc</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infraspinatus</td><td>Suprascapular</td><td>Lateral rotation (pitching injury)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teres minor</td><td>Axillary</td><td>Lateral rotation, adduction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subscapularis</td><td>Upper &amp; lower subscapular</td><td>Medial rotation, adduction (lift-off test)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teres major</td><td>Lower subscapular</td><td>Medial rotation, adduction, extension</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Rotator cuff = SITS</span> (Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Teres minor, Subscapularis); all insert on greater tubercle except subscapularis (lesser).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Quadrangular space</span> (teres minor, teres major, long head triceps, humerus): <b>axillary nerve + posterior circumflex humeral artery</b>. Triangular space: circumflex scapular artery. <b>Triangular interval</b>: radial nerve + profunda brachii.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Shoulder dislocation is usually anterior (inferior)</span> → axillary nerve at risk. Posterior dislocation: seizures/electrocution.</li>\n<li>Clavicle fracture (middle third): medial fragment up (SCM), lateral fragment down (arm weight) and in (pec major). Triangle of auscultation: trapezius, latissimus, scapula.</li>\n<li>Scapular anastomosis (subclavian ↔ axillary) bypasses axillary/subclavian obstruction.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Brachial plexus &amp; spinal levels</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Roots C5–T1 → Trunks (upper C5–6, middle C7, lower C8–T1) → Divisions (anterior/posterior) → Cords (lateral, posterior, medial, named relative to axillary artery) → Branches</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“Randy Travis Drinks Cold Beer”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Roots give <b>dorsal scapular</b> (C5) and <b>long thoracic</b> (C5–7); upper trunk gives <b>suprascapular</b> and nerve to subclavius.</li>\n<li>Lateral cord → musculocutaneous, lateral pectoral, lateral root of median. Medial cord → ulnar, medial pectoral, medial cutaneous nerves, medial root of median. <span class=\"hy\">Posterior cord → axillary, radial, upper/lower subscapular, thoracodorsal</span> <span class=\"mn\">(STAR: Subscapulars, Thoracodorsal, Axillary, Radial)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Injury</th><th>Cause</th><th>Roots</th><th>Picture</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Erb palsy (upper trunk)</td><td>Infant lateral neck traction in delivery; adult fall on shoulder</td><td>C5–C6</td><td><b>“Waiter’s tip”</b>: arm adducted (deltoid, supraspinatus), medially rotated (infraspinatus), elbow extended (biceps/brachialis), forearm pronated</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Klumpke palsy (lower trunk)</td><td>Upward arm traction (grabbing branch, delivery with arm up); Pancoast tumor, cervical rib</td><td>C8–T1</td><td><b>Total claw hand</b> (intrinsic loss), ± <b>Horner syndrome</b> (T1 sympathetics)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thoracic outlet syndrome</td><td>Cervical rib, scalene compression</td><td>Lower trunk + subclavian vessels</td><td>Hand intrinsic atrophy, ischemia, pain</td></tr></table>\n<table><tr><th>Reflex</th><th>Root</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Biceps</td><td>C5 (C5–6)</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Brachioradialis</td><td>C6</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Triceps</td><td>C7</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Patellar</td><td>L4 (L3–4)</td></tr><tr class=\"hy\"><td>Achilles</td><td>S1</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Reflexes count up: S1–2 ankle, L3–4 knee, C5–6 biceps, C7–8 triceps. Dermatomes: C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, T4 nipple, T10 umbilicus, L4 knee/medial leg, L5 dorsum of foot, S1 lateral foot/sole.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Arm, forearm &amp; elbow</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Anterior arm</b> (musculocutaneous): biceps (supination &gt; flexion; radial tuberosity), brachialis (main elbow flexor), coracobrachialis. <b>Posterior arm</b> (radial): triceps, anconeus.</li>\n<li><b>Anterior forearm</b> (median, except FCU and medial half FDP = ulnar): pronators, wrist &amp; finger flexors from <b>medial epicondyle</b> (golfer’s elbow). <b>Posterior forearm</b> (radial/PIN): extensors from <b>lateral epicondyle</b> (<span class=\"hy\">tennis elbow = ECRB</span>). Brachioradialis = radial nerve but flexes elbow (midprone).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Supination: biceps (most powerful) + supinator. Pronation: pronator teres + pronator quadratus.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cubital fossa (lateral → medial): biceps tendon, brachial artery, median nerve</span> <span class=\"mn\">(“TAN”: Tendon, Artery, Nerve; radial nerve lateral)</span>. Median cubital vein superficial (venipuncture). Boundaries: brachioradialis (lateral), pronator teres (medial).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Supracondylar humerus fracture</span> (children, FOOSH): brachial artery + median nerve injury → Volkmann ischemic contracture (compartment syndrome).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Nursemaid’s elbow</span>: radial head subluxation out of the annular ligament (pulling a child’s arm).</li>\n<li>Elbow joint: humeroulnar (hinge), humeroradial; proximal radioulnar (pivot). UCL (Tommy John) resists valgus.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Hand &amp; wrist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Carpal bones: <span class=\"mn\">“So Long To Pinky, Here Comes The Thumb”</span> — proximal row lateral → medial: Scaphoid, Lunate, Triquetrum, Pisiform; distal row medial → lateral: Hamate, Capitate, Trapezoid, Trapezium.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Scaphoid = most commonly fractured carpal</span> (FOOSH; tender in anatomical snuffbox; may be X-ray occult) → <b>avascular necrosis</b> of proximal pole (retrograde blood supply) and nonunion. <span class=\"hy\">Lunate = most commonly dislocated</span> (anteriorly) → acute carpal tunnel syndrome. Hook of hamate fracture → ulnar nerve (Guyon canal).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Carpal tunnel</span> (flexor retinaculum roof): median nerve + 9 tendons (4 FDS, 4 FDP, FPL). FCR not inside (separate). Compression (pregnancy, RA, hypothyroid, diabetes, amyloid, repetitive use) → numb lateral 3½ digits, <b>thenar atrophy</b>, Tinel/Phalen positive; <b>palm sensation spared</b> (palmar cutaneous branch passes superficial to retinaculum).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Guyon canal</span> (pisiform &amp; hook of hamate): ulnar nerve + artery (cyclists).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Anatomical snuffbox</span>: lateral = APL &amp; EPB; medial = EPL; floor = scaphoid &amp; trapezium; contains <b>radial artery</b> (and cephalic vein origin, superficial radial nerve over roof).</li>\n<li><b>Thenar</b> (median recurrent branch): APB, FPB (superficial head), opponens pollicis. <b>Hypothenar</b> (ulnar): ADM, FDMB, ODM. <b>Adductor pollicis</b>, all interossei, lumbricals 3–4 = <b>ulnar</b>; lumbricals 1–2 = median.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Interossei: PAD (palmar adduct), DAB (dorsal abduct)</span>; lumbricals flex MCP + extend IP. Card test (palmar interossei, ulnar).</li>\n<li>Extensor compartments (6): APL/EPB (de Quervain tenosynovitis, Finkelstein test) | ECRL/ECRB | EPL (around Lister tubercle) | ED/EI | EDM | ECU.</li>\n<li>Deformities: mallet finger (terminal extensor), boutonnière (central slip), swan-neck (RA), Dupuytren (palmar fascia), jersey finger (FDP avulsion, ring finger), gamekeeper/skier thumb (UCL of thumb MCP).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Upper limb nerve injuries — MUST KNOW</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Injured by</th><th>Motor loss / sign</th><th>Sensory loss</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Axillary (C5–6)</td><td>Surgical neck fracture, anterior shoulder dislocation</td><td>Deltoid (abduction 15–90°), teres minor; flat shoulder</td><td>Lateral shoulder (“regimental badge”)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Musculocutaneous (C5–7)</td><td>Upper trunk compression</td><td>Biceps, brachialis, coracobrachialis → weak elbow flexion/supination</td><td>Lateral forearm</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Radial (C5–T1)</td><td>Midshaft humerus fracture (radial groove), “Saturday night palsy”, crutches (axilla), radial head fracture (PIN)</td><td><b>Wrist drop</b>, ↓ finger/thumb extension; axillary lesion also triceps loss; midshaft spares triceps. <b>PIN</b>: finger drop, wrist extends with radial deviation (ECRL spared), no sensory loss</td><td>Posterior arm/forearm, dorsal lateral hand &amp; <b>first dorsal web space</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Median (C5–T1)</td><td>Supracondylar fracture (proximal), carpal tunnel/wrist laceration (distal)</td><td>Proximal: <b>“hand of benediction” / OK sign</b> when making a fist (index &amp; middle can’t flex). Distal: <b>ape hand</b>, thenar atrophy, ↓ opposition. <b>AIN</b>: can’t make OK sign, no sensory loss</td><td>Palmar lateral 3½ digits (+ thenar palm if proximal)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ulnar (C8–T1)</td><td>Medial epicondyle fracture (“funny bone”), Guyon canal (hook of hamate, cyclists)</td><td><b>Ulnar claw</b> (4th, 5th: MCP hyperextension, IP flexion) — worse with distal lesion (<b>ulnar paradox</b>: proximal lesion also paralyzes FDP to 4–5, less clawing); ↓ abduction/adduction of fingers; <b>Froment sign</b> (adductor pollicis); hypothenar &amp; 1st dorsal interosseous wasting</td><td>Medial 1½ digits, hypothenar</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Long thoracic (C5–7)</td><td>Mastectomy, axillary node dissection, stab</td><td>Winged scapula (medial), can’t abduct &gt;90°</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Recurrent branch of median</td><td>Superficial laceration of thenar eminence</td><td>Thenar paralysis, ↓ opposition</td><td>None</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">Claw hand: lumbricals lost → MCP extended, IP flexed. “Hand of benediction” appears when trying to make a fist (proximal median); ulnar claw appears at rest (distal ulnar).</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Breast &amp; axilla</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">~75% of breast lymph → axillary nodes</span> (pectoral/anterior first; sentinel node); medial breast → parasternal (internal thoracic) nodes. Suspensory (Cooper) ligaments → skin dimpling; lymphatic blockage → peau d’orange.</li>\n<li>Axilla walls: anterior (pec major/minor), posterior (subscapularis, teres major, latissimus), medial (serratus anterior, ribs), lateral (intertubercular groove). Contents: axillary artery/vein, brachial plexus cords, nodes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Axillary artery divided by pectoralis minor into 3 parts</span>: 1st part: superior thoracic (1 branch); 2nd: thoracoacromial, lateral thoracic (2); 3rd: subscapular, anterior &amp; posterior circumflex humeral (3) <span class=\"mn\">(“Screw The Lawyer Save A Patient”)</span>.</li>\n<li>Axillary dissection risks: <b>long thoracic</b> (winging), <b>thoracodorsal</b> (weak latissimus), intercostobrachial (numb medial arm), lymphedema.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Upper limb blood supply</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Subclavian → (1st rib lateral border) axillary → (teres major inferior border) <b>brachial</b> → (cubital fossa) radial + ulnar.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Profunda brachii runs with the radial nerve in the radial groove</span> (midshaft fractures). Posterior circumflex humeral with axillary nerve (surgical neck).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Superficial palmar arch = mainly ulnar artery; deep palmar arch = mainly radial artery.</span> <b>Allen test</b> checks collateral flow before radial artery puncture/cannulation.</li>\n<li>Veins: cephalic (lateral, deltopectoral groove → axillary), basilic (medial → brachial/axillary), median cubital (connects; venipuncture).</li>\n<li>BP cuff over brachial artery; pulses: radial at wrist lateral to FCR tendon.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Gluteal region &amp; hip joint</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Muscles</th><th>Lesion</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Superior gluteal (L4–S1)</td><td>Gluteus medius, minimus, TFL (abduct, medially rotate thigh; keep pelvis level)</td><td><b>Trendelenburg sign</b>: pelvis drops on the <b>opposite (unsupported) side</b> when standing on the affected limb; waddling gait. Cause: IM injection, hip surgery</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Inferior gluteal (L5–S2)</td><td>Gluteus maximus (extension, lateral rotation; climbing stairs, rising from a chair)</td><td>Difficulty climbing stairs, rising from seated; posterior lurch</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Piriformis = key landmark</span>: superior gluteal nerve/vessels exit <b>above</b> it; sciatic, inferior gluteal, pudendal nerves, posterior femoral cutaneous exit <b>below</b> it (greater sciatic foramen). Pudendal re-enters via lesser sciatic foramen (block near ischial spine).</li>\n<li>Lateral rotators: piriformis, superior/inferior gemelli, obturator internus (nerve to obturator internus), quadratus femoris, obturator externus (obturator n.).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">IM injection: superolateral quadrant of buttock (or ventrogluteal) to avoid sciatic nerve.</span></li>\n<li>Hip joint: ball-and-socket; <span class=\"hy\">iliofemoral ligament (Y of Bigelow) strongest, prevents hyperextension</span>; pubofemoral, ischiofemoral; ligament of head (artery of obturator origin, important in children).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Femoral head blood supply mainly medial circumflex femoral artery</span> (retinacular branches) → femoral neck fracture → <b>avascular necrosis</b>. Displaced fracture: limb shortened, laterally rotated.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Posterior hip dislocation</span> (dashboard injury, most common): limb shortened, <b>flexed, adducted, medially rotated</b>; sciatic nerve risk. Anterior: abducted, laterally rotated.</li>\n<li>Pediatric hip: DDH (Ortolani/Barlow), Legg–Calvé–Perthes (AVN, 4–10 y), SCFE (obese adolescent, knee pain, limp).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Thigh, femoral triangle &amp; adductor canal</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anterior compartment (femoral n.): quadriceps (knee extension; rectus femoris also flexes hip), sartorius, iliopsoas (strongest hip flexor). Medial (obturator n.): adductors, gracilis, (pectineus femoral). Posterior (tibial division of sciatic): hamstrings — semitendinosus, semimembranosus, biceps femoris long head (short head = common fibular).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Femoral triangle: inguinal ligament (superior), sartorius (lateral), adductor longus (medial). Contents lateral → medial: Nerve, Artery, Vein, (Empty space), Lymphatics</span> <span class=\"mn\">(NAVEL)</span>. <b>Femoral sheath</b> contains artery, vein and femoral canal (lymphatics) — NOT the nerve. <span class=\"hy\">Femoral hernia</span> through femoral canal, below and lateral to pubic tubercle, more common in women, high strangulation risk.</li>\n<li>Femoral artery pulse at mid-inguinal point; access for catheterization.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Adductor (Hunter) canal</span>: femoral artery, femoral vein, <b>saphenous nerve</b>, nerve to vastus medialis; roof = sartorius. Femoral vessels pass through the <b>adductor hiatus</b> → popliteal.</li>\n<li>Pes anserinus (medial tibia): Sartorius, Gracilis, semiTendinosus <span class=\"mn\">(“Say Grace before Tea”)</span>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Knee joint &amp; popliteal fossa</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Structure</th><th>Function / injury</th><th>Test</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>ACL (lateral femoral condyle → anterior tibia)</td><td>Prevents anterior tibial translation; non-contact pivot/twist</td><td>Anterior drawer, <b>Lachman</b></td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>PCL (medial condyle → posterior tibia)</td><td>Prevents posterior translation; dashboard injury</td><td>Posterior drawer, sag sign</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>MCL</td><td>Resists valgus (blow to lateral knee); attached to medial meniscus</td><td>Valgus stress</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LCL</td><td>Resists varus; not attached to meniscus</td><td>Varus stress</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Medial meniscus</td><td>C-shaped, less mobile → injured more</td><td>McMurray; locking, joint-line pain</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">“Unhappy triad”: ACL + MCL + meniscus</span> (lateral blow to planted knee; classically medial meniscus, though lateral is more common acutely).</li>\n<li>Screw-home/locking; <b>popliteus unlocks</b> the knee (laterally rotates femur). Prepatellar bursitis (“housemaid’s knee”); Baker cyst (semimembranosus–gastrocnemius bursa).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Popliteal fossa</span>: borders biceps femoris (superolateral), semimembranosus/semitendinosus (superomedial), gastrocnemius heads (inferior). Contents superficial → deep: <b>tibial nerve, popliteal vein, popliteal artery</b> (deepest; aneurysm, injury in knee dislocation). Common fibular nerve along biceps tendon.</li>\n<li>Patellar dislocation usually lateral (vastus medialis oblique resists). Osgood–Schlatter = tibial tuberosity apophysitis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Leg compartments</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Compartment</th><th>Muscles</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Artery</th><th>Action</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Anterior</td><td>Tibialis anterior, EHL, EDL, fibularis tertius</td><td>Deep fibular</td><td>Anterior tibial (→ dorsalis pedis)</td><td>Dorsiflexion, toe extension, inversion (TA)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral</td><td>Fibularis longus, brevis</td><td>Superficial fibular</td><td>(Fibular artery branches)</td><td>Eversion</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Posterior superficial</td><td>Gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris</td><td>Tibial</td><td>Posterior tibial</td><td>Plantarflexion</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Posterior deep</td><td>Tibialis posterior, FDL, FHL, popliteus</td><td>Tibial</td><td>Posterior tibial, fibular</td><td>Plantarflexion, inversion, toe flexion</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Anterior compartment syndrome</span> (tibial fracture, crush, reperfusion): pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense compartment; late pulselessness → fasciotomy. Shin splints = medial tibial stress.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Behind medial malleolus (tarsal tunnel): “Tom, Dick And Very Nervous Harry”</span> — Tibialis posterior, flexor Digitorum longus, posterior tibial Artery, Vein, tibial Nerve, flexor Hallucis longus. Pulse: posterior tibial artery here; dorsalis pedis lateral to EHL tendon on dorsum.</li>\n<li>Achilles (calcaneal) tendon rupture: pop, can’t plantarflex strongly; <b>Thompson test</b> positive.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g12\">12. Ankle &amp; foot</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Ankle (talocrural) = hinge: dorsiflexion/plantarflexion; <span class=\"hy\">most stable in dorsiflexion</span> (wider anterior talus). Inversion/eversion at <b>subtalar</b> and transverse tarsal joints.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Inversion sprain (most common): anterior talofibular ligament (ATFL) tears first</span>, then calcaneofibular. Deltoid ligament (medial) resists eversion — so strong that eversion injuries avulse the medial malleolus.</li>\n<li>Arches: medial longitudinal (keystone talus head/navicular; spring ligament, tibialis posterior, plantar aponeurosis), lateral longitudinal, transverse (fibularis longus).</li>\n<li>Plantar fasciitis (heel pain worst first steps in morning). Jones fracture (5th MT diaphysis, poor healing) vs avulsion of 5th MT tuberosity (fibularis brevis). March fracture (2nd MT). Lisfranc injury (tarsometatarsal).</li>\n<li>Sole: 4 layers; medial plantar nerve (≈ median) and lateral plantar nerve (≈ ulnar).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g13\">13. Lower limb nerves &amp; spinal levels — MUST KNOW</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Nerve</th><th>Injured by</th><th>Motor deficit</th><th>Sensory loss</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Common fibular (L4–S2)</td><td><b>Fibular neck fracture</b>, tight cast, leg crossing, lateral knee trauma</td><td><b>Foot drop</b> (↓ dorsiflexion), steppage gait, ↓ eversion; foot plantarflexed &amp; inverted <span class=\"mn\">(“PED = Peroneal Everts and Dorsiflexes; if injured, foot dropPED”)</span></td><td>Lateral leg, dorsum of foot</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deep fibular</td><td>Anterior compartment syndrome</td><td>Foot drop (TA, EHL, EDL)</td><td><b>First web space</b> only</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Superficial fibular</td><td>Lateral leg trauma</td><td>↓ eversion</td><td>Dorsum of foot (spares 1st web space)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Tibial (L4–S3)</td><td>Knee trauma, Baker cyst, tarsal tunnel</td><td>Can’t plantarflex/invert, can’t stand on toes; foot dorsiflexed &amp; everted <span class=\"mn\">(“TIP = Tibial Inverts and Plantarflexes; if injured, can’t stand on TIPtoes”)</span></td><td>Sole of foot</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Femoral (L2–L4)</td><td>Pelvic fracture, femoral catheterization, psoas hematoma</td><td>↓ knee extension, ↓ hip flexion; ↓ patellar reflex</td><td>Anterior thigh, medial leg (saphenous)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Obturator (L2–L4)</td><td>Pelvic surgery, labor, anterior hip dislocation</td><td>↓ thigh adduction</td><td>Medial thigh</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Superior gluteal (L4–S1)</td><td>IM injection, hip surgery</td><td>Trendelenburg sign (contralateral pelvic drop)</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inferior gluteal (L5–S2)</td><td>Posterior hip dislocation</td><td>Can’t climb stairs / rise from seat</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Sciatic (L4–S3)</td><td>Posterior hip dislocation, misplaced IM injection</td><td>Hamstrings + all below-knee muscles</td><td>Most of leg and foot (not medial)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral femoral cutaneous (L2–3)</td><td>Tight belts, obesity, pregnancy (at ASIS)</td><td>None</td><td><b>Meralgia paresthetica</b>: anterolateral thigh</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Disc herniation: usually posterolateral at L4–L5 or L5–S1 → compresses the nerve root BELOW</span> (L4–5 disc → L5 root: weak dorsiflexion/big toe extension, dorsum of foot numb; L5–S1 → S1: ↓ Achilles reflex, lateral foot). Positive straight-leg raise.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cauda equina syndrome</span>: saddle anesthesia, bowel/bladder dysfunction, bilateral sciatica → emergency.</li>\n<li>Lumbar puncture between L3–L4 or L4–L5 (iliac crest = L4 spinous process). Spinal cord ends L1–L2 in adults (conus medullaris).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g14\">14. Lower limb vessels &amp; lymphatics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>External iliac → (under inguinal ligament) femoral → <b>profunda femoris</b> (medial &amp; lateral circumflex femoral; perforators) → femoral passes adductor hiatus → <b>popliteal</b> → anterior tibial (→ dorsalis pedis) + tibioperoneal trunk (posterior tibial + fibular).</li>\n<li>Pulses: femoral (mid-inguinal point), popliteal (deep, knee flexed), posterior tibial (behind medial malleolus), <b>dorsalis pedis</b> (lateral to EHL tendon).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Great saphenous vein: anterior to medial malleolus → medial leg/thigh → joins femoral vein at the saphenous opening</span> (CABG graft; saphenous nerve runs with it below knee). Small saphenous: behind lateral malleolus → popliteal vein (with sural nerve).</li>\n<li>DVT: deep veins of calf/popliteal/femoral → PE. Varicose veins: incompetent valves of superficial/perforator veins.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Lymph: superficial inguinal nodes drain lower limb (superficial), skin below umbilicus, perineum, external genitalia (not testes), anal canal below pectinate line, lower vagina.</span> Popliteal nodes drain the lateral foot/posterolateral leg (small saphenous territory). Testes → para-aortic nodes.</li>\n</ul>", "msk-embryology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Origins of the musculoskeletal system</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Source</th><th>Gives rise to</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Paraxial mesoderm → somites (from week 3; ~42–44 pairs)</td><td><b>Sclerotome</b> → vertebrae, ribs, part of occipital bone; <b>myotome</b> → skeletal muscle of trunk &amp; limbs; <b>dermatome</b> → dermis of back</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Lateral plate mesoderm (somatic/parietal layer)</td><td>Limb bones, cartilage, tendons, ligaments, sternum, dermis of limbs/body wall</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neural crest</td><td>Most of the face and skull vault (viscerocranium &amp; frontal/parietal parts), melanocytes, Schwann cells</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notochord</td><td class=\"hy\">Nucleus pulposus of intervertebral disc; induces neural tube &amp; sclerotome</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Surface ectoderm</td><td>Epidermis, hair, nails, glands, AER of limb bud</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Cartilage &amp; bone formation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Mesenchyme condenses → chondroblasts (SOX9) or osteoblasts (<b>RUNX2/CBFA1</b>, osterix).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intramembranous ossification: mesenchyme → bone directly (no cartilage) — flat bones of the skull vault, face, mandible, clavicle (also first bone to ossify, week 5–6).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Endochondral ossification: hyaline cartilage model replaced by bone — long bones, vertebrae, pelvis, base of skull.</span> Steps: cartilage model → chondrocytes hypertrophy &amp; matrix calcifies → periosteal bone collar → nutrient artery invades (periosteal bud) → <b>primary ossification center in diaphysis</b> (by week 12) → <b>secondary centers in epiphyses</b> (mostly after birth; distal femur at birth) → epiphyseal plates remain until puberty ends.</li>\n<li>Cartilage growth: interstitial (from within) and appositional (from perichondrium).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cleidocranial dysplasia</span>: RUNX2 mutation (AD) → absent/hypoplastic clavicles (shoulders meet in midline), delayed fontanelle closure, wide sutures, supernumerary teeth — intramembranous bones affected.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Long bone growth</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Length = epiphyseal (growth) plate. Width = appositional growth from periosteum (osteoblasts add, osteoclasts remodel inner surface).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Growth plate zones (epiphysis → diaphysis): reserve/resting → proliferative (columns, stacked coins) → hypertrophic (maturation) → calcified cartilage → ossification.</span></li>\n<li>Regulators: GH/IGF-1 (proliferation), thyroid hormone, <b>estrogen closes the plates</b> (in both sexes — aromatase/estrogen receptor deficiency → tall stature, open plates), PTHrP/Indian hedgehog loop, FGFR3 (inhibits proliferation).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Achondroplasia</span>: activating FGFR3 mutation (AD; most cases new, ↑ paternal age) → ↓ chondrocyte proliferation in the growth plate → short limbs, normal trunk, large head, frontal bossing, normal intelligence; intramembranous bone normal.</li>\n<li>Rickets (vitamin D deficiency, children): defective mineralization at the hypertrophic zone → widened plates, bowed legs, rachitic rosary, craniotabes. Salter–Harris fractures involve the plate.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gigantism</span> (↑ GH before plate closure) vs <b>acromegaly</b> (after closure — only appositional growth: hands, feet, jaw).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Vertebrae &amp; bone disorders</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Resegmentation</span>: each sclerotome splits; the caudal half of one fuses with the cranial half of the next → vertebral body is <b>intersegmental</b>; spinal nerves exit between vertebrae; myotomes bridge discs (so muscles move the spine).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Intervertebral disc: nucleus pulposus = notochord remnant; annulus fibrosus = sclerotome (fibrocartilage).</span> Herniation = nucleus through annulus (posterolateral).</li>\n<li>Neural arch closure defects: <span class=\"hy\">spina bifida occulta</span> (failure of vertebral arch fusion, no herniation; tuft of hair/dimple at L5–S1; normal AFP), <b>meningocele</b> (meninges herniate), <b>myelomeningocele</b> (meninges + cord; ↑ AFP; Arnold–Chiari II). Folate prevents NTDs.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Klippel–Feil syndrome</span>: fusion of cervical vertebrae → short neck, low hairline, limited neck motion.</li>\n<li><b>Hemivertebra</b> (failure of half a vertebra to form) → congenital scoliosis. Scoliosis (lateral), kyphosis (thoracic hump), lordosis (lumbar).</li>\n<li>Cervical rib (from C7) → thoracic outlet syndrome. Lumbar rib, sacralization of L5 / lumbarization of S1.</li>\n<li>Primary curvatures (thoracic, sacral — fetal) vs secondary (cervical when holding head up, lumbar when walking).</li>\n<li>Other congenital bone disorders: <b>osteogenesis imperfecta</b> (type I collagen, blue sclerae, fractures), <b>osteopetrosis</b> (osteoclast failure, dense brittle bones), <b>Marfan</b> (fibrillin-1), <b>arthrogryposis</b> (multiple joint contractures from ↓ fetal movement/oligohydramnios).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Skull, sutures &amp; fontanelles</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Neurocranium</span> (encloses brain): <b>membranous part</b> = flat bones of the vault (intramembranous; neural crest &amp; paraxial mesoderm); <b>cartilaginous part (chondrocranium)</b> = base of skull (endochondral). <b>Viscerocranium</b> = face (mainly neural crest from pharyngeal arches 1–2).</li>\n<li>Sutures (dense CT) allow molding at birth and brain growth. <span class=\"hy\">Anterior fontanelle (diamond; frontal–parietal junction) closes ~18–24 months; posterior fontanelle (triangular) closes ~2–3 months.</span> Bulging fontanelle → ↑ ICP; sunken → dehydration.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Craniosynostosis</span> = premature suture closure → skull grows parallel to the fused suture:\n<ul><li class=\"hy\"><b>Sagittal</b> (most common, males) → <b>scaphocephaly / dolichocephaly</b> (long, narrow head).</li>\n<li><b>Coronal</b> (bilateral) → brachycephaly (short, wide; Apert/Crouzon — FGFR2); unilateral → anterior plagiocephaly.</li>\n<li><b>Lambdoid</b> (unilateral) → posterior plagiocephaly (vs positional plagiocephaly from sleeping position — far more common).</li>\n<li><b>Metopic</b> → trigonocephaly (triangular forehead).</li>\n<li>Multiple sutures → oxycephaly/turricephaly (tower skull), cloverleaf skull (FGFR).</li></ul></li>\n<li>Microcephaly (small brain → small skull; Zika, alcohol) vs anencephaly (failure of anterior neuropore — no vault).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Limb development</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Limb buds appear end of week 4 (upper slightly before lower); key period weeks 4–8</span> — most limb defects (e.g., <b>thalidomide → phocomelia/amelia</b>, days 24–36).</li>\n<li>Bud = lateral plate mesoderm core (bones, CT) covered by ectoderm; <b>muscle from somite myotomes</b> migrates in (dorsal/ventral muscle masses → extensors/flexors).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Signaling center</th><th>Molecule</th><th>Axis / role</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Apical ectodermal ridge (AER)</td><td>FGFs (FGF8)</td><td>Proximal–distal outgrowth; removal → truncated limb</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Zone of polarizing activity (ZPA), posterior</td><td>Sonic hedgehog (SHH)</td><td>Anterior–posterior (thumb ↔ little finger); ectopic anterior ZPA → mirror duplication / polydactyly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dorsal ectoderm</td><td>WNT7a</td><td>Dorsal–ventral (nails dorsal)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HOX genes</td><td>—</td><td>Segment identity; mutations → limb malformations (synpolydactyly)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Digits separate by apoptosis</span> in interdigital regions (weeks 6–8; BMPs) → failure = <b>syndactyly</b>. Extra digits = polydactyly.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Limb rotation (week 7)</span>: upper limb rotates <b>90° laterally</b> (extensors posterior, thumb lateral); lower limb rotates <b>90° medially</b> (extensors anterior, big toe medial) → dermatomes spiral in the leg.</li>\n<li>Defects: amelia (absent limb), meromelia (partial), phocomelia (hands/feet attached close to trunk), clubfoot (talipes equinovarus — most common; inverted, plantarflexed), congenital hip dysplasia, amniotic band syndrome (constriction rings/amputations).</li>\n<li>Joints: interzones of mesenchyme → synovial joints (cavity by apoptosis); fibrous and cartilaginous joints form where mesenchyme differentiates.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Muscle development</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Skeletal muscle: from paraxial mesoderm (somite myotomes)</span>; head muscles from somitomeres/pharyngeal arch mesoderm. Myoblasts (MyoD, Myf5, myogenin) <b>fuse</b> → multinucleated myotubes → fibers; satellite cells remain as stem cells.</li>\n<li><b>Epimere</b> (dorsal) → deep back extensors (dorsal rami); <b>hypomere</b> (ventral) → body wall &amp; limb muscles (ventral rami).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle: splanchnic (visceral) lateral plate mesoderm</span> (gut, vessels); iris sphincter/dilator from neuroectoderm. <b>Cardiac</b>: splanchnic mesoderm around the heart tube.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Poland sequence</span>: absent pectoralis major (sternocostal head) ± ipsilateral syndactyly/breast hypoplasia. <b>Prune belly</b> syndrome: absent abdominal wall muscles. Congenital torticollis (SCM fibrosis).</li>\n<li><b>Duchenne/Becker</b> (dystrophin) — X-linked; Gowers sign; covered in histology/cell bio.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Integumentary system</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Epidermis = surface ectoderm; dermis = mesoderm (dermatome on back, lateral plate elsewhere); melanocytes = neural crest</span> (migrate by weeks 8–12). Langerhans cells = bone marrow; Merkel cells = neural crest.</li>\n<li>Epidermis starts as a single layer + <b>periderm</b> (shed into amniotic fluid → <b>vernix caseosa</b> with sebum); stratified by ~week 21.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hair follicles</span>: epidermal downgrowth into dermis; <b>lanugo</b> (fine fetal hair, shed before/after birth) replaced by vellus/terminal hair. Hair papilla = mesoderm.</li>\n<li>Glands (all from epidermis): sebaceous (from follicle wall), sweat glands, <b>mammary glands</b> (modified sweat glands along the milk line → supernumerary nipples = polythelia).</li>\n<li>Nails start ~week 10 at fingertips (reach tips ~32 weeks; toes later — useful for gestational age).</li>\n<li>Teeth: enamel = ectoderm (ameloblasts); dentin/pulp = neural crest (odontoblasts).</li>\n<li>Disorders: <span class=\"hy\">albinism</span> (normal melanocyte number, ↓ tyrosinase/melanin), vitiligo (↓ melanocytes, autoimmune), <b>ichthyosis</b> (abnormal keratinization; harlequin), <b>ectodermal dysplasia</b> (absent sweat glands, sparse hair, abnormal teeth), epidermolysis bullosa (anchoring proteins: keratin, collagen VII), hemangiomas.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Intramembranous: skull vault, face, clavicle. Endochondral: long bones, vertebrae, skull base.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Achondroplasia = FGFR3 (proliferative zone); cleidocranial dysplasia = RUNX2.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Nucleus pulposus from notochord; vertebrae from sclerotome by resegmentation.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Sagittal synostosis → scaphocephaly (long narrow head); anterior fontanelle closes by ~18–24 months.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">AER = FGF (length); ZPA = SHH (anterior–posterior, polydactyly); digits separate by apoptosis (syndactyly).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Upper limb rotates laterally, lower limb medially; limb buds week 4; thalidomide → phocomelia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Skeletal muscle = myotome (paraxial mesoderm); smooth = splanchnic mesoderm; melanocytes = neural crest.</li>\n</ul>", "crs-biochem": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Fatty acid oxidation (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<h3>Overview &amp; activation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>TAG → <b>lipolysis in adipose</b> → free fatty acids carried in plasma on <span class=\"hy\">albumin</span>.</li>\n<li>Three types: α, β, ω. <span class=\"hy\">β-oxidation is the major pathway</span>: sequential removal of <b>2-carbon units from the carboxyl end</b> as acetyl CoA.</li>\n<li>Site: <b>liver, skeletal muscle</b>. Subcellular: <b>mitochondria</b>.</li>\n<li>Activation in cytosol / on the <b>outer mitochondrial membrane</b> by <span class=\"hy\">acyl CoA synthetase (thiokinase)</span>: FFA + CoA + ATP → acyl CoA + AMP + PPi → 2Pi = <b>2 ATP equivalents</b>.</li>\n<li>Palmitic = 16C, stearic = 18C. <b>Long-chain</b> acyl CoA needs carnitine; <b>MCFA do not</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Carnitine shuttle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>CPT I</b> (outer membrane): acyl CoA → acyl-carnitine. <span class=\"hy\">Inhibited by malonyl CoA</span>.</li>\n<li><b>CACT translocase</b> (inner membrane): <span class=\"hy\">antiporter</span>, acyl-carnitine in / free carnitine out.</li>\n<li><b>CPT II</b> (inner membrane): acyl-carnitine → acyl CoA in the matrix.</li>\n<li>Carnitine sources: <b>meat</b> (exogenous); made from <span class=\"hy\">lysine + methionine in liver and kidney</span>.</li>\n<li>Both CPTs inhibited by <b>sulfonylureas</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Carnitine &amp; CPT deficiencies</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Key features</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Systemic carnitine deficiency</b></td><td>Liver, brain, muscle. ↓ FAO + ↓ gluconeogenesis → <span class=\"hy\">hypoketotic hypoglycemia</span> → encephalopathy. <b>No acylcarnitines in urine</b>. TG in muscle, hepatomegaly. Heart: progressive cardiomyopathy; skeletal: myopathy, myoglobinuria.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Primary</b></td><td>AR, <span class=\"hy\">SLC22A5 → OCTN2</span>. Infants/young children, worse with fasting. Hypotonia, hepatomegaly, encephalopathy, <b>dilated cardiomyopathy</b>. <b>↑ urine free carnitine, ↓ plasma/tissue</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Secondary</b></td><td>Not genetic, milder. ↑ demand (pregnancy, trauma, burns, infection); ↓ synthesis (liver/kidney disease); loss (<b>dialysis</b>); ↓ intake (malnutrition, strict vegetarians); <b>valproate</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CPT I deficiency</b></td><td>Mainly <b>liver</b>: ↓ FAO and ketogenesis.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CPT II deficiency</b></td><td>Liver, heart, skeletal muscle. <span class=\"hy\">Most common cause of recurrent myoglobinuria</span>. Teens/20s; pain + myoglobinuria after <b>prolonged exercise</b>; rhabdomyolysis; <b>normal strength between attacks</b>. Rx: avoid fasting, high carb, low fat with MCFA.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>β-Oxidation reactions</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Acyl CoA dehydrogenase</b> — FAD → FADH2 → trans-2-enoyl CoA</li>\n<li><b>Enoyl CoA hydratase</b> — + H2O → 3-hydroxyacyl CoA</li>\n<li><b>3-Hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase</b> — NAD+ → NADH → β-ketoacyl CoA</li>\n<li><b>Thiolase</b> — + CoASH → acetyl CoA + acyl CoA (2C shorter)</li>\n</ol>\n<ul><li>Chain length: <b>LCAD 12–18C</b>, <b>MCAD 14→4C</b>, <b>SCAD 4 and 6C</b>.</li>\n<li>Palmitoyl CoA: <span class=\"hy\">7 cycles → 8 acetyl CoA, 7 FADH2, 7 NADH</span>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Energetics &amp; regulation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>7 NADH × 3 = 21; 7 FADH2 × 2 = 14; 8 acetyl CoA × 12 = 96 → 131 − 2 = <span class=\"hy\">129 ATP</span> (stearic: 146).</li>\n<li>Complete degradation only for <b>saturated, even-carbon</b> fatty acids.</li>\n<li>Regulation: <b>malonyl CoA ⊣ CPT I</b> (fed state); <b>NADH ⊣ 3-hydroxyacyl CoA DH</b>; <b>acetyl CoA ⊣ thiolase</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Unsaturated &amp; odd-chain fatty acids</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Unsaturated: extra <b>isomerase</b> (odd-numbered double bonds) and <b>reductase + isomerase</b> (even-numbered).</li>\n<li>Odd-chain: last round gives <b>propionyl CoA + acetyl CoA</b>. Valine and isoleucine also feed propionyl CoA.</li>\n<li>Propionyl CoA → <b>carboxylase (biotin)</b> → D-methylmalonyl CoA → <b>racemase</b> → L-methylmalonyl CoA → <b>mutase (B12)</b> → <span class=\"hy\">succinyl CoA</span> → TCA.</li>\n<li><b>Methylmalonic aciduria</b>: B12 deficiency or mutase mutation; buildup of precursors, lack of products; <b>toxic encephalopathy</b> (lethargy, FTT, vomiting, acidosis, dehydration, respiratory distress, hypotonia, delay, seizures, hepatomegaly).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Peroxisomal, α- and ω-oxidation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>VLCFA</b> (&gt;20C): <b>peroxisomes</b>; first enzyme <span class=\"hy\">acyl CoA oxidase</span> (FADH2 + O2 → <b>H2O2</b>). Ends at <b>octanoyl CoA</b> → mitochondria. Up-regulated by fat-rich diets → low-grade inflammation.</li>\n<li><b>α-oxidation</b>: liver peroxisomes; <b>phytanic acid</b> (β-methyl branch; ruminants, dairy) → <span class=\"hy\">phytanoyl CoA hydroxylase</span> → decarboxylated → β-oxidation. Energy inefficient; clears branched FAs.</li>\n<li><b>ω-oxidation</b>: <b>ER</b>; methyl → carboxyl; minor, medium-chain; important when β-oxidation fails → <b>dicarboxylic acids</b> in urine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Disorders</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Key features</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>MCAD deficiency</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Most common FAO inborn error</span>; SIDS. Triggers: infection, overnight fasting; breastfed infants (MCFA). Hypoglycemia (adrenergic + neuroglycopenic), metabolic acidosis (N/V, abdominal pain), aspiration. Pathogenesis: ↑ MCFA → ↑ urine acylcarnitines + dicarboxylic acids (acidosis); ↓ ATP/acetyl CoA → ↓ gluconeogenesis (hypoglycemia), ↓ ketones, ↓ NAG → ↓ urea cycle → <b>hyperammonemia</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Jamaican vomiting sickness</b></td><td>Unripe <b>ackee → hypoglycin</b> inactivates medium- and short-chain acyl CoA DH. Dicarboxylic aciduria (ω-oxidation). Vomiting, abdominal pain, lethargy, profound hypoglycemia.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zellweger</b></td><td>AR, <b>PEX</b> (12 genes) → peroxisomal abiogenesis. High forehead, hypoplastic supraorbital ridges, epicanthal folds, midface hypoplasia, large fontanel; CNS hypomyelination; hepatomegaly, renal cysts. ↑ VLCFA + branched FAs; ↓ plasmalogens, PAF. <b>Cerebrohepatorenal</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>X-ALD</b></td><td>XR, <b>ABCD1 → ALDP</b> (peroxisomal VLCFA transporter). ↑ VLCFA. Brain demyelination; adrenal cortex (adrenal insufficiency); Leydig cells (sexual dysfunction).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adult Refsum</b></td><td>Neurocutaneous; AR, <b>PHYH / PEX7</b>; phytanoyl CoA hydroxylase deficiency (α-oxidation). ↑ phytanic acid → ↑ membrane fluidity → poor conduction. Weakness, polyneuropathy, cerebellar ataxia, ichthyosis, <b>retinitis pigmentosa, anosmia</b>. Infantile Refsum: PEX1/PEX2, Zellweger spectrum.</td></tr></table>\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Fatty acid &amp; triacylglycerol synthesis (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<p><b>NBME focus:</b> the citrate shuttle, acetyl-CoA carboxylase regulation, NADPH sources, palmitate stoichiometry, essential fatty acids, and fed vs fasted control of ACC and hormone-sensitive lipase.</p>\n<h3>Synthesis vs β-oxidation: the table they test</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>β-Oxidation</th><th>Synthesis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Site</td><td>Mitochondria</td><td><b>Cytoplasm</b> of liver, lactating mammary gland, adipose</td></tr>\n<tr><td>C2 unit</td><td>Removed as acetyl-CoA</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Added as malonyl CoA</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cofactor</td><td>Produces NADH, FADH2</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Uses NADPH</span> (vitamin B3, niacin)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Carrier</td><td>CoA</td><td>Acyl carrier protein of fatty acid synthase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enzymes</td><td>Separate enzymes</td><td>One multi-enzyme complex</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ATP</td><td>Small amount to activate</td><td>Large amount</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulation</td><td>Indirect: fatty acid availability (malonyl CoA blocks CPT I)</td><td>Direct: <b>acetyl-CoA carboxylase</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hormones</td><td>Glucagon, adrenaline ↑ · insulin ↓</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Insulin ↑</span> · glucagon, adrenaline ↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3-OH intermediate</td><td>L-isomer</td><td>D-isomer</td></tr></table>\n<h3>★ Citrate shuttle: getting acetyl-CoA to the cytosol</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Acetyl-CoA is made in mitochondria (PDH and fatty acid oxidation) but <b>cannot cross the inner membrane</b>. When it is high, it leaves as <span class=\"hy\">citrate</span>.</li>\n<li>Matrix: acetyl-CoA + OAA → <b>citrate synthase</b> → citrate.</li>\n<li>Cytosol: citrate + CoA + ATP → <span class=\"hy\">ATP-citrate lyase</span> → acetyl-CoA + OAA.</li>\n<li>OAA → <b>malate dehydrogenase</b>, NADH → malate → <span class=\"hy\">malic enzyme</span> → pyruvate + CO2 + <b>NADPH</b>.</li>\n<li>Pyruvate re-enters the matrix → <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>, biotin, ATP → OAA. Two ATP per acetyl moved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>NADPH: 14 per palmitate</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>8</b> from <b>malic enzyme</b>, one per acetyl-CoA moved · <b>6</b> from the <b>HMP pathway</b>.</li>\n<li>Exam twist: G6PD deficiency cuts the HMP share, not the malic enzyme share.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>★ Acetyl-CoA carboxylase: the committed, regulated step</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Acetyl-CoA + HCO3⁻ + ATP → <b>malonyl CoA</b>. Irreversible. <span class=\"hy\">Biotin</span> prosthetic group; carboxybiotin intermediate uses ATP.</li>\n<li>Biotin carboxylases to remember together: <b>ACC</b> and <b>pyruvate carboxylase</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Turns ACC ON</th><th>Turns ACC OFF</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Allosteric</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Citrate</span>: inactive dimer → active polymer</td><td>Long-chain fatty acyl CoA, AMP</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Covalent</td><td>Dephosphorylation by protein phosphatase</td><td>Phosphorylation by <span class=\"hy\">AMPK</span> (high AMP) or cAMP-dependent protein kinase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hormonal</td><td><b>Insulin</b> → protein phosphatase</td><td><b>Glucagon, epinephrine</b> → kinases</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transcriptional</td><td>High carbohydrate → <b>ChREBP</b> · insulin → <b>SREBP-1c</b> · PPARs</td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drug</td><td></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Metformin</span> activates AMPK → ACC off</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Rule:</b> dephosphorylated ACC = active (fed). Phosphorylated ACC = inactive (fasting, exercise, metformin).</p>\n<h3>Fatty acid synthase</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Eukaryotes: <b>one large polypeptide</b>, working as a head-to-tail <b>dimer</b>, in the <b>cytosol</b>; <span class=\"hy\">7 catalytic sites</span>.</li>\n<li>Domain 1: AT, MT, condensing enzyme (Cys–SH) · Domain 2: ACP, KR, DH, ER · Domain 3: <b>thioesterase</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>phosphopantetheine</b> arm of ACP swings the chain between sites.</li>\n<li>Each cycle: <span class=\"hy\">condensation → reduction (NADPH) → dehydration → reduction (NADPH)</span>. Round 1: acetoacetyl → D-3-hydroxybutyryl → crotonyl → butyryl ACP.</li>\n<li>Condensation releases CO2; malonyl decarboxylation drives it.</li>\n<li>7 rounds → C16. <span class=\"hy\">Thioesterase is the ruler</span>; it releases <b>palmitate 16:0</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"hy\"><b>Acetyl-CoA + 7 malonyl CoA + 14 NADPH + 20 H⁺ → palmitate + 7 CO2 + 14 NADP⁺ + 8 CoA + 6 H2O</b></p>\n<h3>Elongation, desaturation, essential fatty acids</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Elongation beyond C16: <b>cytosolic face of the ER</b>; malonyl CoA donates C2 to the <b>carboxyl end</b>.</li>\n<li>Desaturation in the ER: NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase + cytochrome b5 + desaturase (O2, NADH). 4 desaturases, <b>non-heme iron</b>. <b>No ATP</b> is made: not linked to ox-phos.</li>\n<li>Humans desaturate only at <b>C4, C5, C6, C9</b> → can make oleate 18:1 Δ9 but <span class=\"hy\">not linoleate 18:2 Δ9,12 or linolenate 18:3 Δ9,12,15</span>. These are essential; deficiency → scaly dermatitis on fat-free TPN.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Triacylglycerol synthesis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Glucose → <b>DHAP</b> → glycerol-3-P dehydrogenase, NADH → <b>glycerol-3-P</b>. Adipose relies on this; it has little glycerol kinase.</li>\n<li>Acyltransferase ×2 → <b>phosphatidic acid</b> → phosphatidic acid phosphatase → <b>1,2-DAG</b> → acyltransferase → <b>TAG</b>.</li>\n<li>Esterified carboxyls → no charge → <b>\"neutral fat\"</b>.</li>\n<li>Carbohydrate-rich diet → insulin + ChREBP/SREBP-1c → ↑ ACC → ↑ fatty acids → ↑ TAG, sent out of the liver as <b>VLDL</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Fate of TAG and lipolysis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Adipose: stored as <b>depot fat</b>. Liver: exported in <span class=\"hy\">VLDL</span> to muscle or adipose.</li>\n<li>Lipolysis: <b>adipose triglyceride lipase</b>, TAG → DAG (perilipins on the droplet) → <span class=\"hy\">hormone-sensitive lipase</span>, DAG → MAG.</li>\n<li>HSL is <b>active when phosphorylated</b>: glucagon, epinephrine ↑; insulin dephosphorylates → ↓.</li>\n<li>Glycerol → <b>liver</b> → phosphorylated. Fatty acids → <b>albumin</b> → tissues → activated → oxidized in mitochondria.</li>\n</ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>State</th><th>ACC</th><th>HSL</th><th>Net</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fed, insulin</td><td>Dephosphorylated, ON</td><td>Dephosphorylated, OFF</td><td>Make and store fat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fasting, glucagon/epinephrine</td><td>Phosphorylated, OFF</td><td>Phosphorylated, ON</td><td>Release and burn fat</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Same signal, opposite effect:</b> phosphorylation turns ACC <i>off</i> but HSL <i>on</i>. This is a classic NBME trap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Ketone body metabolism (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<ul><li><b>Where:</b> <span class=\"hy\">liver mitochondria only</span>, from acetyl-CoA made by hepatic β-oxidation when acetyl-CoA <b>exceeds the liver's oxidative capacity</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Why (fasting):</b> the brain cannot use fatty acids · ↑acetyl-CoA inhibits PDH and activates pyruvate carboxylase → oxaloacetate used for gluconeogenesis → less citrate · NADH/ATP from β-oxidation inhibit isocitrate dehydrogenase.</li>\n<li><b>Fed vs fasting:</b> fed → acetyl-CoA in the cytosol → fatty acid and cholesterol synthesis. Fasting → acetyl-CoA in mitochondria → β-oxidation and ketogenesis.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ketogenesis</h3>\n<p>2 Acetyl-CoA →(<b>thiolase</b>)→ acetoacetyl-CoA →(<span class=\"hy\">HMG-CoA synthase, rate-limiting</span>)→ HMG-CoA →(<b>HMG-CoA lyase</b>)→ acetoacetate → β-hydroxybutyrate (dehydrogenase, NADH) or → acetone (spontaneous decarboxylation)</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Ketone body</th><th>Key facts</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Acetoacetate</td><td>Gives 2 acetyl-CoA in tissues · detected by urine dipstick</td></tr>\n<tr><td>β-Hydroxybutyrate</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Predominant ketone in blood and urine in ketosis</span> · stores an NADH equivalent · ratio to acetoacetate set by NADH/NAD⁺ · not seen by dipstick</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Acetone</td><td>Non-metabolizable, volatile · <b>fruity breath</b></td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Low pKa → water-soluble → <b>no carrier needed</b> (unlike fatty acids on albumin).</li></ul>\n<h3>Ketolysis (extrahepatic)</h3>\n<p>β-Hydroxybutyrate → acetoacetate (NAD⁺ → NADH) →(<span class=\"hy\">thiophorase: succinyl-CoA → succinate; absent in liver</span>)→ acetoacetyl-CoA →(thiolase)→ 2 acetyl-CoA → TCA</p>\n<ul><li>Users: muscle, heart, kidney, brain (prolonged starvation). <b>Not</b> the liver (no thiophorase) or RBCs (no mitochondria).</li>\n<li>Brain: ~25% of energy from ketones at 3 days · ~50% at 7 days · ~2/3 at 2–3 weeks → <b>spares muscle protein</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Ketosis</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Insulin inhibits</span>, <span class=\"hy\">glucagon stimulates</span> ketogenesis.</li>\n<li>Normal &lt; 0.1 mM · physiological ketosis up to 2–3 mM · DKA up to 20–25 mM.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Hypoglycemia + hypoketosis</th><th>Hypoglycemia + hyperketosis</th><th>Hyperglycemia + hyperketosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Carnitine deficiency · MCAD · CPT-I · carnitine translocase</td><td>Von Gierke · hereditary fructose intolerance · galactosemia · alcoholism · pyruvate carboxylase deficiency</td><td>Type 1 diabetes (DKA)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Diabetic ketoacidosis</h3>\n<ul><li>↓Insulin:glucagon → ↑lipolysis and β-oxidation → acetyl-CoA exceeds the liver's capacity → ↑ketones. Mainly type 1 diabetes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Glucose &gt; 300 mg/dL · pH &lt; 7.2 high anion gap acidosis · HCO₃⁻ &lt; 15 mEq/L</span> · glucosuria and ketonuria → dehydration.</li>\n<li>Nausea and vomiting · abdominal pain (acute pancreatitis from hypertriglyceridemia) · Kussmaul breathing · fruity breath · hypotension, coma.</li>\n<li>Management: IV fluids, IV regular insulin, and potassium monitoring and replacement (insulin shifts K⁺ into cells).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Complex lipid metabolism (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<h3>Phospholipids</h3>\n<ul><li>FA + glycerol + phosphate → <b>glycerophospholipid</b> · FA + sphingosine + phosphate → <b>sphingophospholipid</b>. Phosphatidate is the key intermediate.</li>\n<li>Made by all cells except mature RBCs: <b>SER → Golgi → plasma membrane</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Phospholipids you must know</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Lipid</th><th>NBME fact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dipalmitoyllecithin (DPPC)</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Lung surfactant</span> → ↓surface tension, prevents atelectasis. Deficiency → <span class=\"hy\">neonatal RDS in preterm infants</span>. Fetal maturity: <span class=\"hy\">L/S ratio &gt; 2</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phosphatidylinositol / PIP2</td><td>Stearate C1, <span class=\"hy\">arachidonate C2 (reservoir)</span> · <span class=\"hy\">phospholipase C → IP3 + DAG</span> second messengers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phosphatidylserine</td><td>Flips outward in apoptosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cardiolipin</td><td>Inner mitochondrial membrane · ↓proton permeability → proton-motive force</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plasmalogens</td><td>Ether phospholipids from DHAP · ethanolamine type in myelin, choline type in heart</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Platelet-activating factor</td><td>Alkyl ether + acetyl at C2 · <span class=\"hy\">anaphylaxis</span>, platelet aggregation, serotonin release</td></tr></table>\n<h3>GPI anchors &amp; PNH</h3>\n<ul><li>Proteins tethered by a carbohydrate bridge to PI; released by phospholipase C. Examples: lipoprotein lipase, RBC acetylcholinesterase, placental alkaline phosphatase, <b>decay-accelerating factor</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PNH: acquired PIGA mutation in hematopoietic cells → no GPI anchor → CD55/CD59 lost → complement lysis</span> → hemolytic anemia, red morning urine, <b>venous thrombosis</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Phospholipases</h3>\n<ul><li><b>A1</b> cuts C1 · <span class=\"hy\">A2 cuts C2 → arachidonic acid</span> for prostaglandins and leukotrienes (snake venom, pancreas) · <b>C</b> removes the phosphate head group (PIP2 → IP3 + DAG) · <b>D</b> removes the head group base.</li>\n<li>Products are lysophospholipids (lysolecithin lyses membranes).</li></ul>\n<h3>Sphingolipids</h3>\n<ul><li>Sphingosine + FA at C2 = <b>ceramide</b>. Ceramide + phosphocholine (sphingomyelin synthase) = <b>sphingomyelin</b> (myelin).</li>\n<li>Ceramide + sugars = glycolipids: cerebrosides (galacto in neurons, gluco minor), sulfatides (+ sulfate from PAPS), globosides, gangliosides (+ sialic acid/NANA; G-M/D/T/Q-number naming).</li>\n<li>Glycolipids: blood group antigens · <span class=\"hy\">receptors for cholera and tetanus toxins</span>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Sphingolipidoses (all autosomal recessive except Fabry)</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Disease</th><th>Enzyme</th><th>Accumulates</th><th>Classic clue</th></tr>\n<tr><td><span class=\"hy\">Tay-Sachs</span></td><td>Hexosaminidase A (α-subunit, chr 15)</td><td>GM2</td><td>Cherry-red spot, <b>no</b> hepatosplenomegaly, Ashkenazi</td></tr>\n<tr><td><span class=\"hy\">Niemann-Pick</span></td><td>Sphingomyelinase</td><td>Sphingomyelin</td><td>Cherry-red spot <b>plus</b> hepatosplenomegaly, foam cells, zebra bodies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><span class=\"hy\">Gaucher</span> (most common)</td><td>Glucocerebrosidase</td><td>Glucocerebroside</td><td>Crumpled-tissue-paper macrophages, HSM, bone disease · enzyme replacement</td></tr>\n<tr><td><span class=\"hy\">Fabry</span> (X-linked)</td><td>α-Galactosidase A</td><td>Globotriaosylceramide</td><td>Burning extremity pain, angiokeratomas, renal and cardiac failure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Krabbe</td><td>Galactocerebrosidase</td><td>Galactocerebroside, psychosine</td><td>Globoid cells, CNS demyelination</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Metachromatic leukodystrophy</td><td>Arylsulfatase A</td><td>Sulfatide</td><td>CNS and PNS demyelination, ataxia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sandhoff</td><td>Hexosaminidase A and B (β-subunit)</td><td>GM2, globoside</td><td>Like Tay-Sachs, faster</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GM1 gangliosidosis</td><td>β-Galactosidase</td><td>GM1</td><td>Organomegaly, skeletal involvement</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Farber</td><td>Ceramidase</td><td>Ceramide</td><td>Painful deformed joints, skin nodules</td></tr></table>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Eicosanoid metabolism (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<h3>Classes</h3>\n<ul><li>Eicosanoids = <b>prostaglandins (PGs), thromboxanes (TXs), leukotrienes (LTs)</b>. <span class=\"hy\">PGs + TXs = prostanoids.</span> Named for prostate, platelets (thrombocytes) and leukocytes.</li>\n<li><b>Local hormones</b>: act near where they are made, rapidly degraded. Act via <b>GPCRs</b> → ↑ or ↓ cAMP, or PI pathway → ↑ Ca²⁺.</li>\n<li>Effects: inflammation (joints, skin, eyes), pain &amp; fever, reproduction (labor), <b>↓ gastric acid</b>, BP (vasodilation / constriction), platelet aggregation.</li>\n<li>PGs have a <b>cyclopentane ring</b>; TXs a <b>6-membered ring</b>. Letter = ring substituents; <span class=\"hy\">subscript = number of C=C</span> (2 for prostanoids, 4 for leukotrienes).</li>\n<li>All cells make eicosanoids <span class=\"hy\">except erythrocytes</span>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Arachidonic acid release</h3>\n<ul><li>Principal eicosanoids come from <b>arachidonic acid</b>, at <b>C-2</b> of membrane phospholipids, released by <span class=\"hy\">phospholipase A2</span> (activated by epinephrine, thrombin, bradykinin).</li>\n<li>Immediate dietary precursor = <b>linoleic acid</b>. Minor eicosanoids come from EPA (from α-linolenic acid).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Corticosteroids inhibit PLA2</span> (via lipocortin) → ↓ prostaglandins AND leukotrienes.</li></ul>\n<h3>Cyclooxygenase (cyclic) pathway</h3>\n<ul><li><b>PGH₂ synthase</b> (prostaglandin endoperoxide synthase): heme dioxygenase on the ER, two activities: <b>cyclooxygenase</b> (+2 O₂: arachidonate → PGG₂) and <b>peroxidase</b> (2 GSH: PGG₂ → PGH₂).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">PGH₂ = branch point</span> → PGI₂ (prostacyclin synthase, endothelium), PGE₂, PGD₂, PGF₂α, TXA₂ (thromboxane synthase, platelets) → stable TXB₂.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>COX-1 (PGS-1)</th><th>COX-2 (PGS-2)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Expression</td><td>Constitutive</td><td>Inducible</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Gastric mucosa, kidney, platelets, endothelium</td><td>Macrophages, monocytes in inflammation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trigger</td><td>Always on</td><td><b>PAF and IL-1</b></td></tr></table>\n<h3>Drugs</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Target / mechanism</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Aspirin</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Acetylates a serine</span> near the COX active site → <b>irreversible</b>, COX-1 &amp; 2. Platelets have no nucleus → no new enzyme → effect lasts the platelet lifespan; daily low dose = anticlotting. Stop ~1 week before surgery.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ibuprofen, indomethacin, naproxen</td><td>Block the <b>hydrophobic channel</b> arachidonate uses to enter COX → reversible, COX-1 &amp; 2</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Celecoxib (and valdecoxib, lumiracoxib, rofecoxib)</td><td><b>Selective COX-2</b> → fewer ulcers; ↓ PGI₂ with TXA₂ spared → thrombotic risk</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Corticosteroids</td><td>Inhibit <b>PLA2</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Zileuton</td><td>Inhibits <b>5-lipoxygenase</b> (hepatotoxicity → LFTs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Montelukast, zafirlukast</td><td>Block <b>leukotriene receptors</b> on airway smooth muscle</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">COX-1 inhibition in the gut → NSAID ulcers</span>.</li>\n<li>Blocking COX shunts arachidonate to 5-LOX → ↑ leukotrienes → aspirin/NSAID-exacerbated asthma.</li></ul>\n<h3>Lipoxygenase (linear) pathway</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">5-Lipoxygenase: arachidonate → 5-HPETE → LTA₄</span> → LTB₄, or + glutathione → LTC₄ → (−glutamate) LTD₄ → (−glycine) LTE₄ → (+glutamate) LTF₄.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Peptidoleukotrienes = LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄, LTF₄</span> (contain amino acids). <b>LTC₄, LTD₄, LTE₄ = slow-reacting substance of anaphylaxis</b> → bronchoconstriction in asthma. LTB₄ is not a peptidoleukotriene.</li></ul><h2 id=\"g6\">6. Lipoprotein metabolism (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<ul><li>Structure: <b>core</b> = TAG + cholesteryl esters; <b>shell</b> = phospholipid, free cholesterol, apolipoproteins.</li>\n<li>Electrophoresis: <span class=\"hy\">HDL = alpha, VLDL = pre-beta, LDL = beta, chylomicrons stay at the origin</span>. Ultracentrifugation: chylomicrons float, HDL is densest.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Lipoprotein</th><th>Apoproteins</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Chylomicron</td><td>B-48, C-II, E</td><td>Dietary TAG from intestine to tissues</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VLDL</td><td>B-100, C-II, E</td><td>Liver-made TAG (~60%) to tissues</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IDL</td><td>B-100, E</td><td>VLDL partly stripped of TAG, precursor of LDL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LDL</td><td>B-100</td><td>Cholesterol to liver and extrahepatic tissues</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HDL</td><td><b>A-I</b> (main), C-II, E</td><td>Reverse cholesterol transport</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Chylomicrons and lipoprotein lipase</h3>\n<ul><li>Assembly needs <b>MTP</b>; nascent chylomicron (B-48) leaves via lymph and becomes mature after <span class=\"hy\">HDL gives it apo C-II and apo E</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">LPL: extracellular, anchored by heparan sulfate on capillaries, activated by apo C-II</span>, made more under <b>insulin</b>; mainly adipose, heart, skeletal muscle. TAG → free fatty acids + glycerol.</li>\n<li>Remnant returns C-II to HDL, keeps E; <b>apo E receptor on the liver</b> takes it up.</li></ul>\n<h3>VLDL → IDL → LDL</h3>\n<ul><li>LPL turns VLDL into IDL; <b>hepatic lipase</b> turns IDL into LDL (more cholesterol, less TAG). Apo C and E go back to HDL.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">CETP</span>: HDL gives cholesteryl esters to VLDL in exchange for TAG.</li>\n<li>LDL (apo B-100) binds the LDL receptor → <b>clathrin-coated pits</b> → lysosome → free cholesterol. Lysosomal storage: Wolman, Niemann-Pick C.</li>\n<li>High cell cholesterol inhibits <b>SREBP</b> → fewer LDL receptors. <b>ACAT</b> stores excess as cholesteryl esters (acyl-CoA donor).</li></ul>\n<h3>Atherosclerosis</h3>\n<ul><li>LDL trapped in intima → <span class=\"hy\">oxidized LDL</span> → monocytes become macrophages → <b>scavenger receptors</b> (not downregulated) → <b>foam cells</b> → <b>fatty streak</b> (earliest lesion) → cytokines → smooth muscle migration, collagen, calcium → thin fibrous cap ruptures → platelets and <b>thrombus</b> → MI or stroke.</li></ul>\n<h3>HDL</h3>\n<ul><li>Nascent discoidal pre-β HDL (liver, intestine) gets cholesterol via <span class=\"hy\">ABCA1</span> (raised by LXR and PPAR) → <b>LCAT</b> (lecithin donor, activated by apo A-I) esterifies it → spherical HDL → <b>SR-B1</b> delivers esters to liver → bile.</li>\n<li>Exercise raises HDL; smoking and obesity lower it; diabetes and metabolic syndrome make it dysfunctional.</li></ul>\n<h3>Disorders</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Disorder</th><th>Defect</th><th>Key findings</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Type I</td><td>LPL or apo C-II</td><td>↑ Chylomicrons, creamy plasma, pancreatitis, eruptive xanthomas, hepatosplenomegaly, <b>no ↑ atherosclerosis</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Type IIa (FH)</td><td>LDL receptor or apo B-100, AD</td><td>↑ LDL, early CAD, <b>tendon xanthomas</b>, xanthelasma</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Type III</td><td>Apo E</td><td>↑ Remnants, ↑ cholesterol and TAG, <b>palmar and tuberous xanthomas</b>, early atherosclerosis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Type IV</td><td>Liver VLDL overproduction</td><td>↑ TAG, pancreatitis if &gt;1,000 mg/dL, eruptive xanthomas, metabolic syndrome</td></tr>\n<tr><td>LCAT deficiency</td><td>LCAT</td><td>↑ Free cholesterol, <b>lipoprotein X</b>, corneal opacity, kidney disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fish-eye disease</td><td>Partial LCAT</td><td>Corneal opacity, low HDL, no kidney disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tangier</td><td>ABCA1</td><td>Near-zero HDL, orange tonsils, neuropathy, hepatosplenomegaly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Abetalipoproteinemia</td><td>MTTP</td><td>No apo B-48/100, steatorrhea, acanthocytes, ataxia, retinal degeneration, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Cholesterol biosynthesis (Dr. Shetty)</h2>\n<ul><li>Cholesterol = 27-carbon sterol: <b>4 fused rings</b> (steroid nucleus), <span class=\"hy\">3-OH</span>, <b>C5–C6 double bond</b>, 8-carbon side chain at C-17. Esterified at the 3-OH → cholesteryl ester (more hydrophobic).</li>\n<li>Uses: membranes (myelin), <b>bile acids</b>, <b>steroid hormones</b>, vitamin D. Plant sterols (β-sitosterol) are pumped back into the gut lumen with cholesterol → lower serum cholesterol.</li>\n<li>Made in nearly all tissues (liver, intestine, adrenal cortex, gonads) in the <span class=\"hy\">cytoplasm + ER</span>. All 27 carbons from <b>acetyl-CoA</b> (carried out of mitochondria as citrate → citrate lyase); <b>NADPH</b> supplies reducing power.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pathway</h3>\n<p>Acetyl-CoA + acetoacetyl-CoA → <b>HMG-CoA</b> (HMG-CoA synthase) → <span class=\"hy\">mevalonate (HMG-CoA reductase, 2 NADPH, rate-limiting)</span> → isopentenyl-PP (ATP, CO₂ lost) → geranyl-PP → <b>farnesyl-PP</b> → squalene → lanosterol → 7-dehydrocholesterol → cholesterol.</p>\n<ul><li>Farnesyl-PP branches: <b>ubiquinone (CoQ), dolichol, heme a, prenylated proteins</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Smith-Lemli-Opitz</span>: 7-dehydrocholesterol 7-reductase deficiency → low cholesterol, high 7-DHC, microcephaly, 2–3 toe syndactyly, cleft palate, polydactyly.</li></ul>\n<h3>Regulation of HMG-CoA reductase</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>What happens</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sterol and SREBP</b></td><td>High sterol: SREBP–SCAP held in ER by <b>Insig</b>. Low sterol: SREBP–SCAP → Golgi → cleaved → nucleus → binds <b>SRE</b> → more reductase (and LDL receptor) made</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sterol-accelerated degradation</b></td><td>High sterol → reductase binds Insig → ubiquitinated and degraded</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Phosphorylation</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">AMPK (high AMP) phosphorylates → inactive</span>; phosphoprotein phosphatase reactivates</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Hormones</b></td><td><b>Insulin</b> → dephosphorylated (active) and gene up. <b>Glucagon</b> → phosphorylated and gene down</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Drugs</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Statins = competitive inhibitors</span> → low cell sterol → SREBP → more LDL receptors → ↓ plasma LDL</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Hemoglobin &amp; myoglobin (Dr. Shetty) · respiratory system</h2>\n<ul><li><b>Heme</b> = protoporphyrin IX (4 pyrrole rings, methenyl bridges) + <span class=\"hy\">Fe²⁺</span> held by 4 N bonds. Fe³⁺ = methemoglobin (no O₂ binding). In Hb, Mb, cytochromes, catalase, tryptophan pyrrolase.</li>\n<li><b>Myoglobin</b>: heart and skeletal muscle; 1 chain, 153 aa, ~80% α-helix (8 helices A–H); polar outside, nonpolar inside. <span class=\"hy\">Proximal His F8 (His-93) binds Fe</span>; distal His E7 (His-64) stabilizes bound O₂. O₂ reservoir. Hyperbolic curve, <b>P₅₀ ≈ 1 mm Hg</b>.</li>\n<li><b>HbA</b> = α₂β₂ (α 141 aa, β 146 aa), two αβ dimers. <span class=\"hy\">T (taut) = deoxy, dimers tight, low affinity, unloads O₂</span>; R (relaxed) = oxy, high affinity. Sigmoid curve, <b>P₅₀ ≈ 26 mm Hg</b>; positive cooperativity (heme-heme interaction): affinity for the last O₂ ≈ 300× the first.</li></ul>\n<h3>Curve shifts and binding sites</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Right shift (↓ affinity, more unloading): ↑ CO₂, ↑ H⁺ (↓ pH), ↑ 2,3-BPG, ↑ temperature, ↓ pO₂</span>. Left shift: the opposite, plus HbF and CO.</li>\n<li><b>Bohr effect</b>: CO₂ + H₂O → H₂CO₃ → HCO₃⁻ + H⁺; H⁺ protonates N-terminal amino groups and histidines → ionic bonds stabilize T → O₂ released in tissues.</li>\n<li>Binding sites: O₂ and CO → Fe²⁺. <span class=\"hy\">2,3-BPG → crevice between the β chains of T-form Hb, lined with positive residues</span>. CO₂ → amino-terminal ends (carbamino). H⁺ → His-146 of β.</li></ul>\n<h3>2,3-BPG and HbF</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Chronic hypoxia or anemia (COPD, high altitude) → ↑ 2,3-BPG → right shift</span>.</li>\n<li><b>HbF (α₂γ₂)</b> binds 2,3-BPG weakly (γ has fewer positive residues) → higher O₂ affinity → takes O₂ across the placenta.</li>\n<li>Stored blood (acid-citrate-dextrose) loses 2,3-BPG → Hb becomes an O₂ trap; recovers in 4–24 h. <span class=\"hy\">Inosine</span> in storage medium → ribose → HMP pathway → 2,3-BPG.</li></ul>\n<h3>CO, cyanide, methemoglobin</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Toxin</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Key points</th></tr>\n<tr><td>CO</td><td>Binds Fe²⁺ 200–250× O₂; stabilizes R → left shift; inhibits complex IV</td><td>Fires, heaters, car exhaust, methylene chloride; CNS and heart toxicity</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cyanide</td><td>Inhibits cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV)</td><td>Bitter almonds, lactic acidosis, cherry-red skin, coma. <span class=\"hy\">Amyl/sodium nitrite → MetHb traps CN⁻ (cyanmet-Hb) → sodium thiosulfate → thiocyanate (urine) → methylene blue</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Methemoglobin</td><td>Fe³⁺ heme (normally 1–2%); reduced by NADH-cytochrome b₅ (methemoglobin) reductase</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Chocolate-brown blood, cyanosis with normal pO₂</span>; nitrates, oxidants, congenital reductase or globin defects, G6PD; treat with methylene blue</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Globin genes and hemoglobin types</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">α-family (2 α + ζ) on chromosome 16; β-family (ε, Gγ, Aγ, δ, β) on chromosome 11</span>.</li>\n<li>Embryonic: Gower 1 (ζ₂ε₂), Gower 2 (α₂ε₂), Portland (ζ₂γ₂). Site: yolk sac → liver → bone marrow.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>Chains</th><th>Adult fraction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>HbA</td><td>α₂β₂</td><td>~90% (from 8th month of gestation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HbF</td><td>α₂γ₂</td><td>&lt;2% (dominant at birth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HbA₂</td><td>α₂δ₂</td><td>2–5% (from ~12 weeks after birth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HbA₁c</td><td>α₂β₂-glucose</td><td>3–9%</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Hemoglobinopathies</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">HbS: β6 Glu → Val</span> (AR). Deoxy-HbS valine binds hydrophobic patch (Ala 70, Phe 85, Leu 88) → polymers → sickling, Ca²⁺ influx, K⁺/water loss (Gardos channel), hemolysis, free Hb scavenges NO → vaso-occlusion. RBC life 10–20 days (trait 90, normal 120).</li>\n<li>Symptoms start at <b>5–6 months</b> as HbF falls: dactylitis, jaundice, acute chest syndrome, pain crises, infections (autosplenectomy). Triggers: low pO₂, ↑ pCO₂, ↓ pH, ↑ 2,3-BPG, fever, dehydration.</li>\n<li>Diagnosis: metabisulfite sickling test, electrophoresis, HPLC. Treatment: hydration, analgesia, antibiotics, <span class=\"hy\">hydroxyurea (↑ HbF)</span>, transfusion after stroke, penicillin prophylaxis; marrow transplant is the only cure.</li>\n<li><b>Sickle trait (HbAS)</b>: ~40% HbS, usually silent; protects against <i>P. falciparum</i> (infected cells sickle and are cleared).</li>\n<li><b>HbC</b>: β6 Glu → Lys; target cells, HbC crystals, mild anemia, no crises; moves slowest toward the anode. <b>HbSC</b>: compound heterozygote, Hb near normal, infarctive crisis after childbirth or surgery. <b>HbE</b>: β26 Glu → Lys, Southeast Asia, benign, mild β-thal phenotype; HbE/β-thal is a common severe form.</li></ul>\n<h3>Thalassemias</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>α-thalassemia</th><th>β-thalassemia</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Usually gene <b>deletions</b></td><td>Usually <b>point mutations</b> (β⁰ none, β⁺ reduced)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spectrum</td><td>1 gene silent carrier · 2 trait (mild microcytic) · <b>3 HbH (β₄)</b> · <span class=\"hy\">4 Hb Bart's (γ₄) → hydrops fetalis</span></td><td>Minor (trait, asymptomatic) · intermedia · <b>major (Cooley's, β⁰/β⁰)</b>: transfusion-dependent within 2 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Markers</td><td>HbH and Hb Bart's (no heme-heme interaction, very high O₂ affinity)</td><td><span class=\"hy\">↑ HbA₂ and ↑ HbF</span></td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>β-thal major: unpaired α chains precipitate → kill erythroblasts (ineffective erythropoiesis) + splenic hemolysis → ↑ EPO → marrow expansion (<b>chipmunk facies, frontal bossing, hair-on-end skull</b>) and extramedullary erythropoiesis (hepatosplenomegaly), growth failure, iron overload.</li></ul>\n", "crs-physiology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Cardiac action potential: ventricular myocytes (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>The big picture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>The heart's <b>electrical activity dictates its mechanical function</b>, the pump.</li>\n<li>Job 1, <b>generate a rhythm</b>: pacemaker cells create the impulse. Job 2, <b>coordinate contraction</b>: the conduction system makes the <span class=\"hy\">atria contract before the ventricles</span>.</li>\n<li>Arrhythmias are diseases of this system. <b>Antiarrhythmic drugs target specific Na⁺, Ca²⁺ and K⁺ channels.</b></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Two cardiac cell archetypes</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Contractile myocytes · \"workers\"</th><th>Pacemaker / conducting cells · \"managers\"</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Location</b></td><td>Atrial and ventricular myocardium</td><td>SA node, AV node, bundle of His, Purkinje fibers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Function</b></td><td>Force production, mechanical work</td><td>Automaticity and rapid impulse conduction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>AP type</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Fast response</span></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Slow response</span> in the SA and AV nodes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Key feature</b></td><td>Gap junctions in <b>intercalated discs</b> → <b>functional syncytium</b>, contracts as a single unit</td><td>Can <b>spontaneously depolarize</b></td></tr></table>\n<h3>Conduction pathway</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>SA node</b>: right atrial wall, inferior to the opening of the SVC → <b>internodal pathways</b> → <b>AV node</b>: interatrial septum, anterior to the opening of the coronary sinus.</li>\n<li>→ <b>AV bundle of His</b> in the interventricular septum → <b>left and right bundle branches</b> → <b>Purkinje fibers</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">The AV bundle is the ONLY muscular connection between atria and ventricles</span>; the cardiac fibrous skeleton insulates the rest.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Fast vs slow response</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fast · ventricular myocardium</th><th>Slow · SA / AV node</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Resting potential</td><td>Stable, ≈ −90 mV</td><td>Unstable, no true rest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 0</td><td>Fast Na⁺ influx, voltage-gated Na⁺ channels</td><td>Slow Ca²⁺ influx, L-type Ca²⁺ channels</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plateau</td><td>Present</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Automaticity</td><td>Absent, needs an external stimulus</td><td>Present, driven by Phase 4</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Memory hook:</b> Fast = Na⁺ upstroke · Slow = Ca²⁺ upstroke · only the slow ones fire on their own.</p>\n<h3>The ventricular AP, phase by phase</h3>\n<p>Occurs in <b>all cardiac myocytes except the SA and AV nodes</b>. Rest about −85 to −90 mV; peak overshoot about +30 mV.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Current</th><th>What happens</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>0</b> · upstroke</td><td>I<sub>Na</sub></td><td>A neighbour depolarizes the cell past <span class=\"hy\">threshold ≈ −65 mV</span> → fast voltage-gated Na⁺ channels open → Na⁺ rushes in → TMP just above 0 mV. <b>Steep upstroke = fast conduction</b>; nodal cells lack it, so they conduct slowly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1</b> · notch</td><td>I<sub>TO</sub> / transient K⁺</td><td>Na⁺ channels <b>inactivate</b>; transient K⁺ channels open → brief partial repolarization toward ~0 mV.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2</b> · plateau</td><td>I<sub>Ca</sub> ≈ I<sub>K</sub></td><td><span class=\"hy\">150–300 ms</span> near 0 mV. L-type Ca²⁺ influx balances delayed-rectifier K⁺ efflux. Ca²⁺ in → <b>Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release</b> from the SR → contraction.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3</b> · repolarization</td><td>I<sub>K</sub></td><td>Ca²⁺ channels inactivate; massive K⁺ efflux through voltage-gated <b>slow (delayed-rectifier) K⁺ channels</b>. K⁺ out &gt; Ca²⁺ in → net outward → back to −90 mV. <b>Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger and ATPases</b> restore gradients.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4</b> · rest</td><td>I<sub>K1</sub></td><td>Constant K⁺ efflux through <b>inward-rectifier</b> channels holds −90 mV; high K⁺ permeability; Na⁺ and Ca²⁺ channels closed. <span class=\"hy\">No spontaneous Phase 4 depolarization</span> → waits for an external stimulus.</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Phase 2 is the contraction trigger</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Phase 2 Ca²⁺ influx links directly to SR Ca²⁺ release: <b>excitation–contraction coupling</b>. It is not just an electrical plateau.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Boards: cardiac myocytes REQUIRE extracellular Ca²⁺ to release SR Ca²⁺ — skeletal muscle does not.</span> A Ca²⁺-free bath stops cardiac contraction, but APs still fire.</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ channel blockers, such as verapamil: less Ca²⁺ influx → weaker contraction; also slow SA and AV nodal cells, whose upstroke is Ca²⁺ carried; vasodilation in blood vessels.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Na⁺ channel gates and states</h3>\n<p><b>m = activation gate · h = inactivation gate.</b> Handwritten lecture note: \"H-gates DO NOT respond to voltage\" — they close on their own shortly after the channel opens, and reopen only as the cell repolarizes.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Phase</th><th>State</th><th>Gates</th></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Resting / available: closed but able to open</td><td>m closed · h open</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>Open / activated: rapid Na⁺ influx</td><td>m open · h open</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1–2</td><td>Inactivated: closed and unavailable, regardless of stimulus strength</td><td>m open · h closed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Late 3</td><td>Recovering back to resting</td><td>h gate reopening</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">Excitability depends on Na⁺ channel state, not just on membrane voltage.</span> Lidocaine, a local anesthetic antiarrhythmic, blocks I<sub>Na</sub>.</p>\n<h3>Refractory periods</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Period</th><th>When</th><th>Why / what fires</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Absolute (effective)</b></td><td>Phases 0, 1, 2 and the first half of 3, to about −50 mV</td><td>Fast Na⁺ channels inactivated → <b>no new AP, whatever the stimulus</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Relative</b></td><td>Second half of Phase 3, about −50 to −70 mV</td><td>Some channels recovered → needs a <b>stronger</b> stimulus; AP has <b>lower amplitude</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Supranormal</b></td><td>Very short, at the end of repolarization</td><td>Heightened excitability: a weaker stimulus can fire</td></tr></table>\n<p><b>Objective check:</b> stimulus during the plateau → no AP. Strong stimulus in late Phase 3 → a smaller AP.</p>\n<h3>Why the AP and refractory period are so long</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuron ≈ 1 ms, skeletal muscle ≈ 10 ms, <b>cardiac ≈ 300 ms</b>: <span class=\"hy\">30–300× longer</span>. The single phase responsible is the <b>Phase 2 plateau</b>.</li>\n<li>Long plateau + long refractory period → no re-excitation mid-contraction → <span class=\"hy\">no tetanic contraction</span>; heart tetany would be fatal.</li>\n<li><b>Diastole:</b> guaranteed relaxation → time for ventricular filling. <b>Systole:</b> contraction runs to completion → full ejection before the next impulse.</li>\n<li><b>Flip side:</b> <span class=\"hy\">heterogeneous refractory periods → re-entrant arrhythmias</span> when an impulse arrives at the wrong time.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ionic currents at a glance</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Current</th><th>Turned on</th><th>Turned off</th></tr>\n<tr><td>I<sub>Na</sub></td><td>Phase 0, a brief large inward spike</td><td>Within milliseconds, by inactivation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I<sub>TO</sub></td><td>Right after the upstroke</td><td>Early in the plateau; makes the notch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I<sub>Ca</sub></td><td>After Phase 0, inward through L-type channels</td><td>Decays slowly through the plateau; closes in Phase 3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I<sub>K</sub></td><td>Builds slowly during the plateau</td><td>Peaks in Phase 3, then turns off at rest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I<sub>K1</sub></td><td>On at rest; returns in late Phase 3</td><td>Shuts off during depolarization and the plateau</td></tr></table>\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Pacemaker potential: SA and AV nodes (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<p><b>NBME focus:</b> which current drives each nodal phase, why the nodal upstroke is Ca²⁺ and slow, how drugs change rate (Ca²⁺ blockers, HCN blockers), and which pacemaker takes over when one fails.</p>\n<h3>Key terms from the lecture</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Calcium is the coupler</span> between the electrical (chemical) and mechanical processes.</li>\n<li><b>Chronotropy</b> = heart rate · <b>positive inotropy</b> = more contractility.</li>\n<li><b>Automaticity</b> = self-excitation: a self-generated, unstable Phase 4. Only the slow ones fire on their own.</li>\n<li>Phase 4 is the RMP in working cells but the <b>pacemaker potential</b> (prepotential) in nodal cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The nodal action potential, phase by phase</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Current</th><th>What happens</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4</b> · pacemaker potential</td><td><span class=\"hy\">If</span> then <b>ICa-T</b></td><td>Funny HCN channels open at about −60 mV, <b>turned on by hyperpolarization</b>, carrying inward Na⁺. K⁺ channels shut. T-type (transient) Ca²⁺ joins at about −55 to −50 mV and pushes toward threshold.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>0</b> · upstroke</td><td><span class=\"hy\">ICa-L</span></td><td>Threshold ≈ <b>−40 mV</b> → L-type (long-lasting) Ca²⁺ channels open. Slow, shallow upstroke (less steep dV/dt); peaks around 0 to +10 mV. Fast Na⁺ channels are inactivated in nodal cells.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3</b> · repolarization</td><td><b>IK</b></td><td>Ca²⁺ channels inactivate; delayed-rectifier K⁺ efflux → back to about <b>−60 mV</b>, the reset point.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1 and 2</td><td>None</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Absent — no plateau, no true resting potential.</span></td></tr></table>\n<h3>Three reasons Phase 4 is unstable (lecture notes)</h3>\n<ol><li><b>K⁺ decay</b>: K⁺ channels shut right after repolarization, so less K⁺ leaves.</li><li><b>If</b>, the funny Na⁺ current through HCN channels, opened by repolarization.</li><li><b>T-type Ca²⁺</b> channels near threshold.</li></ol>\n<p>Permeability sequence: ↑PNa (f), ↓PK → ↑PCa (T), ↓PNa (f) → at threshold ↑PCa (L), ↓PCa (T) → at the peak ↑PK, ↓PCa (L).</p>\n<h3>Ventricular vs SA node AP</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Ventricular (working)</th><th>SA node (pacemaker)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 0 ion</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Na⁺</span></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Ca²⁺</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phases</td><td>5, including RMP</td><td>3: 0, 3, 4</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rest</td><td>Stable, −90 mV</td><td>Unstable, less negative, drifts up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Plateau</td><td>Yes, 150–300 ms</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Overshoot</td><td>Up to about +30 mV</td><td>Up to about +10 mV, no spike</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Phase 0 slope</td><td>Steep → fast conduction</td><td>Less steep → slow conduction</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Drugs: the classic NBME angle</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Verapamil</b> and other Ca²⁺ channel blockers block <span class=\"hy\">ICa-L, not ICa-T</span>. They hit the <b>SA node more than ventricular contraction</b>: slower rate and slower AV conduction.</li>\n<li>Blocking <b>HCN/If</b> (for example ivabradine) flattens the Phase 4 slope → threshold reached later → lower heart rate.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Pacemaker hierarchy and overdrive suppression</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Pacemaker</th><th>Order</th><th>Intrinsic rate</th><th>Conduction</th></tr>\n<tr><td>SA node</td><td>First, dominant</td><td><b>60–80/min</b></td><td></td></tr>\n<tr><td>AV node</td><td>Second</td><td><b>40–50/min</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Slowest: the crucial delay</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>His, bundle branches</td><td>Third</td><td rowspan=\"2\"><b>30–40/min</b></td><td rowspan=\"2\"><span class=\"hy\">Fastest</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Purkinje fibers</td><td>Fourth</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Path: SA → atria → AV node (delay) → His → Purkinje → ventricles.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Overdrive suppression</span>: the fastest pacemaker, the SA node, resets slower ones before they reach threshold, so it sets the rate.</li>\n<li>SA node fails → AV node escape at 40–50/min. AV node fails (complete block) → atria keep the SA rate; ventricles follow Purkinje at 30–40/min.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Effects of cardiac nerves (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Sympathetic: the gas pedal</th><th>Parasympathetic: the brake pedal</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Transmitter → receptor</td><td>NE → <b>β1</b></td><td>ACh (vagus) → <b>M2</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>G protein</td><td><b>Gs</b> → adenylyl cyclase → <span class=\"hy\">↑ cAMP → PKA</span></td><td><b>Gi</b>: <span class=\"hy\">αi ↓ adenylyl cyclase (↓ cAMP)</span> + <span class=\"hy\">βγ opens K⁺ channels (IK-ACh)</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chronotropy (rate, SA node)</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dromotropy (conduction, AV node)</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inotropy (contractility)</td><td>↑ (ventricles)</td><td>Ventricles: minimal or no effect</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>The ANS <b>modulates, it does not create</b>, the SA node's rhythm. It acts on nodal tissue, so it changes rate without rewriting the ventricular plateau. <b>Vagal tone dominates at rest.</b></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Why vagal slowing is faster in onset:</span> the parasympathetic path has <b>two parallel arms</b>, and the βγ arm opens K⁺ channels directly with no second messenger. Sympathetic relies on a single cAMP/PKA arm.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Why ventricles show no vagal effect:</span> parasympathetic fibers supply the <b>atria and nodes only</b>; sympathetic supply atria and ventricles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Sympathetic \"triple effect\": PKA phosphorylation targets</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Site</th><th>PKA target</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>SA node</td><td>If and Ca²⁺ channels</td><td>Steeper phase 4 → threshold (≈ −40 mV) sooner → <b>↑ HR</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>AV node</td><td>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</td><td><b>↑ conduction velocity</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ventricular muscle</td><td>L-type Ca²⁺ channels + <span class=\"hy\">phospholamban</span></td><td>↑ Ca²⁺ influx and SR release → ↑ force (<b>inotropy</b>); phospholamban releases SERCA → faster relaxation (<b>lusitropy</b>)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Integrated effect on the SA node pacemaker potential</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sympathetic:</b> <span class=\"hy\">↑ ICa-T, ↑ If</span> → steeper phase 4 → threshold reached faster → (+) chronotropic.</li>\n<li><b>Parasympathetic:</b> <span class=\"hy\">↓ ICa-T, ↓ If, ↑ IK-ACh</span> → shallower phase 4 and a hyperpolarized cell → threshold later → (−) chronotropic, and ↓ AV conduction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Drugs: the classic Step 1 discriminators</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Target</th><th>Net effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Metoprolol (β-blocker, class II)</td><td>β1</td><td>Flatter phase 4: <b>↓ HR, ↓ contractility</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Verapamil, diltiazem (non-DHP CCB, class IV)</td><td>L-type Ca²⁺ in SA and AV nodes</td><td><b>↓ HR, ↓ AV conduction</b> (and ↓ contractility)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ivabradine</td><td>If, SA node only</td><td><span class=\"hy\">↓ HR only</span>: no effect on contractility or AV conduction; ventricular plateau unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adenosine</td><td>A1 → ↑ K⁺ efflux (like ACh)</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Transient AV block</span></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lidocaine, flecainide (class I)</td><td>Fast Na⁺, phase 0</td><td>Slow upstroke and conduction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Amiodarone, sotalol (class III)</td><td>K⁺ channels, phase 3</td><td>Prolong AP and refractory period</td></tr></table>\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. The cardiac cycle: Wiggers diagram (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Atria</th><th>Ventricles</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Systole</td><td>0.1 s</td><td>0.3 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diastole</td><td>0.7 s</td><td>0.5 s</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">At higher heart rates, diastole shortens more than systole</span>, which limits ventricular filling time. The phase cut first is <b>reduced filling (diastasis)</b>, the longest phase of the cycle.</li></ul>\n<h3>Key volumes</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Normal</th></tr>\n<tr><td>EDV</td><td>Volume at the end of diastole: maximum volume = <b>preload</b></td><td>~120 mL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ESV</td><td>Volume at the end of systole: minimum volume</td><td>~50 mL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SV</td><td><b>EDV − ESV</b></td><td>~70 mL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EF</td><td><b>SV / EDV</b>: the <span class=\"hy\">preferred index of contractility</span></td><td>~58% (55–70%)</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>EDV filling: <b>70–80% passive</b> (general diastole) + <b>20–30% (~25 mL) atrial kick</b> (atrial systole).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">↑ Preload alone</span> (↑EDV): SV ↑, ESV unchanged, EF ↑ slightly (Frank–Starling); contractility unchanged. <span class=\"hy\">↓ Contractility alone</span>: SV ↓ and EF ↓ at the same EDV. Only the second is true pump dysfunction.</li></ul>\n<h3>Phases A–G and the valve events between them</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Valves</th><th>What happens</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A · Atrial systole</td><td>AV open, semilunar closed</td><td>Atrial P ↑ (RA ~8, LA ~10 mm Hg) → rapid active filling; a wave; S4 if the ventricle is stiff</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><i>AV valves close → S1</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>B · Isovolumetric contraction</td><td>All closed</td><td>P rises steeply at constant volume (EDV); c wave; ends when LV P &gt; aortic (~80) and RV P &gt; pulmonary (~10)</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><i>Semilunar valves open</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>C · Rapid ejection</td><td>Semilunar open</td><td>First 1/3 of ejection, <b>~70% of SV</b>; arterial P rises to systolic peak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>D · Reduced ejection</td><td>Semilunar open</td><td>Last 2/3; ejection rate and P fall; v wave begins to build</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><i>Semilunar valves close → S2, dicrotic notch</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>E · Isovolumetric relaxation</td><td>All closed</td><td>P falls at constant volume (ESV); ends when LV P &lt; LA P</td></tr>\n<tr><td colspan=\"3\"><i>AV valves open</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>F · Rapid filling</td><td>AV open</td><td>Rapid passive inflow; S3 if present</td></tr>\n<tr><td>G · Reduced filling (diastasis)</td><td>AV open</td><td><b>Longest phase</b>; most affected by HR</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Jugular venous pulse and the aortic pressure curve</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Wave</th><th>Cause</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>a</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Atrial contraction</span> (follows the P wave); no inlet valves, so the pulse travels up the jugular vein</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>c</b></td><td>Tricuspid bulging back into the atrium during ventricular contraction</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>v</b></td><td><span class=\"hy\">Atrial filling</span>: gradual rise during reduced ejection and isovolumetric relaxation, before the AV valve opens</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Aortic curve: <b>anacrotic</b> (ascending) limb = ejection · <span class=\"hy\">dicrotic notch = aortic valve closure, end of systole</span> (not ejection) · <b>dicrotic wave</b> = rebound off the closed valve · <b>catacrotic</b> (descending) limb = run-off after ejection.</li></ul>\n<h3>Normal pressures (mm Hg, min–max)</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>RA</th><th>RV</th><th>Pulmonary artery</th><th>LA</th><th>LV</th><th>Aorta</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0–8</td><td>5–25</td><td>8–25</td><td>2–12</td><td>10–120</td><td>80–120</td></tr></table>\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Cardiac output &amp; pressure-volume loops (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>Cardiac output</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">CO = SV × HR</span> (~5 L/min at rest). <b>SV = EDV − ESV</b> = blood ejected per beat.</li>\n<li><b>Cardiac reserve</b> = maximal CO − resting CO.</li>\n<li>The right and left heart must pump the same output over time; the <b>pulmonary circulation</b> takes the whole 5 L/min at low pressure because it is very compliant.</li>\n<li>Very fast heart rates cut <b>diastole</b> most → less filling → ↓EDV → ↓SV, so CO can fall in tachyarrhythmias.</li></ul>\n<h3>The three determinants of stroke volume</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>What it is</th><th>Raised by</th><th>Effect on SV</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Preload</b></td><td>Stretch before contraction = <b>EDV</b> (≈ LVEDP)</td><td>↑ venous return, venoconstriction (α1), blood volume, slower HR, leg raise</td><td>↑ (Frank–Starling)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Afterload</b></td><td>Load the ventricle ejects against ≈ <b>aortic pressure</b>; wall tension (Laplace: T ∝ P·r / wall thickness)</td><td>Hypertension, <b>aortic stenosis</b>, α1 vasoconstriction</td><td>↓ (larger ESV)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Contractility</b></td><td>Force at a given preload (inotropy); best index = <b>EF</b></td><td>β1 / sympathetic, ↑ intracellular Ca²⁺, digoxin, ↓ ECF Na⁺ (less NCX Ca²⁺ efflux)</td><td>↑ (smaller ESV)</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">↓ contractility:</span> acidosis, hypercapnia, hypoxia, ischemia, β-blockers, heart failure.</li>\n<li>LV hypertrophy is the response to chronic afterload: a thicker wall <b>lowers wall tension</b> (Laplace).</li></ul>\n<h3>Frank–Starling law</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">More EDV = more stretch = stronger contraction</span> → SV matches venous return beat by beat. It is intrinsic (no nerves needed).</li>\n<li>Mechanism: better actin–myosin overlap and <b>↑ myofilament Ca²⁺ sensitivity</b> at longer sarcomere lengths.</li>\n<li>Keeps RV and LV outputs equal: extra RV output → ↑ LV filling → ↑ LV SV.</li>\n<li>Sympathetic stimulation / inotropes shift the whole curve <b>up</b>; heart failure shifts it <b>down</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>The LV pressure-volume loop (counter-clockwise)</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Corner / side</th><th>Event</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Bottom right</td><td><b>Mitral closes</b> (EDV) → isovolumetric contraction (vertical line up)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Top right</td><td><b>Aortic opens</b> → ejection along the top (volume falls)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Top left</td><td><b>Aortic closes</b> (ESV) → isovolumetric relaxation (vertical line down)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bottom left</td><td><b>Mitral opens</b> → filling along the bottom</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Width = <b>SV</b>; EF = width ÷ EDV. <b>Area inside = stroke work</b>.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Change</th><th>Loop</th></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ Preload (fluids, AR)</td><td>EDV moves right, ESV ≈ same → wider loop, ↑SV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ Afterload (HTN, AS)</td><td>Taller loop, ESV moves right → narrower, ↓SV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ Contractility (β1, dobutamine)</td><td>ESV moves left → wider, ↑SV, ↑EF</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↓ Contractility (HF)</td><td>ESV moves right → narrower, ↓EF</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Chronic aortic regurgitation:</b> very large EDV (big preload), wide pulse pressure, no true isovolumetric phases.</li></ul>\n<h3>Cardiac work</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Stroke work ≈ SV × mean aortic pressure</b> (pressure–volume work) + a small kinetic-energy part (1/2 mv²).</li>\n<li>Kinetic energy is normally ~1% but becomes large when blood is ejected fast through a narrow opening (<b>aortic stenosis</b>).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pressure work costs far more O₂ than volume work</span> because O₂ use tracks wall tension.</li></ul>\n<h3>Measuring cardiac output</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Fick:</b> <span class=\"hy\">CO = O₂ consumption ÷ (arterial O₂ − mixed venous O₂)</span>. Mixed venous blood must come from the <b>pulmonary artery</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Indicator (dye) dilution:</b> CO = amount injected ÷ (mean concentration × time of first pass), ignoring recirculation.</li>\n<li>Thermodilution works on the same principle with cold saline.</li></ul>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Hemodynamics: flow, pressure &amp; resistance (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>The master equation</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Q = ΔP / R</span> (hemodynamic Ohm's law). For the whole circulation: <b>CO = (MAP − RAP) / TPR</b>, so <span class=\"hy\">MAP = CO × TPR</span>.</li>\n<li>ΔP = aortic pressure − vena cava / right atrial pressure (≈ 100 − 0 mmHg). Normal TPR ≈ 100/5 = 20 mmHg·min/L.</li>\n<li>The body redirects blood flow by changing <b>resistance</b>, not pressure. Sympathetic α₁ → arteriolar constriction → ↑ TPR, ↓ flow to that bed.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Biggest pressure drop = arterioles</span> (small radius, highest wall-to-lumen ratio). Small drop from aorta to small arteries; small drop across capillaries. Pulsations are damped almost to 0 by the capillaries.</li></ul>\n<h3>Poiseuille's law</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">R = 8ηL / πr⁴</span>. η = viscosity (set by <b>hematocrit</b>: ↑ polycythemia, ↓ anemia). L = length (↑ with obesity). r = radius.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">R ∝ 1/r⁴</span>: halve r → R ×16; double r → R ÷16 and flow ×16 (at the same ΔP).</li>\n<li>Percent change: r falls 20% → R × 1/0.8⁴ ≈ 2.4. r rises 20% → flow × 1.2⁴ ≈ 2.1.</li>\n<li>Arterioles have abundant smooth muscle → they are the <b>faucets</b> and the main site of TPR control.</li></ul>\n<h3>Series vs parallel</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Series</th><th>Parallel</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Formula</td><td>R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃</td><td>1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Total</td><td>Greater than any one</td><td>Less than any one</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Same at each level</td><td>Flow</td><td>Pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Artery → arteriole → capillary → venule within one organ</td><td>Organ beds off the aorta</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Removing a parallel bed (nephrectomy, amputation) ↑ TPR.</span> Adding a bed ↓ TPR. Parallel arrangement lets each organ regulate its own flow.</li></ul>\n<h3>Laminar vs turbulent flow</h3>\n<ul><li>Laminar = silent, parallel layers, <b>fastest in the center</b>, slowest at the wall.</li>\n<li>Turbulent = eddies → <span class=\"hy\">sound: murmur over a valve, bruit over a vessel</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Reynolds number = ρDv / η</span>. Turbulence ↑ with ↑ velocity, ↑ diameter, ↑ density, <b>↓ viscosity (anemia)</b>, and abrupt geometry changes (plaque, stenotic valve). Above ~<b>2000</b> → turbulent even in a straight smooth vessel.</li>\n<li>Anemia → ↓ viscosity + ↑ CO → flow murmur with normal valves.</li></ul>\n<h3>Flow vs velocity</h3>\n<ul><li>Flow (L/min) is the same at every level of the circuit. <span class=\"hy\">Velocity v = Q / A</span> (cm/s).</li>\n<li>Aorta: small area (~4 cm²) → fastest (~20 cm/s at 5 L/min). <span class=\"hy\">Capillaries: huge total area → slowest</span> → time for O₂, CO₂ and nutrient exchange.</li></ul>\n<h3>Compliance &amp; blood pressure</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">C = ΔV / ΔP</span> (ability to stretch). <b>Veins</b> = high compliance, volume reservoir (capacitance vessels, <b>unstressed volume</b>). <b>Arteries</b> = low compliance, pressure reservoir (<b>stressed volume</b>). Aging → elastin loss → ↓ arterial compliance.</li>\n<li><b>Systolic</b> ← SV and arterial compliance. <b>Diastolic</b> ← TPR. <span class=\"hy\">PP = SP − DP ≈ SV / compliance.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">MAP ≈ DP + ⅓ PP</span> (diastole lasts longer). 120/80 → MAP ≈ 93.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Pulse pressure</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Aortic regurgitation</td><td>Wide</td><td>↑ SV (bounding pulses, low DP)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arteriosclerosis / aging</td><td>Wide</td><td>↓ compliance → isolated systolic HTN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hyperthyroidism, anemia, exercise</td><td>Wide</td><td>↑ SV ± ↓ TPR</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cardiac tamponade</td><td>Narrow</td><td>↓ SV (Beck triad)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aortic stenosis, heart failure, cardiogenic shock</td><td>Narrow</td><td>↓ SV</td></tr></table>\n\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Cardiac &amp; vascular function curves (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>Core idea</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">At steady state CO = VR.</span> CO = SV × HR. Venous return sets preload, which raises SV (Frank–Starling). Sympathetic stimulation and venoconstriction raise venous return. Cardiac output has a ceiling (plateau).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">CVP = right atrial pressure (RAP)</span>, the shared x-axis of both curves.</li></ul>\n<h3>Vascular (venous return) function curve</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">VR = (MSFP − RAP) / RVR</span>. MSFP (mean systemic filling pressure, ≈ 7 mmHg) is the upstream pressure in the peripheral veins; RAP (≈ 2 mmHg) is the back-pressure. Example: (7 − 2)/1 = 5 L/min.</li>\n<li>If the gradient falls (RAP rises toward MSFP), venous return falls. <b>x-intercept = MSFP</b>: when RAP = MSFP, VR = 0.</li>\n<li>Below RAP ≈ 0 the great veins collapse entering the thorax → the curve <b>plateaus</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">MSFP is set by blood volume and venous tone only.</span></li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Change</th><th>Effect on VR curve</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ Blood volume or venous tone</td><td class=\"hy\">Parallel shift up/right, ↑ MSFP</td><td>Transfusion, fluid infusion, sympathetic venoconstriction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↓ Blood volume or venous tone</td><td class=\"hy\">Parallel shift down/left, ↓ MSFP</td><td>Hemorrhage, spinal anesthesia (venodilation)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↓ TPR (arteriolar dilation)</td><td>Rotates up (steeper), same MSFP</td><td>Exercise, AV shunt</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ TPR (arteriolar constriction)</td><td>Rotates down (flatter), same MSFP</td><td>Vasopressors</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Cardiac function (Frank–Starling) curve</h3>\n<ul><li>CO vs RAP/LVEDP. Rises with preload, then plateaus at the ceiling.</li>\n<li><b>Inotropy</b>: positive (catecholamines, dobutamine, digoxin, exercise) shifts the curve <b>up</b>; negative (HFrEF, narcotic overdose, sympathetic inhibition, MI) shifts it <b>down</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Afterload</b>: ↓ afterload (↓ TPR) shifts the curve up/left; ↑ afterload shifts it down/right.</li></ul>\n<h3>The operating point</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Where the two curves cross: CO = VR at one RAP.</span> If CO briefly exceeds VR, the atrium drains, RAP falls, VR rises and CO falls until they match again.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Curve that moves</th><th>CO</th><th>RAP</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>↑ Blood volume (transfusion, fluids)</td><td>VR shifts up</td><td>↑</td><td>↑</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>↓ Blood volume (hemorrhage, spinal anesthesia)</td><td>VR shifts down (↓ MSFP)</td><td>↓</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Positive inotropy (dobutamine, digoxin)</td><td>Cardiac curve up</td><td>↑</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Negative inotropy (MI, HFrEF, narcotics)</td><td>Cardiac curve down</td><td>↓</td><td>↑</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ TPR (vasopressors)</td><td>Both curves down</td><td>↓</td><td>≈ unchanged</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↓ TPR (exercise, AV shunt)</td><td>Both curves up</td><td>↑</td><td>≈ unchanged</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Positive inotropy chain:</span> ↑ contractility → ↓ ESV → ↑ CO → less blood left in the RA → ↓ RAP → wider MSFP − RAP gradient → ↑ VR. Negative inotropy is the reverse: ↓ contractility → ↑ RAP → ↓ CO.</p><h2 id=\"g8\">8. Regulation of blood pressure (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">BP = CO × TPR</span>. Short-term control is neural (baroreceptors); long-term control is hormonal and renal (RAAS, ANP), because <b>baroreceptors reset within days</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Baroreceptor reflex</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Site</th><th>Afferent</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Carotid sinus</td><td><b>CN IX</b> → NTS</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Aortic arch</td><td><b>CN X</b> → NTS</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Stretch receptors: ↑ pressure → ↑ firing. <span class=\"hy\">Sympathetic activation is inversely proportional to baroreceptor firing</span>.</li>\n<li>Hypotension → ↓ firing → ↑ sympathetic, ↓ parasympathetic → ↑ HR, ↑ contractility (↑ CO) and vasoconstriction (↑ TPR).</li>\n<li>Hypertension → ↑ firing → ↓ sympathetic, ↑ parasympathetic → ↓ HR, vasodilation.</li>\n<li>Vasomotor center: pressor and depressor areas; cardioacceleratory and cardioinhibitory parts.</li></ul>\n<h3>Chemoreceptors</h3>\n<ul><li>Peripheral: <b>carotid and aortic bodies</b>; stimulus <span class=\"hy\">PaO2 &lt; 60 mmHg</span> (primary), ↑ PaCO2, ↓ pH → pressor and acceleratory zones → ↑ sympathetic → ↑ TPR, CO, MABP and ↑ breathing.</li>\n<li>Central: medulla, sense CSF pH; CO2 crosses the blood-brain barrier.</li></ul>\n<h3>RAAS</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Rate-limiting step: renin from JG cells</span>. Triggers: ↓ renal perfusion pressure, ↑ sympathetic <b>β1</b>, ↓ NaCl at the <b>macula densa</b>.</li>\n<li>Angiotensinogen (liver) → renin → Ang I → <b>ACE (lung endothelium)</b> → Ang II (octapeptide).</li>\n<li>Ang II: vasoconstriction, aldosterone (zona glomerulosa), ADH, thirst. Aldosterone: ↑ Na⁺ reabsorption, ↑ K⁺ secretion in distal tubule and collecting duct.</li>\n<li>Sustained RAAS: ↑ volume and pressure, compensatory then maladaptive.</li></ul>\n<h3>ANP</h3>\n<ul><li>From atria on <b>stretch</b> (↑ volume, VR, atrial pressure) → vasodilation, natriuresis and diuresis, ↓ renin, ↓ Ang II, ↓ aldosterone → ↓ volume and pressure.</li></ul>\n<h3>Drugs and renal artery stenosis</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Drug</th><th>Blocks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>ACE inhibitors</td><td>Ang I → Ang II</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ARBs</td><td>Ang II receptors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Spironolactone, eplerenone</td><td>Aldosterone receptors in the distal tubule</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>RAS: ↓ perfusion → ↑ renin → ↑ Ang II → hypertension.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ACEI/ARB first-line in unilateral RAS</span> with a normal other kidney, but relatively contraindicated in <b>bilateral RAS or a solitary kidney</b>: losing Ang II's <b>efferent arteriolar constriction</b> drops GFR → AKI.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. ECG essentials (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>Paper and conventions</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value at 25 mm/s</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Small square (1 mm)</td><td class=\"hy\">0.04 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Large square (5 mm)</td><td class=\"hy\">0.20 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Amplitude</td><td class=\"hy\">10 mm = 1 mV</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">HR = 1500 ÷ small squares between R waves</span> (= 300 ÷ large squares = 60 ÷ R-R in s).</li>\n<li>Check paper speed: at 50 mm/s each small square is 0.02 s, so waves look twice as wide.</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-paper.jpg\" alt=\"ECG paper grid\"><figcaption>ECG paper: small square 0.04 s, large square 0.20 s, 10 mm = 1 mV.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Deflection rules</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Depolarization toward (+) = up. Away = down. Perpendicular = flat.</span></li>\n<li>Repolarization is the reverse: toward (+) = down, away = up. The T wave is upright because repolarization runs <b>epicardium → endocardium</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Leads</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Lead</th><th>Wiring / axis</th><th>Views</th></tr>\n<tr><td>I</td><td>RA − → LA +, 0°</td><td>Lateral</td></tr>\n<tr><td>II</td><td>RA − → LL +, +60°</td><td>Inferior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>III</td><td>LA − → LL +, +120°</td><td>Inferior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>aVR / aVL / aVF</td><td>Right arm / left arm / left foot</td><td>aVR normally negative; aVF inferior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>V1–V2</td><td>4th ICS right / left of sternum</td><td class=\"hy\">Septum</td></tr>\n<tr><td>V3–V4</td><td>Between V2–V4 / 5th ICS MCL</td><td class=\"hy\">Anterior</td></tr>\n<tr><td>V5–V6</td><td>Anterior / mid-axillary line</td><td class=\"hy\">Lateral</td></tr></table>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-einthoven.jpg\" alt=\"Einthoven's triangle\"><figcaption>Einthoven's triangle: lead II +60°, lead III +120°.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Waves, segments, intervals</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Normal</th></tr>\n<tr><td>P wave</td><td>Atrial depolarization (bigger = atrial enlargement)</td><td>&lt; 0.11 s, 0.1–0.3 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>QRS</td><td>Ventricular depolarization (Q septum, R LV, S RV). Hides atrial repolarization.</td><td>About 0.06–0.10 s, 0.5–2.0 mV</td></tr>\n<tr><td>T wave</td><td>Ventricular repolarization</td><td>Upright</td></tr>\n<tr><td>U wave</td><td>Slow papillary muscle repolarization</td><td>Small</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PR interval</td><td class=\"hy\">AV conduction time incl. AV delay</td><td>0.12–0.20 s</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ST segment</td><td>Plateau, ventricles fully depolarized</td><td>Isoelectric</td></tr>\n<tr><td>QT interval</td><td>Electrical systole (depolarization + repolarization)</td><td>0.25–0.43 s</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"mn\">The slide lists QRS duration as 0.6–0.8 s; the standard value is about 0.06–0.10 s (wide if ≥ 0.12 s).</p>\n<h3>Electrolytes and ischemia</h3>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Hyperkalemia → tall peaked T</span>, then flat P and wide QRS (less negative RMP inactivates Na⁺ channels).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hypokalemia → flat T, prominent U</span>, long QT (loop diuretics).</li>\n<li>Injury current from partly depolarized cells: <span class=\"hy\">ST elevation = acute transmural MI; ST depression = ischemia</span>. PR lengthens with vagal tone, AV block and drugs that slow conduction.</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-injury-current.jpg\" alt=\"Injury current and ST elevation\"><figcaption>Injury current shifts the ST segment.</figcaption></figure>\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. ECG part 2: blocks and arrhythmias (Dr. Sampath)</h2>\n<h3>Acute coronary syndrome</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Type</th><th>ECG</th><th>Enzymes</th><th>Lesion</th></tr>\n<tr><td>STEMI</td><td class=\"hy\">ST elevation (later Q waves)</td><td>Raised</td><td>Complete occlusion, transmural</td></tr>\n<tr><td>NSTEMI</td><td>ST depression and/or T inversion</td><td class=\"hy\">Raised</td><td>Subendocardial</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unstable angina</td><td>Same as NSTEMI</td><td class=\"hy\">Normal</td><td>No necrosis</td></tr></table>\n<h3>AV blocks</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Block</th><th>ECG</th><th>Site</th><th>Treatment</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1st degree</td><td>PR &gt; 0.20 s, constant, no drops</td><td>AV node</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mobitz I</td><td class=\"hy\">PR longer, longer, drop</td><td>AV node</td><td>Usually benign; atropine improves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mobitz II</td><td class=\"hy\">Constant PR, sudden drop</td><td>Below AV node</td><td>Pacemaker; atropine worsens</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3rd degree</td><td class=\"hy\">P and QRS dissociated</td><td>Complete</td><td>Pacemaker; escape 20–40/min if ventricular</td></tr></table>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-mobitz1.jpg\" alt=\"Mobitz I\"><figcaption>Mobitz I: PR₁ &lt; PR₂ &lt; PR₃, then a dropped QRS.</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-mobitz2.jpg\" alt=\"Mobitz II\"><figcaption>Mobitz II: constant PR, then a dropped QRS.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Systematic approach</h3>\n<ul><li>P for every QRS? Regular? Rate (1500 ÷ small squares)?</li>\n<li>Sinus tachycardia &gt; 100 (exercise, fever, anxiety, hypovolemia). Sinus bradycardia &lt; 60 (athletes, beta-blockers, vagal tone).</li></ul>\n<h3>Torsades de pointes</h3>\n<ul><li>Polymorphic VT with twisting QRS; <span class=\"hy\">long QT predisposes</span>; can become VF → sudden death.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Drugs ABCDE: antiarrhythmics IA and III, macrolides, haloperidol, TCAs, ondansetron</span>; plus diuretics.</li>\n<li>Electrolytes: ↓ K⁺, ↓ Mg²⁺, ↓ Ca²⁺. Congenital: <span class=\"hy\">Romano-Ward (AD, hearing normal), Jervell and Lange-Nielsen (AR, deaf)</span>.</li>\n<li>Treatment: <span class=\"hy\">IV magnesium sulfate</span>, stop drugs, correct electrolytes, cardioversion/defibrillation.</li></ul>\n<h3>Atrial fibrillation vs flutter</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th></th><th>Atrial fibrillation</th><th>Atrial flutter</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Atria</td><td>Chaotic, no P waves</td><td class=\"hy\">Sawtooth F waves, about 300/min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rhythm</td><td class=\"hy\">Irregularly irregular</td><td>Regular (2:1, 4:1) or variable</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ventricular rate set by</td><td class=\"hy\">AV node refractory period</td><td>Conduction ratio</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Risk</td><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"hy\">Thromboembolism (stroke) → anticoagulate (warfarin, DOACs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rate control</td><td colspan=\"2\">Beta-blockers, non-dihydropyridine CCBs, digoxin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Definitive</td><td>Cardioversion, ablation</td><td class=\"hy\">Ablation of the cavotricuspid isthmus</td></tr></table>\n<p>AF risk factors: hypertension, CAD, binge drinking (\"holiday heart\").</p>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"figs/crs/ecg-flutter.jpg\" alt=\"Atrial flutter 4:1\"><figcaption>Atrial flutter with 4:1 block.</figcaption></figure>\n", "crs-histology": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. Smooth muscle (Lecture 1)</h2>\n<h3>Six defining features</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Smooth muscle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Spindle-shaped, unbranched cells with tapering ends</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td class=\"hy\">One central, elongated (“cigar”) nucleus; organelles at its poles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striations</td><td>None: no myofibrils or sarcomeres; actin and myosin form a lattice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Troponin</td><td class=\"hy\">None: Ca²⁺ binds calmodulin, which regulates myosin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gap junctions</td><td>Abundant; no neuromuscular junctions (motor end plates)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Connective tissue</td><td>Fine endomysium only: external lamina + reticular fibers (no epimysium/perimysium)</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle can undergo both hypertrophy AND hyperplasia (e.g., myometrium in pregnancy). Cardiac muscle cannot.</span></li>\n<li>Smooth muscle cells secrete their own matrix: <b>collagen, elastin, proteoglycans</b> → central role in atherosclerosis and vessel wall remodelling.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Identifying smooth muscle on H&amp;E</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Longitudinal section: <b>staggered cells</b> whose tapered ends tuck between neighbors; central elongated nuclei; pink, uniform cytoplasm with <b>no cross-striations</b>.</li>\n<li>Cross-section: profiles vary in diameter, and <b>only the widest (cut through the middle) show a nucleus</b> — a common image question.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Don’t confuse:</span> dense regular CT = thin, dark nuclei squeezed between collagen fibers; nerve = wavy fibers with S-shaped nuclei.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Contracted cell: filaments pull on dense bodies in many directions → cell bulges into a globular shape and the nucleus becomes corkscrew-shaped.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Single-unit vs multi-unit</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Single-unit (visceral)</th><th>Multi-unit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Gap junctions</td><td>Many — sheet contracts as one unit</td><td>Few — each cell acts separately</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control</td><td>Spontaneous/pacemaker activity, stretch, hormones</td><td>Mainly autonomic nerves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>GI tract, uterus, ureter, bladder, small vessels</td><td>Iris, ciliary body, vas deferens, arrector pili, large arteries</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Nerves end in <b>varicosities</b> that release transmitter near the cells (diffuse junctions), not true neuromuscular junctions.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Gut wall: inner circular + outer longitudinal layers produce peristalsis; the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies between them. Interstitial cells of Cajal are the gut pacemakers.</span> Clinical link: <b>Hirschsprung disease</b> = absent myenteric ganglion cells.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Ultrastructure: every striated structure has a smooth-muscle stand-in</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Striated muscle</th><th>Smooth muscle equivalent</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Z disc</td><td>Dense body (α-actinin)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>T-tubule</td><td>Caveolae</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Troponin C</td><td>Calmodulin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sarcomere</td><td>None — actin–myosin lattice</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dense bodies</b> contain <b>α-actinin</b>. Two kinds: cytoplasmic and membrane-associated (the latter pass force to neighboring cells and reticular fibers). Thin (actin) and intermediate filaments both insert into dense bodies.</li>\n<li>Intermediate filaments: <b>desmin in all smooth muscle</b>, plus <b>vimentin in vascular smooth muscle</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Desmin and smooth muscle actin (SMA) immunostains confirm that a tumor is of smooth muscle origin.</span></li>\n<li>Thin filaments greatly outnumber thick (~12–15 : 1). Lattice arrangement lets the cell shorten much more than striated muscle.</li>\n<li><b>Caveolae</b> = flask-shaped invaginations of the sarcolemma that take up and release Ca²⁺. SR is sparse (stores Ca²⁺). <b>No T-tubules.</b></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Contraction: Ca²⁺–calmodulin–MLCK</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Ca²⁺ rises: influx from ECF (L-type and receptor-operated channels) + release from SR (IP₃)</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ binds <b>calmodulin</b></li>\n<li>Ca²⁺–calmodulin activates <b>myosin light chain kinase (MLCK)</b></li>\n<li>MLCK phosphorylates the myosin light chain</li>\n<li>Myosin binds actin → contraction</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Relaxation: <b>myosin light chain phosphatase</b> removes the phosphate. <b>Latch state</b> = tone held for a long time with very little ATP (slow, sustained, energy-efficient).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle is regulated on the THICK filament (myosin); striated muscle is regulated on the THIN filament (troponin).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Where to find smooth muscle on slides</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Layer</th><th>What to look for</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Arteries, veins</td><td>Tunica media</td><td>Circular layers; thickest in muscular arteries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GI tract</td><td>Muscularis mucosae + muscularis externa</td><td>Inner circular + outer longitudinal; plexus between</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uterus</td><td>Myometrium</td><td>Thick, interlacing bundles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bladder, ureter</td><td>Detrusor / muscularis</td><td>Bundles running in several directions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bronchioles</td><td>Smooth muscle layer</td><td>Prominent ring; no cartilage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skin, eye</td><td>Arrector pili; iris, ciliary body</td><td>Small bundles beside follicle / in iris stroma</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Esophagus muscularis externa: upper third skeletal, middle third mixed, lower third smooth.</span></li></ul>\n<h3>Growth, repair and tumors</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hypertrophy + hyperplasia</b>: pregnant uterus; thick bladder wall in outflow obstruction; airway smooth muscle in chronic asthma.</li>\n<li><b>Regeneration</b>: cells divide by mitosis; <b>pericytes</b> around small vessels also give rise to new smooth muscle cells.</li>\n<li><b>Atherosclerosis</b>: medial SMCs migrate into the intima, proliferate and make collagen → fibrous cap.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Leiomyoma = benign “fibroid”, the most common uterine tumor; whorled bundles of spindle cells. Malignant form = leiomyosarcoma. Both desmin/SMA positive.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Cardiac muscle (Lecture 1)</h2>\n<h3>Six defining features</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Cardiac muscle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Cylindrical cells that <b>branch</b> and join end-to-end</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>One (at most two) <b>central</b> nucleus with a pale perinuclear zone</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striated</td><td>Same sarcomeres and myofilaments as skeletal muscle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intercalated discs</td><td>Fascia adherens, desmosomes and gap junctions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>High energy use</td><td>Mitochondria up to ~40% of cytoplasm; rich capillary supply; glycogen + lipid droplets as fuel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Growth</td><td class=\"hy\">Hypertrophy only — no hyperplasia, no satellite cells</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">The pale perinuclear zone holds lipofuscin (“wear-and-tear” pigment that builds up with age). Cardiac muscle is found only in the myocardium and roots of the great vessels.</span></li>\n<li>Combines both other types: striated and strong like skeletal; involuntary and continuously active like smooth; modulated by autonomic and hormonal input.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Light microscopy</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>H&amp;E: branching fibers, central nuclei, faint cross-striations, pale zone at nuclear poles.</li>\n<li>Intercalated discs = <b>dark, step-like cross lines</b>; hard to see on H&amp;E — <b>iron hematoxylin</b> shows them as dark transverse lines.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">ID RULE: striations + central nuclei + discs = cardiac. Striations + peripheral nuclei = skeletal. No striations = smooth.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The intercalated disc: three junctions</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Junction</th><th>Part of disc</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fascia adherens</td><td>Transverse</td><td>Anchors actin thin filaments of the last sarcomere (cardiac version of zonula adherens)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Desmosome</td><td>Transverse</td><td>Binds cells together (desmin IFs); resists pulling apart</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Gap junction</td><td>Lateral</td><td>Ionic coupling — spreads excitation cell to cell</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Gap junctions (connexin 43) make the myocardium a functional syncytium — the cells contract together.</span> The disc runs step-wise: transverse parts (adhesion) alternate with lateral parts (gap junctions).</li></ul>\n<h3>T-tubules and diads</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Cardiac</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>T-tubule site</td><td>A–I junction</td><td>Z line; wider</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Per sarcomere</td><td>Two</td><td>One</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>With SR</td><td>Triad</td><td>Diad</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SR</td><td>Elaborate</td><td>Less developed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mitochondria</td><td>Fewer</td><td>Very many, between myofibrils</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Diad = one T-tubule + one terminal cistern. Because the SR is smaller, <b>cardiac muscle depends on Ca²⁺ entering from the ECF</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Contraction: Ca²⁺-induced Ca²⁺ release</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>AP spreads through gap junctions and down T-tubules</li>\n<li>L-type Ca²⁺ channels open: small Ca²⁺ influx</li>\n<li>This Ca²⁺ opens <b>ryanodine receptors</b> → large SR release</li>\n<li>Ca²⁺ binds <b>troponin C</b> → cross-bridges form</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Relaxation: <b>SERCA</b> pumps Ca²⁺ back into SR; <b>Na⁺/Ca²⁺ exchanger</b> removes Ca²⁺ from the cell.</li>\n<li>Contrast: skeletal muscle uses mechanical DHP–ryanodine coupling and does not need extracellular Ca²⁺.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">When myocytes die, cardiac troponin I and T leak into the blood — the most specific lab markers of myocardial infarction.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Specialized cardiac muscle cells</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Features</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Purkinje fibers</td><td>Large, pale cells under the endocardium; few myofibrils, much glycogen; conduct fastest, to the ventricles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nodal cells (SA, AV)</td><td>Small cells, few myofibrils; depolarize spontaneously = pacemakers; SA node sets the rate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Atrial myocytes</td><td>Granules of <b>ANP</b>, released on atrial stretch → Na⁺ and water loss, vasodilation, ↓ BP</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Purkinje fibers are modified cardiac muscle cells, NOT neurons. Pale, glycogen-rich cells beneath the endocardium = Purkinje fibers.</span></li>\n<li>Ventricular myocytes make <b>BNP</b> (clinical marker of heart failure).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Injury and adaptation</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Myocardial infarction</b>: myocytes cannot divide and there are no satellite cells → dead muscle replaced by a <b>fibrous (collagen) scar</b> that does not contract.</li>\n<li><b>Early MI histology</b>: coagulative necrosis (cell outlines remain, nuclei lost), contraction bands, neutrophils within the first days.</li>\n<li><b>Hypertrophy</b> (hypertension, aortic stenosis): larger cells, not more cells, with enlarged <b>“boxcar” nuclei</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Cardiac muscle = hypertrophy only. Skeletal muscle regenerates from satellite cells; smooth muscle by mitosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Skeletal vs cardiac vs smooth (must know)</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Skeletal</th><th>Cardiac</th><th>Smooth</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cell shape</td><td>Long cylinder, unbranched</td><td>Branched cylinder</td><td>Spindle</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Nuclei</td><td>Many, peripheral</td><td>1–2, central</td><td>1, central</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Striations</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>T-tubules</td><td>A–I junction; triads</td><td>Z line; diads</td><td>None (caveolae)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Ca²⁺ binds</td><td>Troponin C</td><td>Troponin C</td><td>Calmodulin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gap junctions</td><td>No</td><td>Yes (intercalated discs)</td><td>Yes (single-unit)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control</td><td>Voluntary, motor end plates</td><td>Involuntary, pacemaker</td><td>Involuntary, autonomic/hormones</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Repair</td><td>Satellite cells</td><td>None → scar</td><td>Mitosis, pericytes</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Structure of arteries (Lecture 2)</h2>\n<h3>Three tunics, two elastic laminae</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Tunica intima</b>: endothelium (simple squamous) + basal lamina + subendothelial loose CT.</li>\n<li><b>Tunica media</b>: concentric smooth muscle with elastic and reticular fibers.</li>\n<li><b>Tunica adventitia</b>: longitudinal collagen + elastic fibers; vasa vasorum and nerves.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Internal elastic lamina (IEL) separates intima from media; external elastic lamina (EEL) separates media from adventitia. Both are fenestrated so nutrients can diffuse into the wall.</span></li>\n<li><b>Vasa vasorum</b> (“vessels of the vessel”) supply adventitia and outer media of large vessels; the inner wall is fed by diffusion from the lumen.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Three types of arteries, three jobs</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Size</th><th>Examples</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Elastic (conducting)</td><td>&gt;10 mm</td><td>Aorta, pulmonary trunk, common carotid, subclavian, common iliac</td><td>Stretch in systole, recoil in diastole → smooth, continuous flow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muscular (distributing)</td><td>2–10 mm</td><td>Most named arteries: femoral, radial, coronary, renal, splenic</td><td>Contract/relax to control supply to each organ</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Arterioles (resistance)</td><td>&lt;0.1 mm</td><td>Inside organs, just before capillary beds</td><td>Main site of peripheral resistance → set BP and capillary flow</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Moving away from the heart, <b>elastic tissue decreases and smooth muscle becomes dominant</b> in the media. Small arteries (0.1–2 mm) look like muscular arteries with fewer SMC layers; transitions are gradual.</li></ul>\n<h3>Layer by layer</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Elastic artery</th><th>Muscular artery</th><th>Arteriole</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Intima</td><td>Thick; subendothelial CT with some SMCs</td><td>Thin subendothelial layer</td><td>Endothelium; very thin subendothelium</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>IEL</td><td>Present but indistinct (looks like medial lamellae)</td><td>Prominent, wavy</td><td>Thin in large arterioles; absent in small</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Media</td><td>40–70 fenestrated elastic lamellae, SMCs between</td><td>Up to 40 layers of SMCs, few elastic fibers</td><td>1–2 layers of SMCs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>EEL</td><td>Thin, indistinct</td><td>Present in larger ones</td><td>Absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adventitia</td><td>Relatively thin; vasa vasorum</td><td>About as thick as media; vasa vasorum, lymphatics, nerves</td><td>Thin, loose CT</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Memory hook:</span> elastic artery = elastic lamellae; muscular artery = crisp IEL; arteriole = 1–2 SMC layers.</p>\n<h3>Elastic artery (aorta)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>H&amp;E: thick intima with SMCs; media of wavy elastic lamellae alternating with SMCs; thin adventitia whose vasa vasorum enter the outer media.</li>\n<li>Elastin stains (<b>orcein, Verhoeff, resorcin-fuchsin</b>) show lamellae as dark, wavy, parallel bands. ~40 lamellae at birth → ~70 in adults.</li>\n<li>Lamellae stretch in systole and recoil in diastole, keeping diastolic pressure up. The IEL is hard to see because it looks just like the medial lamellae.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">The media has NO fibroblasts: its SMCs make the elastin, collagen and ground substance.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Marfan syndrome (fibrillin-1) weakens medial elastic fibers → aortic root dilation and dissection (cystic medial degeneration).</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">With age, elastic fibers fragment and collagen increases → stiff aorta → higher systolic pressure (isolated systolic hypertension of the elderly).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Muscular artery</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Thin intima on a <b>prominent wavy IEL</b>; media up to 40 layers of circular SMCs; EEL in larger ones; thick adventitia with vasa vasorum, lymphatics and sympathetic nerves.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">The IEL looks wavy because the media contracts after death/fixation. Coronary, renal and cerebral arteries are muscular arteries — prime sites of atherosclerosis.</span></li>\n<li><b>Spotting:</b> artery = round lumen, thick media, crisp wavy IEL, <b>media thickest</b>. Companion vein = collapsed lumen, thin media, no IEL, <b>adventitia thickest</b>, may contain blood.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Arteriole</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>&lt;0.1 mm; <b>wall thickness ≈ lumen diameter</b>; IEL thin or absent; media 1–2 layers of SMCs; no EEL; thin adventitia blending with surrounding CT.</li>\n<li>Terminal arterioles → metarterioles with <b>precapillary sphincters</b> regulating flow into capillary beds.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Arterioles are the main resistance vessels. In hypertension their walls thicken — hyaline or hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Elastin vs smooth muscle in the media</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Vessel</th><th>Elastin</th><th>SMC</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Elastic</td><td>+++ lamellae</td><td>+ between lamellae</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muscular</td><td>+ IEL, EEL, fine fibers</td><td>+++ up to 40 layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arteriole</td><td>± thin IEL only</td><td>++ 1–2 layers</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">On an elastin stain: many dark lamellae = elastic artery. One crisp dark line under the endothelium, then a thick band of muscle = muscular artery.</span></li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. Diseased arterial wall (Lecture 2)</h2>\n<h3>Arteriosclerosis = “hardening” of arteries (umbrella term)</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Type</th><th>Vessels</th><th>Layer hit</th><th>Key histology</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Atherosclerosis</td><td>Elastic + large/medium muscular</td><td class=\"hy\">Intima</td><td>Plaque: lipid core + fibrous cap</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hyaline arteriolosclerosis</td><td>Arterioles</td><td>Whole wall</td><td>Pink, glassy, homogeneous thickening</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis</td><td>Arterioles</td><td>Media/intima</td><td>“Onion-skin” concentric SMC layers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mönckeberg medial calcific sclerosis</td><td>Medium muscular</td><td>Media</td><td>Calcium rings; lumen NOT narrowed</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Atherosclerosis is a disease of the intima. Hypertensive arteriosclerosis targets arterioles. Mönckeberg is medial and usually harmless (“pipestem” on X-ray).</span></li></ul>\n<h3>Three basic changes in a diseased wall</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Change</th><th>Layer</th><th>What happens</th><th>Seen in</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Smooth muscle hypertrophy</td><td>Media</td><td>Bigger (and more) SMCs → thick media, narrow lumen</td><td>Hypertension; arterioles and muscular arteries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intimal thickening</td><td>Intima</td><td>SMCs migrate from media into intima, proliferate, lay down matrix</td><td>Aging, atherosclerosis, hypertension</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collagen deposition</td><td>Intima + media</td><td>Collagen replaces elastin and SMCs → stiff, fibrotic wall</td><td>Fibrous cap, aging aorta, hyaline change</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">The smooth muscle cell drives all three: it grows, migrates into the intima, and secretes collagen (stimulated by PDGF and other growth factors).</span> It is the only matrix-producing cell of the media, so it repairs — and scars — the wall.</li>\n<li><b>Hypertensive muscular artery</b>: thicker media (SMC hypertrophy), thicker fibrotic intima, <b>IEL split/duplicated</b>, narrowed lumen → ↑ resistance (vicious cycle).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>How a plaque builds up</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Endothelial injury (HTN, smoking, diabetes, high LDL)</li>\n<li>LDL enters the intima and is oxidized</li>\n<li>Monocytes enter → macrophages eat lipid → <b>foam cells</b> (fatty streak)</li>\n<li><b>PDGF</b> → SMCs migrate from media to intima and proliferate</li>\n<li>SMCs make collagen → <b>fibrous cap</b> over a lipid core (atheroma)</li>\n</ol>\n<ul>\n<li>Fatty streak → fibrofatty plaque → complicated atheroma. Fatty streaks can appear in childhood and are reversible. Complicated = calcified, ulcerated, ruptured or thrombosed.</li>\n<li>Common sites: <b>abdominal aorta &gt; coronary arteries &gt; popliteal &gt; internal carotid &gt; circle of Willis</b>.</li>\n<li>Thin caps with large lipid cores and many inflammatory cells = <b>vulnerable</b> (rupture-prone); thick collagen-rich caps = stable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>How atherosclerosis breaks down wall layering</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Normal</th><th>In atherosclerosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Endothelium</td><td>Continuous, smooth</td><td>Dysfunctional, eroded or ulcerated → thrombus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Intima</td><td>Thin</td><td>Greatly thickened: lipid core, foam cells, SMCs, collagen cap (largest layer)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IEL</td><td>Intact boundary</td><td>Fragmented; intima–media border lost</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Media</td><td>Organized SMC/elastic layers</td><td>Thinned, atrophic; SMCs lost → wall weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adventitia</td><td>CT, vasa vasorum</td><td>Inflammation, fibrosis, new vessels</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Thick intima → narrowing (angina, claudication). Thin media → aneurysm (abdominal aorta). Cap rupture → thrombosis (MI, stroke).</span></li></ul>\n<h3>Hypertensive arteriolosclerosis</h3>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Hyaline</th><th>Hyperplastic</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Setting</td><td>Benign HTN, diabetes, aging</td><td>Malignant (severe) HTN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Mechanism</td><td>Plasma proteins leak into wall + extra basement membrane</td><td>Concentric SMC layers + reduplicated basement membrane</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Look</td><td>Pink, glassy, homogeneous; few nuclei</td><td>“Onion-skin”, cellular; may show fibrinoid necrosis; lumen almost closed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kidney</td><td>Benign nephrosclerosis</td><td>“Flea-bitten” kidney (petechial hemorrhages)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Atherosclerosis vs hypertensive arteriosclerosis</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Atherosclerosis</th><th>Hypertensive arteriosclerosis</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Vessels</td><td>Elastic + large/medium muscular</td><td>Arterioles and small muscular arteries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pattern</td><td>Focal, eccentric plaque</td><td>Diffuse, concentric thickening</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main layer</td><td>Intima</td><td>Whole wall (media + intima)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key cells/material</td><td>Foam cells, lipid core, cholesterol clefts, fibrous cap</td><td>Hypertrophied SMCs, hyaline material, basement membrane layers</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>IEL / media</td><td>IEL fragmented; media thinned</td><td>IEL duplicated; media thickened</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Stenosis, thrombosis, aneurysm</td><td>Organ ischemia (kidney, brain, retina)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Capillaries (Lecture 3)</h2>\n<h3>Vessel wall overview</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Tunic</th><th>Contents</th><th>Artery vs vein</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Intima</td><td>Endothelium + basal lamina + thin subendothelial CT</td><td>Arteries have a distinct IEL; veins do not</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Media</td><td>Circular smooth muscle + elastic &amp; reticular fibers</td><td>Thickest in arteries; thin in veins; pericytes in capillaries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Adventitia</td><td>Longitudinal collagen + elastic fibers; vasa vasorum, nerves</td><td class=\"hy\">Thickest layer in veins</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Capillaries = intima only</b> (endothelium + basal lamina ± pericytes) — no media or adventitia.</li>\n<li>Flow: arteriole → capillary bed → postcapillary venule → muscular venule → small/medium vein → large vein → heart.</li></ul>\n<h3>Capillaries: the exchange vessels</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Diameter <b>5–10 µm</b> — RBCs pass in single file. Cross-section shows <b>1–3 endothelial nuclei</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Pericytes</b> wrap the endothelium and share its basal lamina.</li>\n<li>Exchange by diffusion, pinocytotic vesicles (transcytosis), fenestrae and intercellular gaps.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pericytes = contractile mesenchymal stem cells → regenerate smooth muscle and fibroblasts after injury. Pericyte loss in diabetes → retinal microaneurysms (diabetic retinopathy).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The three capillary types</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Continuous (somatic)</th><th>Fenestrated (visceral)</th><th>Sinusoidal (discontinuous)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Endothelium</td><td>Unbroken, tight junctions</td><td>Fenestrae (60–80 nm) with thin diaphragm</td><td>Large fenestrae without diaphragms + gaps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basal lamina</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Discontinuous / absent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Diameter</td><td>5–10 µm</td><td>5–10 µm</td><td>30–40 µm, tortuous (slows flow)</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Permeability</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Intermediate</td><td>Highest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Main transport</td><td>Pinocytotic vesicles</td><td>Through fenestrae</td><td>Through gaps</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Classic site</td><td>Muscle, CT, lung, skin, exocrine glands, CNS (BBB)</td><td>Kidney, intestinal villi, endocrine glands, choroid plexus</td><td>Liver, spleen, bone marrow, some endocrine glands</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Memory hook:</span> Continuous → Fenestrated → Sinusoidal = increasing leakiness.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">CNS continuous capillaries form the blood–brain barrier — tight junctions + very few vesicles.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Glomerular capillaries: fenestrae WITHOUT diaphragms + thick shared GBM.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Sinusoids are the most permeable type → whole cells and large proteins can pass. Liver macrophages = Kupffer cells.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Blood–brain barrier</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Components: continuous endothelium, <b>tight junctions</b> sealing clefts, very few pinocytotic vesicles, thick basal lamina + pericytes, <b>astrocyte foot processes</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Lipid-soluble molecules cross; glucose and amino acids need carriers.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">No BBB in circumventricular organs (e.g., area postrema = vomiting centre) — fenestrated capillaries.</span></li>\n<li>Infection, tumour or trauma opens tight junctions → protein leak → <b>vasogenic cerebral edema</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Renal glomerulus</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Filters ~180 L plasma/day but keeps albumin in blood. Three layers:\n<ol><li>Fenestrated endothelium — fenestrae <b>lack diaphragms</b> (70–90 nm)</li>\n<li>GBM — thick, fused basal laminae; <b>negatively charged (heparan sulfate)</b></li>\n<li>Podocyte slit diaphragms between foot processes (<b>nephrin</b>) — final size barrier</li></ol></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Barrier works by size + charge → albumin (negative) is repelled. Damage to GBM charge or podocytes → proteinuria (nephrotic syndrome).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Liver sinusoids</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Discontinuous endothelium, no diaphragms, <b>no basal lamina</b>; <b>space of Disse</b> separates endothelium from hepatocyte microvilli; Kupffer cells line the lumen.</li>\n<li>Plasma bathes hepatocytes directly → secretion of albumin, clotting factors, lipoproteins; Kupffer cells phagocytose old RBCs and bacteria.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Space of Disse holds stellate (Ito) cells — store vitamin A; activated in cirrhosis → fibrosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Spotter strategy: naming the capillary type</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Organ?</b> Brain, muscle, lung → continuous. Kidney, gut, endocrine → fenestrated. Liver, spleen, marrow → sinusoidal.</li>\n<li><b>Lumen size?</b> Single RBC → capillary. Wide, irregular, between plates of cells → sinusoid.</li>\n<li><b>EM endothelium?</b> Unbroken with vesicles → continuous. Pores with thin line across → fenestrated.</li>\n<li><b>EM basal lamina?</b> Continuous → continuous or fenestrated. Broken/absent → sinusoidal.</li>\n</ol>\n<ul><li>Light microscopy cannot separate capillary types — use organ + EM clues. On LM a capillary lumen fits one RBC and its wall is one flattened endothelial nucleus.</li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Venules &amp; veins (Lecture 3)</h2>\n<h3>Veins: general features</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Compared with arteries: thinner wall, wider and often collapsed/irregular lumen, thin media, <b>adventitia thickest</b>, <b>no distinct IEL</b>, valves in limb veins.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Veins are capacitance vessels — hold ~65–70% of blood volume. Low pressure: return depends on valves + skeletal-muscle pump. Vasa vasorum are more frequent in veins (less O₂ in lumen).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Wall changes: venule → large vein</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Vessel</th><th>Size</th><th>Intima</th><th>Media</th><th>Adventitia</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Postcapillary venule</td><td>10–50 µm</td><td>Endothelium + pericytes</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Muscular venule</td><td>50–100 µm</td><td>Endothelium</td><td>1–2 SMC layers</td><td>Thin CT</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Small/medium vein</td><td>0.1–10 mm</td><td>Endothelium; valves</td><td>2–3 thin layers</td><td>Thicker than media</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Large vein</td><td>&gt;10 mm</td><td>Endothelium + CT</td><td>Thin, few layers</td><td>Thickest; longitudinal smooth muscle</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>The wall thickens mainly by growth of the <b>adventitia</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Postcapillary venules</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>10–50 µm; endothelium + basal lamina + pericytes; no true media. Exchange continues here.</li>\n<li><b>Main site of leukocyte exit</b> (margination → rolling → diapedesis; selectins, ICAM-1).</li>\n<li><b>HEVs</b> in lymph nodes: tall cuboidal endothelium → lymphocyte homing.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Histamine, bradykinin and serotonin act mainly on postcapillary venules: endothelial cells contract → gaps open → protein-rich fluid leaks → inflammatory edema.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Small/medium and large veins</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Small/medium</b> (1–10 mm): intima may form valves; media = few small SMC bundles; adventitia well developed, collagenous, thicker than media. Examples: radial, ulnar, popliteal, femoral, great saphenous — usually beside a named artery.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Large veins (SVC, IVC, portal, pulmonary veins): longitudinal smooth muscle in the adventitia is the ID clue. Near the heart, cardiac muscle extends into the wall.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Venous valves</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Two semilunar <b>folds of tunica intima</b>; elastic CT core covered by endothelium on both sides; direct blood toward the heart.</li>\n<li>Numerous in limb veins (esp. lower limb). <b>Absent</b>: venae cavae, CNS veins, portal system, most visceral veins.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Valve failure → reflux → varicose veins. Blood pools in valve pockets → common start point of DVT.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Artery vs companion vein on a slide</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Clue</th><th>Muscular artery</th><th>Companion vein</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Wall vs lumen</td><td>Thick wall, small lumen</td><td>Thin wall, large lumen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lumen shape</td><td>Round, holds shape</td><td>Collapsed/irregular</td></tr>\n<tr><td>IEL</td><td>Wavy, bright pink line</td><td>Absent or indistinct</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Thickest layer</td><td>Tunica media</td><td>Tunica adventitia</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contents</td><td>Often empty</td><td>Often packed with RBCs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Valves</td><td>Never</td><td>May be seen</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Tip:</span> they lie side by side in a neurovascular bundle — compare wall thickness first.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Vascular clinical correlations (Lecture 3)</h2>\n<h3>Inflammatory edema</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Injury/allergen → mast cells release histamine → postcapillary venule endothelium contracts → gaps → protein-rich exudate → swelling.</li>\n<li><b>Exudate</b>: inflammation, ↑ permeability, high protein, cells. <b>Transudate</b>: ↑ hydrostatic or ↓ oncotic pressure (heart failure, cirrhosis), low protein.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Postcapillary venules are the leakiest segment in acute inflammation — same site as leukocyte diapedesis (selectins, ICAM-1). Classic: urticaria, allergic angioedema, bee sting.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Proteinuria</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal: albumin (negative, ~69 kDa) held back; urine protein &lt;150 mg/day.</li>\n<li>Damage: loss of GBM negative charge or effaced podocyte foot processes → albumin escapes; <b>&gt;3.5 g/day = nephrotic range</b>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Nephrotic syndrome: proteinuria + hypoalbuminemia + edema (↓ oncotic pressure) + hyperlipidemia. Minimal change disease — podocyte foot-process effacement seen only on EM.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Varicose veins</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Superficial leg veins (esp. great saphenous); incompetent valves → reflux, high pressure; wall dilated, tortuous, media thinned in places and fibrosed in others.</li>\n<li>Risks: prolonged standing, pregnancy, obesity, age, family history. Symptoms: aching, swelling, stasis dermatitis, venous ulcers.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Root cause = valve failure (folds of tunica intima). Superficial varicosities rarely cause pulmonary emboli.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Deep vein thrombosis</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Virchow’s triad</b>: stasis (immobility, flights, surgery), endothelial injury (trauma, surgery, catheters), hypercoagulability (OCPs, pregnancy, cancer, factor V Leiden).</li>\n<li>Thrombi start in <b>valve pockets of deep calf veins</b>; extend popliteal → femoral → iliac.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Most pulmonary emboli arise from DVT of proximal leg veins. Signs: unilateral calf swelling, pain, warmth. Tests: D-dimer, duplex ultrasound.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Blood: composition &amp; normal values (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Blood = specialized connective tissue (plasma = its ECM); ~5 L in adults (~7–8% body weight); formed elements made in red bone marrow after birth.</li>\n<li>Centrifuged with anticoagulant: <b>plasma ~55%</b> (top), <b>buffy coat &lt;1%</b> (WBCs + platelets), <b>RBCs ~45%</b> (hematocrit; ~45% men, ~40% women).</li>\n<li>Plasma: water, proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen), electrolytes, nutrients. <b>Serum = plasma without clotting factors</b> (what is left after clotting).</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Element</th><th>Normal value</th><th>% of WBCs</th><th>Diameter</th><th>Lifespan</th></tr>\n<tr><td>RBC</td><td>~4–6 million/µL</td><td>—</td><td>7.5 µm</td><td class=\"hy\">~120 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutrophil</td><td>—</td><td>50–70%</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>Hours in blood; 1–4 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eosinophil</td><td>—</td><td>1–4%</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>1–2 weeks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basophil</td><td>—</td><td>0.5–1%</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>Several months</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymphocyte</td><td>—</td><td>20–40%</td><td>6–18 µm</td><td>Hours to many years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Monocyte</td><td>—</td><td>2–8%</td><td>12–20 µm</td><td>1–3 days in blood; months as macrophage</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Platelet</td><td>150,000–400,000/µL</td><td>—</td><td>2–4 µm</td><td class=\"hy\">~10 days</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Total WBC 4,500–11,000/µL · Hb 14–18 g/dL men, 12–16 g/dL women · reticulocytes ~1% · Hct ~45% / ~40%.</li>\n<li>RBCs/platelets have fixed lifespans (no nucleus, no repair); RBCs removed by <b>splenic macrophages</b> (heme → bilirubin, iron recycled); ~1/3 of platelets pooled in spleen. <b>Memory lymphocytes live years</b> (basis of vaccination).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><b class=\"t\">Differential: Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas</b> — Neutrophils 50–70% → Lymphocytes 20–40% → Monocytes 2–8% → Eosinophils 1–4% → Basophils &lt;1%.</div>\n<ul>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Granulocytes (specific granules) = neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils = polymorphonuclear. Agranulocytes = lymphocytes, monocytes = mononuclear.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Most common WBC: neutrophil · Rarest: basophil · Largest: monocyte · Smallest: small lymphocyte.</span> Trap: lymphocytes (not monocytes) are second most common.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Smear preparation and Romanowsky stains (Wright, Giemsa)</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Dye</th><th>Charge</th><th>Stains</th><th>Color</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Eosin</td><td>Acidic</td><td>Basic proteins → acidophilic/eosinophilic</td><td>Pink–orange (RBCs, eosinophil granules)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Methylene blue</td><td>Basic</td><td>Nucleic acids, acid groups → basophilic</td><td>Blue (nuclei, lymphocyte cytoplasm, basophil granules)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Azure</td><td>Basic</td><td>Lysosomes → azurophilic</td><td>Purple (azurophilic granules in all WBCs)</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Read the <b>monolayer near the feathered edge</b>, where RBCs just touch. Size every cell against an RBC (7.5 µm).</li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Erythrocytes &amp; reticulocytes (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>7.5 µm</b> diameter, ~2.6 µm thick at the rim (built-in ruler); <b>biconcave disc</b> → central pallor ≈ 1/3 of diameter; large surface-to-volume ratio; folds through narrow capillaries.</li>\n<li>Eosinophilic (pink–orange) because packed with hemoglobin; <b>no nucleus, no organelles</b> — Hb + glycolytic enzymes only; <b>anaerobic glycolysis only</b> (no mitochondria); HMP shunt makes NADPH (G6PD).</li>\n<li><b>Rouleaux</b>: stacks of coins when plasma fibrinogen/globulins rise → ↑ ESR.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Normocytic = MCV 80–100 fL. Smaller pale cells = microcytic hypochromic; larger cells = macrocytic.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Membrane &amp; hemoglobin</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Spectrin</b> network under the membrane; <b>ankyrin</b> anchors spectrin to <b>band 3</b> (Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchanger, CO₂ transport); band 4.1 + actin; <b>glycophorin</b> carries sialic acid (negative charge).</li>\n<li>Hemoglobin: 4 globins, each with iron-containing heme. <b>HbA (α₂β₂)</b> adult; HbA₂ (α₂δ₂); <b>HbF (α₂γ₂)</b> fetal.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hereditary spherocytosis (ankyrin/spectrin): spherocytes — small, round, no central pallor; ↑ MCHC, splenomegaly, positive osmotic fragility.</span></li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">G6PD deficiency: Heinz bodies and bite cells.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Reticulocytes</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>~1% of RBCs; slightly larger, bluish-grey on Wright stain (<b>polychromasia</b>); <b>supravital stain</b> (new methylene blue, brilliant cresyl blue) shows residual ribosomal RNA as a blue reticulum; mature in 1–2 days.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">↑ Reticulocytes = marrow responding → hemolysis or acute blood loss. ↓ Reticulocytes = marrow failing → iron, B12 or folate deficiency, aplastic anemia.</span></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Leukocytes (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Specific granules</b> only in granulocytes — their color identifies the cell. <b>Azurophilic granules = lysosomes, present in ALL leukocytes.</b></li>\n<li>Granulocytes: lobed nucleus, short life, end cells. Agranulocytes: round/indented nucleus, can divide or differentiate further. All leukocytes work mainly outside blood (diapedesis).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Neutrophil</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>12–15 µm (~2 RBCs); <b>3–5 lobes</b> joined by thin strands, dense dark chromatin; pale pink–lavender cytoplasm with fine granules; 50–70% of WBCs. Females: <b>drumstick (Barr body)</b> appendage.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Pus = dead neutrophils. Neutrophils dominate acute inflammation (first 24–48 h); macrophages follow.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Granule</th><th>Contents</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Specific (secondary) — most numerous, small, pale</td><td>Lactoferrin, lysozyme, alkaline phosphatase, type IV collagenase</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Azurophilic (primary) — lysosomes, purple</td><td>Myeloperoxidase (MPO), defensins, elastase, acid hydrolases (cathepsin G)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tertiary — migration</td><td>Gelatinase, cathepsins, adhesion molecules</td></tr></table>\n<ul>\n<li>Killing: chemotaxis → diapedesis → phagocytosis → <b>respiratory burst</b> (NADPH oxidase → O₂⁻ → H₂O₂) → <b>MPO makes HOCl</b> (bleach).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Chronic granulomatous disease = NADPH oxidase defect → recurrent catalase-positive infections (S. aureus, Aspergillus). Leukocyte adhesion deficiency = no diapedesis → neutrophilia with no pus.</span></li>\n<li><b>Band (stab)</b>: curved horseshoe nucleus, no lobes; normally ≤5% of neutrophils; ↑ bands = marrow releasing cells early.</li>\n<li><b>Hypersegmented</b>: ≥6 lobes (or &gt;5% with 5 lobes); defective DNA synthesis → <b>B12/folate deficiency</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Eosinophil</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>12–15 µm; <b>bilobed (“spectacles”)</b> nucleus; large, refractile, uniform <b>orange-red</b> granules.</li>\n<li>Granules: <b>major basic protein</b> (crystalloid core, toxic to helminths), eosinophil peroxidase, cationic protein, neurotoxin, <b>histaminase</b>.</li>\n<li>Function: kill helminths (antibody-dependent), damp allergic reactions, phagocytose Ag–Ab complexes.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Eosinophilia: parasites (helminths), asthma/allergy, drug reactions, adrenal insufficiency. Eosinopenia: corticosteroids, Cushing syndrome.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Basophil</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>12–15 µm; rarest (&lt;1%); bilobed/S-shaped nucleus <b>obscured by coarse dark blue-purple granules</b> (metachromatic — heparin).</li>\n<li>Contents: <b>heparin</b>, <b>histamine</b>, leukotrienes, eosinophil chemotactic factor. <b>FcεRI (IgE) receptors</b> → degranulation in allergy/anaphylaxis (type I hypersensitivity).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Basophilia is uncommon and points to a myeloproliferative disorder, classically CML. Basophil ≈ mast cell in function, but mast cells live in tissue (round nucleus, separate lineage).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Lymphocyte</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Small (6–8 µm, <b>≈ RBC size</b>, most common) and large (up to 18 µm; activated cells, NK cells). Round, slightly indented, dense dark nucleus filling the cell; thin rim of <b>sky-blue</b> cytoplasm (free ribosomes).</li>\n<li>B, T and NK cells look identical on a smear (need CD markers). Recirculate between blood and lymphoid tissue.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Atypical (reactive) lymphocytes = infectious mononucleosis (EBV). Lymphocytosis: viral infections, pertussis, CLL (smudge cells).</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Monocyte</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>12–20 µm, <b>largest WBC</b>; kidney/horseshoe-shaped, eccentric nucleus with <b>lacy, pale chromatin</b>; abundant grey-blue <b>“ground glass”</b> cytoplasm, fine azurophilic granules, vacuoles. 2–8%; 1–3 days in blood → tissue macrophages.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Monocytosis: chronic infection (tuberculosis), recovery from infection.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Lymphocyte vs monocyte</th><th>Lymphocyte</th><th>Monocyte</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td>6–8 µm small (≈ RBC); up to 18 µm</td><td>12–20 µm, largest</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nucleus</td><td>Round, slightly indented</td><td>Kidney/horseshoe, eccentric</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chromatin</td><td>Dense, dark, clumped</td><td>Lacy, pale, delicate</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cytoplasm</td><td>Thin rim, clear sky-blue</td><td>Abundant grey-blue ground glass, vacuoles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>N:C ratio</td><td>High</td><td>Lower</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Mononuclear phagocyte system</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Macrophage</th><th>Location</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Kupffer cells</td><td>Liver sinusoids</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alveolar (dust) cells</td><td>Lung</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Osteoclasts</td><td>Bone (multinucleated)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Microglia</td><td>CNS (yolk-sac origin)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Langerhans cells</td><td>Epidermis (antigen-presenting dendritic cells)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Splenic &amp; nodal macrophages</td><td>Remove old RBCs</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Functions: phagocytosis, antigen presentation, cytokines (IL-1, TNF), recycling iron from old RBCs.</li></ul>\n\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Platelets (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Anucleate <b>fragments of megakaryocyte cytoplasm</b>; 2–4 µm biconvex discs; ~10-day lifespan; 150,000–400,000/µL; ~1/3 stored in spleen.</li>\n<li><b>Hyalomere</b> = pale-blue periphery with a marginal ring of microtubules (+ actin). <b>Granulomere</b> = central purple zone with granules.</li>\n<li>Megakaryocyte = giant polyploid marrow cell extending proplatelets into sinusoids; <b>thrombopoietin from the liver</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Granule</th><th>Key contents</th><th>Role</th></tr>\n<tr><td>α granules</td><td>Fibrinogen, vWF, factor V, PDGF, platelet factor 4</td><td>Clotting, repair</td></tr>\n<tr><td>δ (dense) granules</td><td>ADP, ATP, Ca²⁺, serotonin</td><td>Recruit platelets, vasoconstriction</td></tr>\n<tr><td>λ granules</td><td>Lysosomal enzymes</td><td>Clot resorption</td></tr></table>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Adhesion</b>: GPIb ↔ vWF on exposed collagen</li>\n<li><b>Activation</b>: granule release; TXA₂ made</li>\n<li><b>Aggregation</b>: GPIIb/IIIa ↔ fibrinogen</li>\n<li><b>Retraction &amp; repair</b>: actin–myosin; PDGF</li>\n</ol>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Bernard–Soulier: no GPIb → large platelets. Glanzmann: no GPIIb/IIIa. Aspirin blocks TXA₂ (COX) for the platelet’s whole life.</span></li></ul>\n<h3>One-page smear table</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Cell</th><th>Size</th><th>Nucleus</th><th>Cytoplasm / granules</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Erythrocyte</td><td>7.5 µm</td><td>None</td><td>Pink–orange, central pallor 1/3</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutrophil</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>3–5 lobes, dense</td><td>Pale lavender, fine granules</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Eosinophil</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>Bilobed</td><td>Large, uniform orange-red granules</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Basophil</td><td>12–15 µm</td><td>Bilobed/S, obscured</td><td>Coarse dark blue-purple granules</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymphocyte</td><td>6–8 µm (≈ RBC)</td><td>Round, dense, fills cell</td><td>Thin rim, sky-blue</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Monocyte</td><td>12–20 µm</td><td>Kidney/horseshoe, lacy</td><td>Grey-blue ground glass, vacuoles</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Platelet</td><td>2–4 µm</td><td>None (fragment)</td><td>Pale rim, purple granular centre</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"mn\">Spotting order:</span> size vs nearby RBCs → nuclear shape &amp; lobes → granule color → cytoplasm color &amp; amount.</p>\n\n<h2 id=\"g12\">12. Interpreting the CBC (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>-penia</b> = too few: neutropenia, lymphopenia, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia (all lines ↓).</li>\n<li><b>-philia</b> = too many granulocytes: neutrophilia, eosinophilia, basophilia.</li>\n<li><b>-cytosis</b> = too many other cells: leukocytosis, lymphocytosis, monocytosis, thrombocytosis.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Caution: anisocytosis (size variation), poikilocytosis (shape variation) and spherocytosis describe RBC morphology, not numbers. Erythrocytosis/polycythemia = ↑ RBC mass.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th>Change</th><th>Classic causes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutrophilia</td><td>Acute bacterial infection, tissue necrosis (MI), corticosteroids, stress</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutropenia</td><td>Chemotherapy, aplastic anemia, some viral infections, drugs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eosinophilia</td><td>Helminths, asthma/allergy, drug reactions, adrenal insufficiency</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Basophilia</td><td>CML, other myeloproliferative disorders</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymphocytosis</td><td>Viral infections (EBV), pertussis, CLL</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Lymphopenia</td><td>HIV, corticosteroids, DiGeorge syndrome</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Monocytosis</td><td>Chronic infection (TB), recovery from infection</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li><b>Steroids: ↑ neutrophils (demargination), ↓ eosinophils, ↓ lymphocytes.</b></li></ul>\n<h3>Left vs right shift</h3>\n<p>Maturation: myeloblast → promyelocyte → myelocyte → metamyelocyte → band → segmented → hypersegmented.</p>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>Left shift</th><th>Right shift</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Smear</td><td>↑ Bands and immature granulocytes (&gt;10% bands)</td><td>↑ Hypersegmented neutrophils (≥6 lobes)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Means</td><td>Marrow releasing cells early</td><td>Impaired DNA synthesis in precursors</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Causes</td><td>Acute bacterial infection, sepsis, inflammation</td><td>B12 or folate deficiency (megaloblastic anemia)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usually with</td><td>Neutrophilia, toxic granulation</td><td>Macrocytic RBCs (MCV &gt;100 fL)</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g13\">13. Anemias &amp; RBC shapes (Lecture 4)</h2>\n<p>Anemia = ↓ Hb, Hct or RBC mass. First split by size (MCV) and color (MCH/MCHC); for normocytic, the reticulocyte count is the next branch point.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Microcytic hypochromic (MCV &lt;80)</th><th>Normocytic (80–100)</th><th>Macrocytic (&gt;100)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Iron deficiency (most common), thalassemia, anemia of chronic disease (late), lead poisoning, sideroblastic <span class=\"mn\">(TAILS)</span></td><td>Acute blood loss, hemolytic anemias, aplastic anemia, CKD (↓ EPO), anemia of chronic disease (early)</td><td>Megaloblastic: B12/folate (hypersegmented neutrophils). Non-megaloblastic: alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism, reticulocytosis</td></tr></table>\n<h3>By cause</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Cause</th><th>Examples</th><th>Retics</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Blood loss</td><td>Acute (trauma, surgery → normocytic); chronic (menorrhagia, GI bleed, hookworm → iron deficiency)</td><td>↑ after 2–3 days</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↓ Production</td><td>Iron, B12, folate; aplastic anemia (drugs, radiation, parvovirus B19); marrow infiltration; CKD (↓ EPO); chronic disease</td><td>↓</td></tr>\n<tr><td>↑ Destruction (hemolysis)</td><td>Intrinsic: membrane (HS, PNH), enzyme (G6PD, pyruvate kinase), Hb (sickle cell, HbC, thalassemia). Extrinsic: autoimmune, microangiopathic (DIC, TTP/HUS), prosthetic valves, malaria</td><td>↑, ↑ bilirubin, ↑ LDH</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Microcytic subdivisions</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Subtype</th><th>Defect</th><th>Smear</th><th>Key labs</th></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Iron deficiency</td><td>↓ iron → ↓ heme</td><td>Pale cells, pencil cells, ↑ RDW</td><td>↓ ferritin, ↑ TIBC, ↓ serum iron</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Anemia of chronic disease</td><td>Hepcidin (IL-6) traps iron in macrophages</td><td>Normocytic → microcytic</td><td>↑ ferritin, ↓ TIBC, ↓ serum iron</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Thalassemia (α, β)</td><td>↓ globin chain synthesis</td><td>Target cells; RBC count normal/high</td><td>Normal iron; Hb electrophoresis (β: ↑ HbA₂, HbF)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Normocytic</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Non-hemolytic (retics ↓)</b>: aplastic anemia (pancytopenia, hypocellular fatty marrow), CKD (↓ EPO; burr cells), chronic disease (early), acute blood loss (first hours–days).</li>\n<li><b>Intrinsic hemolytic</b>: HS (spherocytes, ↑ MCHC); PNH (CD55/59 loss → complement lysis); G6PD (Heinz bodies, bite cells); pyruvate kinase deficiency; sickle cell; HbC (crystals).</li>\n<li><b>Extrinsic hemolytic</b>: autoimmune (warm IgG → spherocytes; cold IgM; Coombs +); microangiopathic (DIC, TTP/HUS → schistocytes); macroangiopathic (prosthetic valves, aortic stenosis); malaria, Babesia.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Hemolysis labs: ↑ reticulocytes, ↑ unconjugated bilirubin, ↑ LDH, ↓ haptoglobin. Intravascular → hemoglobinuria; extravascular (spleen) → splenomegaly, jaundice.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Macrocytic</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Megaloblastic</b> (impaired DNA synthesis): B12 deficiency (pernicious anemia, gastrectomy, ileal disease, vegans, fish tapeworm; ↑ MMA; neuro signs), folate deficiency (poor diet, alcoholism, pregnancy, methotrexate; normal MMA; no neuro signs), orotic aciduria (UMP synthase defect; treat with uridine).</li>\n<li><b>Non-megaloblastic</b>: alcohol, liver disease (extra membrane lipid; target cells), hypothyroidism, reticulocytosis (young RBCs are larger).</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Macrocytosis + hypersegmented neutrophils = megaloblastic. Macrocytosis with normal neutrophils = look for alcohol, liver disease, hypothyroidism or reticulocytosis.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Iron deficiency anemia</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Smear: microcytic, hypochromic (pallor &gt;1/3), anisocytosis, pencil cells. Indices: ↓ MCV, ↓ MCH, ↑ RDW.</li>\n<li>Iron studies: <b>↓ ferritin (most specific)</b>, ↓ serum iron, ↑ TIBC, ↓ % saturation.</li>\n<li>Causes: chronic blood loss (menorrhagia, GI bleeding — think colon cancer in older men/postmenopausal women), hookworm, poor intake, pregnancy. Signs: pallor, fatigue, <b>koilonychia</b>, pica.</li>\n<li><span class=\"hy\">Plummer–Vinson syndrome: iron deficiency anemia + esophageal webs + atrophic glossitis. Low ferritin = iron deficiency.</span></li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Megaloblastic anemia</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Impaired DNA synthesis → <b>nucleus matures slower than cytoplasm</b> (nuclear–cytoplasmic asynchrony) → megaloblasts in marrow, <b>macro-ovalocytes</b> in blood, hypersegmented neutrophils; all dividing cells affected (pancytopenia, glossitis).</li>\n<li>B12 absorption: intrinsic factor from <b>gastric parietal cells</b> → absorbed in <b>terminal ileum</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<table><tr><th></th><th>B12 deficiency</th><th>Folate deficiency</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Causes</td><td>Pernicious anemia, gastrectomy, ileal disease, strict vegan, fish tapeworm</td><td>Poor diet, alcoholism, pregnancy, methotrexate</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neuro signs</td><td>Yes (subacute combined degeneration)</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Labs</td><td>↑ homocysteine, ↑ methylmalonic acid</td><td>↑ homocysteine, normal MMA</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Iron deficiency vs megaloblastic</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Iron deficiency</th><th>Megaloblastic</th></tr>\n<tr><td>RBC size</td><td>Microcytic (&lt;80)</td><td>Macrocytic (&gt;100)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>RBC color</td><td>Hypochromic, wide pallor</td><td>Normochromic, oval macrocytes</td></tr>\n<tr class=\"hy\"><td>Neutrophils</td><td>Normal</td><td>Hypersegmented (right shift)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Defect</td><td>↓ heme → ↓ Hb synthesis</td><td>↓ DNA synthesis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Key labs</td><td>↓ ferritin, ↑ TIBC, ↑ RDW</td><td>↓ B12 or folate, ↑ homocysteine (± MMA)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reticulocytes</td><td>Low</td><td>Low</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Classic patient</td><td>Menstruating woman, GI bleed, hookworm</td><td>Vegan/pernicious anemia (B12), alcoholic (folate)</td></tr></table>\n<h3>RBC shapes NBME likes to show</h3>\n<table><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Look</th><th>Think</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Spherocytes</td><td>Small, round, no pallor</td><td>Hereditary spherocytosis, autoimmune hemolysis</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Schistocytes (helmet cells)</td><td>Fragments</td><td>DIC, TTP/HUS, prosthetic valves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Elliptocytes</td><td>Oval</td><td>Hereditary elliptocytosis (spectrin)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Acanthocytes</td><td>Irregular spikes</td><td>Abetalipoproteinemia, liver disease</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Target cells</td><td>Central dot of Hb</td><td>Thalassemia, liver disease, HbC</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bite cells / Heinz bodies</td><td>Bitten edge / inclusions</td><td>G6PD deficiency</td></tr></table>\n\n<h2 id=\"g14\">14. Rapid-fire NBME pearls</h2>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"hy\">Smooth muscle: Ca²⁺ → calmodulin → MLCK (thick-filament regulation); dense bodies = Z disc; caveolae = T-tubules; hypertrophy + hyperplasia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Cardiac: gap junctions (lateral part of intercalated disc) = functional syncytium; T-tubules at Z line → diads; no regeneration → MI heals by scar.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Purkinje fibers = large, pale, glycogen-rich modified cardiac muscle under the endocardium (not neurons).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Leiomyoma = most common uterine tumor; desmin/SMA positive.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Aorta = 40–70 elastic lamellae, indistinct IEL; muscular artery = crisp wavy IEL, up to 40 SMC layers; arteriole = 1–2 SMC layers.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Medial SMC (no fibroblasts in media) makes elastin/collagen; migrates to intima via PDGF to form the fibrous cap.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Atherosclerosis = intima; hyaline arteriolosclerosis = benign HTN/diabetes; hyperplastic (onion-skin) = malignant HTN; Mönckeberg = media, lumen not narrowed.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Continuous (BBB, muscle) &lt; fenestrated (kidney, gut, endocrine, area postrema) &lt; sinusoidal (liver, spleen, marrow) in leakiness.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Glomerulus: fenestrae without diaphragms + negatively charged GBM + podocyte slits; loss → nephrotic proteinuria.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Postcapillary venules: histamine leak + leukocyte diapedesis; HEVs in lymph nodes.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Vein: adventitia thickest; valves = folds of intima; large veins have longitudinal SMC in adventitia.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">DVT starts in valve pockets of deep calf veins; most PEs come from proximal leg DVT.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Neutrophil most common; lymphocyte ≈ RBC size; monocyte largest; basophil rarest.</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Orange granules = eosinophil (MBP kills helminths); dark granules hiding the nucleus = basophil (CML).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Left shift = bands (bacterial infection); right shift = hypersegmented (B12/folate).</li>\n<li class=\"hy\">Iron deficiency: ↓ ferritin, ↑ TIBC, microcytic hypochromic; B12 vs folate: MMA and neuro signs.</li>\n<li>RBC 120 days, platelet 10 days; reticulocytes ~1% (↑ = hemolysis/bleeding, ↓ = production failure).</li>\n<li>Bernard–Soulier (no GPIb), Glanzmann (no GPIIb/IIIa), aspirin blocks TXA₂ for platelet life.</li>\n</ul>", "crs-anatomy": "<h2 id=\"g1\">1. The Thorax: Thoracic Cage, Apertures, Sternum, Ribs and Vertebrae</h2>\n<p>The thorax is the upper part of the trunk, running from the neck above to the abdomen below. Its skeleton, the thoracic cage, is an elastic frame made of bone and cartilage. It protects the heart and lungs, gives attachment to muscles, and, most importantly, it can change its volume. When the cage gets bigger, the pressure inside the chest drops and air rushes in (inspiration). When it gets smaller, pressure rises and air is pushed out (expiration).</p>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> The whole design of the chest wall is about changing intrathoracic pressure. Bigger cage → lower pressure → air in. Smaller cage → higher pressure → air out.</div>\n<h3>The thoracic cage</h3>\n<p>The cage has three walls:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Front (anterior):</b> the sternum (breastbone).</li><li><b>Back (posterior):</b> the 12 thoracic vertebrae and the intervertebral discs between them.</li><li><b>Sides (lateral):</b> the 12 pairs of ribs and their costal cartilages.</li></ul>\n<h3>The two openings of the thorax</h3>\n<h4>Superior thoracic aperture (the &quot;thoracic inlet&quot;)</h4>\n<p>This is the small, kidney-shaped opening at the top of the cage, through which the neck communicates with the chest. Its boundaries are:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Behind:</b> the body of the first thoracic vertebra (T1).</li><li><b>In front:</b> the upper border of the manubrium of the sternum (the jugular notch).</li><li><b>On each side:</b> the first rib and its costal cartilage.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box wo\"><b class=\"t\">Watch out.</b> Naming trap. Anatomists call the top opening the superior thoracic aperture or thoracic inlet. Clinicians call this same top opening the &quot;thoracic outlet&quot;, which is why &quot;thoracic outlet syndrome&quot; is a problem at the top of the chest (first rib and clavicle), not at the diaphragm.</div>\n<h4>Inferior thoracic aperture (the anatomical &quot;thoracic outlet&quot;)</h4>\n<p>This is the much larger opening at the bottom of the cage. It is closed by the diaphragm, which separates the thoracic cavity above from the abdominal cavity below. Its boundaries are:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Behind:</b> the body of the twelfth thoracic vertebra (T12).</li><li><b>Behind and to the side:</b> the 11th and 12th ribs.</li><li><b>In front and to the side:</b> the joined costal cartilages of ribs 7 to 10, which together form the costal margin.</li><li><b>In front:</b> the xiphisternal joint.</li></ul>\n<h3>The sternum</h3>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig01.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The sternum in side view and the vertebral levels that line up with its landmarks.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The sternum is a flat bone in the midline of the front of the chest. It has three parts:</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Manubrium</span>: the top part, lying opposite the third and fourth thoracic vertebrae. Its upper border has the jugular (suprasternal) notch, which you can feel at the base of the neck.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Body of the sternum</span>: the long middle part, lying opposite the fifth to ninth thoracic vertebrae. Its sides articulate with the costal cartilages of ribs 2 to 7. Its lower end meets the xiphoid process at the xiphisternal joint.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Xiphoid process</span>: the small, flat bottom tip. It starts as cartilage and fuses with the body of the sternum after middle age (about 40 years). Its lower end gives attachment to the linea alba of the abdominal wall.</li></ul>\n<h4>The sternal angle (angle of Louis)</h4>\n<p>The sternal angle is the slight ridge you can feel where the manubrium meets the body of the sternum. <span class=\"hy\">It is the single most important surface landmark in the chest</span>, because a horizontal plane through it (the transverse thoracic plane) marks many events at the same level:</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The second ribs (second costal cartilages) articulate with the sternum here.</span> This is how you count ribs and intercostal spaces on a patient: find the angle, slide sideways onto the second rib, and count down.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">The arch of the aorta begins and ends at this level.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The trachea divides into the right and left main bronchi at the carina.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The plane passes backward through the intervertebral disc between T4 and T5.</span></li><li>It separates the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum.</li><li>The azygos vein arches over the right lung root to join the superior vena cava.</li><li>The pulmonary trunk divides into right and left pulmonary arteries just below this plane.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;Louis lies at 2 and 4/5.&quot; Rib 2 at the side, T4/T5 at the back, and in between: aortic arch starts and stops, the trachea splits, and the mediastinum is divided into superior and inferior parts.</div>\n<h3>Ribs and costal cartilages</h3>\n<h4>Classification by front attachment</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Group</th><th>Ribs</th><th>How they reach the sternum</th></tr><tr><td>True ribs (vertebrosternal)</td><td>1 to 7</td><td>Directly, each through its own costal cartilage</td></tr><tr><td>False ribs (vertebrochondral)</td><td>8 to 10</td><td>Indirectly, each cartilage joins the cartilage above</td></tr><tr><td>Floating ribs (free ribs)</td><td>11 and 12</td><td>No front attachment; they end in the abdominal wall muscles</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Typical ribs (ribs 3 to 9)</h4>\n<p>A typical rib has these parts, from back to front:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Head:</b> has two facets separated by a crest. The lower facet articulates with the body of its own vertebra (for example, rib 6 with T6). The upper facet articulates with the body of the vertebra above (T5).</li><li><b>Neck:</b> the short part between head and tubercle.</li><li><b>Tubercle:</b> has a facet that articulates with the transverse process of its own vertebra.</li><li><b>Body (shaft):</b> thin, flat and curved. Just beyond the tubercle it bends sharply forward at the <b>angle</b>, which is the weakest point of the rib.</li><li><b>Costal groove:</b> a groove on the inner, lower border of the shaft. It shelters the intercostal vein, artery and nerve.</li></ul>\n<h4>Atypical ribs (1, 2, 10, 11 and 12)</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Rib 1:</b> the broadest, shortest and most sharply curved rib. It is flat, with an upper and lower surface instead of inner and outer. Its head has only one facet (for T1). Its upper surface has two shallow grooves, one for the subclavian vein in front and one for the subclavian artery behind, separated by the scalene tubercle. Its tubercle and angle lie at the same point.</li><li><b>Rib 2:</b> thinner and longer than rib 1. Its distinguishing feature is a rough area on its upper surface, the tuberosity for serratus anterior, where part of that muscle starts.</li><li><b>Ribs 10, 11 and 12:</b> each head has only one facet, for its own vertebra.</li><li><b>Ribs 11 and 12:</b> short, with no neck and no tubercle.</li></ul>\n<h3>Thoracic vertebrae</h3>\n<p>There are 12 thoracic vertebrae. Their features are designed for the ribs:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Body:</b> heart-shaped, with facets (costal facets) on its sides for the heads of the ribs.</li><li><b>Spinous process:</b> long and slanted steeply downward, overlapping the vertebra below.</li><li><b>Transverse process:</b> carries a facet for the tubercle of the rib (the costotransverse joint). The lower thoracic vertebrae (usually T11 and T12) have no such facet.</li><li><b>Pedicle:</b> connects the body to the transverse process.</li><li><b>Lamina:</b> connects the transverse process to the spinous process.</li></ul>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Rib fracture.</b> <span class=\"hy\">A rib usually breaks just in front of its angle, which is its weakest point. The middle ribs are injured most often.</span> Rib 1 is protected by the clavicle and ribs 11 and 12 are mobile, so they break less often. A broken end can puncture internal organs: lungs and pleura (pneumothorax), or spleen and liver with the lower ribs.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Flail chest.</b> Several adjacent ribs each broken in two or more places leave a loose segment of chest wall. This segment moves the opposite way to the rest of the chest (paradoxical movement): it is sucked <span class=\"hy\">inward on inspiration and pushed outward on expiration</span>. It is very painful, impairs ventilation and lowers blood oxygen. Treatment may include fixing the loose segment with plates or wires.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Cervical rib.</b> About 1% of people have an extra rib that articulates with the seventh cervical vertebra (C7). It can press on the <span class=\"hy\">lower trunk of the brachial plexus (spinal nerves C8 and T1)</span>, causing tingling and numbness along the medial (inner) border of the forearm and hand. It can also compress the subclavian artery, causing pain in the arm muscles from poor blood supply (ischemic pain). A lumbar rib is less common; its main problem is confusion when counting vertebrae on imaging.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Thoracic outlet syndrome.</b> Compression of the nerves and vessels passing through the top of the chest. One type is costoclavicular syndrome: the subclavian artery and brachial plexus are <span class=\"hy\">squeezed between the first rib and the clavicle</span>. <span class=\"hy\">A sign is a weaker radial pulse when the shoulder is pulled back and down.</span></div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Thoracotomy (surgical opening of the chest to reach the pleural cavity).</b> In an anterior thoracotomy, H-shaped incisions are made through the perichondrium (the covering of the costal cartilage) so pieces of cartilage can be removed. A posterolateral thoracotomy goes through the 5th to 7th intercostal spaces. A median sternotomy, splitting the sternum down the middle, is used to reach the mediastinum, for example in coronary artery bypass graft surgery. A lateral thoracotomy is used to reach the pulmonary cavity.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Rib dislocation and separation.</b> &quot;Slipping rib syndrome&quot; is dislocation of a rib from its sternocostal joint, <span class=\"hy\">usually involving ribs 8 to 10</span>. Rib separation is a dislocation at the costochondral junction (between rib and cartilage). Both cause pain that is worse with deep breathing.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Sternal fracture.</b> Not common, but dangerous because of what lies behind: bruising of the heart muscle (<span class=\"hy\">myocardial contusion</span>), cardiac rupture, cardiac tamponade or lung injury.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Paralysis of the diaphragm.</b> Usually from injury to the phrenic nerve. It is recognised by paradoxical movement: the paralysed half rises instead of descending during inspiration, because it is pushed up by abdominal pressure.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g2\">2. Intercostal Spaces</h2>\n<h3>What an intercostal space is</h3>\n<p>An intercostal space is the gap between two neighbouring ribs (and their costal cartilages). Each space is named after the rib that forms its upper border, so the 4th intercostal space lies below the 4th rib.</p>\n<ul><li>There are 11 intercostal spaces on each side.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">The space below the 12th rib does not lie between two ribs, so it is called the subcostal space.</span></li><li>At the back, all 11 spaces are bounded by ribs. At the front, only the upper 9 are closed in by costal cartilages; the 10th and 11th open into the abdominal wall because ribs 11 and 12 are floating.</li></ul>\n<h3>Contents of each space</h3>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">Each space contains three layers of intercostal muscle (external, internal and innermost)</span>, together with an intercostal nerve, an intercostal artery (posterior and anterior) and an intercostal vein (posterior and anterior). The transversus thoracis also lies in the deepest layer at the front.</p>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig02.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. Section through an intercostal space: three muscle layers, the vein, artery and nerve in the costal groove, and the safe needle path above the rib below.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The intercostal vein, artery and nerve run together as a neurovascular bundle between the internal intercostal and innermost intercostal layers (the middle and deepest layers). They shelter in the costal groove on the lower border of the rib above.</p>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">From above downward the order is: intercostal Vein, Artery, Nerve (VAN).</span></p>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic. VAN</b> from top to bottom. The vein is highest, tucked deepest in the costal groove; <span class=\"hy\">the nerve is lowest and least protected</span>, which is why the nerve is the structure most at risk if a needle is placed just under a rib.</div>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> Because the main bundle runs along the <b>lower</b> border of each rib, needles and chest drains go in just <b>above the upper border of the rib below</b>, in the lower part of the space.</div>\n<h3>Muscles of the thoracic wall</h3>\n<h4>Summary</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Muscle</th><th>Fibre direction</th><th>Main action</th></tr><tr><td>External intercostal</td><td>Downward and forward (&quot;hands in pockets&quot;)</td><td>Elevates the ribs (inspiration)</td></tr><tr><td>Internal intercostal</td><td>Downward and backward, at right angles to the external</td><td>Depresses the ribs (forced expiration)</td></tr><tr><td>Innermost intercostal</td><td>Same as the internal</td><td>Acts with the internal intercostals</td></tr><tr><td>Subcostals</td><td>Same as the internal, but span two or three ribs</td><td>May depress the ribs</td></tr><tr><td>Transversus thoracis</td><td>Upward and outward from the lower sternum</td><td>Pulls costal cartilages down (weak expiration)</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">External intercostal: origin from the lower border of the rib above, insertion into the upper border of the rib below; it moves the ribs upward and elevates the cage during inspiration.</span> At the front it is replaced by the external intercostal membrane. The internal intercostal runs from the upper border of each rib up to the rib above, at right angles to the external, and is replaced at the back by the internal intercostal membrane. The innermost intercostal is the faintest layer, best seen on the side wall, and the neurovascular bundle runs between it and the internal intercostal. The subcostals lie in the same plane at the back near the rib angles and each one spans two or three ribs.</p>\n<h4>Transversus thoracis</h4>\n<ul><li>Lies on the inner surface of the front chest wall, in the same plane as the innermost intercostals.</li><li>Origin: the back of the xiphoid process and the lower part of the body of the sternum.</li><li>Insertion: its fibres pass upward and outward to the lower borders of the costal cartilages of ribs 2 to 6.</li><li>Action: probably pulls those cartilages downward (weak expiration).</li><li>Nerve supply: intercostal nerves T2 to T6.</li></ul>\n<h3>Arteries of the thoracic wall</h3>\n<p>The chest wall gets blood from two main sources:</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">1. The thoracic aorta, through the posterior intercostal and subcostal arteries.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">2. The subclavian artery, through the internal thoracic and supreme (superior) intercostal arteries.</span></li></ul>\n<h4>Posterior intercostal arteries</h4>\n<ul><li>Each space has <b>one</b> posterior intercostal artery, which runs in the costal groove.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Spaces 1 and 2: from the supreme (superior) intercostal artery</span>, a branch of the costocervical trunk of the subclavian artery.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Spaces 3 to 11, plus the subcostal artery: directly from the descending thoracic aorta.</span></li></ul>\n<h4>Anterior intercostal arteries</h4>\n<ul><li>Each of the upper spaces has <b>two</b> small anterior intercostal arteries, one near the upper border and one near the lower border of the space.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Spaces 1 to 6: from the internal thoracic artery</span> (the old name is internal mammary artery), a branch of the subclavian.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Spaces 7 to 9: from the musculophrenic artery</span>, one of the two terminal branches of the internal thoracic artery.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Spaces 10 and 11 have no anterior intercostal arteries</span>; they are supplied only by the posterior arteries.</li><li>Anterior and posterior arteries join (anastomose) within each space.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key.</b> These front-to-back connections are why, in <b>coarctation of the aorta</b>, blood can bypass the narrowing: subclavian artery → internal thoracic artery → anterior intercostal arteries → posterior intercostal arteries → descending aorta. The enlarged, tortuous posterior intercostal arteries erode the lower borders of the ribs, <span class=\"hy\">producing </span><b><span class=\"hy\">rib notching</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> on X-ray</span>.</div>\n<h3>Veins of the thoracic wall</h3>\n<ul><li>On each side there are 11 posterior intercostal veins and one subcostal vein.</li><li>The anterior intercostal veins drain into the internal thoracic and musculophrenic veins.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Most posterior intercostal veins (spaces 4 to 11) end in the azygos and hemiazygos system, which carries the blood to the superior vena cava.</span></li></ul>\n<p>More detail on the posterior veins:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Space 1:</b> the first posterior intercostal vein drains into the brachiocephalic (or vertebral) vein of its own side.</li><li><b>Spaces 2 and 3 (sometimes 4):</b> join to form the superior intercostal vein. On the right, it drains into the azygos vein. On the left, <span class=\"hy\">the left superior intercostal vein crosses the aortic arch to drain into the left brachiocephalic vein</span>.</li><li><b>Right side, spaces 4 to 11:</b> drain into the azygos vein.</li><li><b>Left side, spaces 4 to 8:</b> drain into the accessory hemiazygos vein.</li><li><b>Left side, spaces 9 to 11 and the subcostal vein:</b> drain into the hemiazygos vein.</li><li>The hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins cross the midline (behind the aorta) to join the azygos vein, <span class=\"hy\">which arches over the root of the right lung at T4 to enter the superior vena cava</span>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nerve supply</h3>\n<ul><li>The intercostal muscles are supplied by the intercostal nerve of the same space.</li><li>The intercostal nerves are the anterior rami of the first 11 thoracic spinal nerves (T1 to T11).</li><li>The anterior ramus of T12 lies below the 12th rib and runs into the abdominal wall as the subcostal nerve.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Intercostal nerves 7 to 11 continue past the costal margin</span> to supply the skin and muscles of the abdominal wall (thoracoabdominal nerves).</li></ul>\n<h4>Branches of a typical intercostal nerve</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">1. Lateral cutaneous branch: reaches the skin on the side of the chest and divides into an anterior and a posterior branch.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">2. Anterior cutaneous branch: the end of the main nerve, reaching the skin near the midline; it divides into a medial and a lateral branch.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">3. Collateral branch: runs forward below the main nerve, along the upper border of the rib below.</span></li><li>Also: muscular branches to the intercostal muscles and sensory branches to the costal parietal pleura.</li></ul>\n<h3>Dermatomes of the chest</h3>\n<p>A dermatome is the strip of skin supplied by one spinal nerve.</p>\n<ul><li>C5 supplies the skin at the level of the clavicles.</li><li>Just below the C5 dermatome on the front of the chest comes the T1 dermatome. This jump happens because <span class=\"hy\">dermatomes C6 to C8 are carried out into the upper limb.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">T4 includes the nipple.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">T10 includes the umbilicus.</span></li><li>T12 lies just above the inguinal ligament; L1 covers the groin.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;T4 at the teat-pore, T10 at the belly button (be-L-LY butt-TEN).&quot;</div>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Herpes zoster (shingles).</b> A viral disease of the spinal (dorsal root) ganglia caused by reactivation of the varicella-zoster virus, the chickenpox virus, which has been dormant in the ganglion. It produces a sharp, burning pain followed by a band-shaped rash of blisters that <span class=\"hy\">follows exactly one dermatome on one side of the body, stopping at the midline</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Intercostal nerve block.</b> Local anaesthetic is injected around the intercostal nerve trunk and its collateral branches. Because each area of skin overlaps with the nerves above and below, usually two or more neighbouring nerves must be blocked. <span class=\"hy\">Indications: repairing cuts (lacerations) of the chest and abdominal walls, relieving the pain of rib fractures, and allowing pain-free breathing movements.</span></div>\n<div class=\"box ep\"><b class=\"t\">Exam pearl.</b> Inspiration uses the diaphragm (main muscle) plus the external intercostals. <span class=\"hy\">Quiet expiration is passive (elastic recoil of the lungs).</span> Forced expiration uses the internal intercostals and abdominal wall muscles.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g3\">3. Mediastinum</h2>\n<h3>What the mediastinum is</h3>\n<p>The mediastinum (&quot;middle septum&quot;) is the central compartment of the chest, lying between the two pleural cavities. It holds everything in the chest except the lungs and pleura.</p>\n<p><b>Boundaries:</b></p>\n<ul><li><b>In front:</b> the sternum.</li><li><b>Behind:</b> the vertebral column (bodies of the thoracic vertebrae and their intervertebral discs).</li><li><b>Above:</b> the superior thoracic aperture.</li><li><b>Below:</b> the diaphragm.</li><li><b>On each side:</b> the mediastinal pleura.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Contents (overview):</b> the thymus; the heart inside its pericardial sac; the great arteries and veins (thoracic aorta, pulmonary trunk, venae cavae); the trachea; the esophagus; the thoracic duct; the sympathetic trunks, vagus nerves and phrenic nerves; and lymph nodes.</p>\n<h3>Divisions of the mediastinum</h3>\n<p>The transverse thoracic plane, running from the sternal angle in front to the disc between T4 and T5 (the lower border of T4) behind, divides the mediastinum into:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Superior mediastinum:</b> above the plane.</li><li><b>Inferior mediastinum:</b> below the plane. This is further divided by the pericardium into:</li></ul>\n<ul><li><b>Anterior mediastinum:</b> in front of the pericardium.</li><li><b>Middle mediastinum:</b> the pericardium and heart.</li><li><b>Posterior mediastinum:</b> behind the pericardium.</li></ul>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig03.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The divisions of the mediastinum seen from the side.</figcaption></figure>\n<h3>Superior mediastinum</h3>\n<p><b>Boundaries:</b> in front, the manubrium; behind, the bodies of the upper four thoracic vertebrae; above, the plane of the superior thoracic aperture; below, the transverse thoracic plane; on each side, the mediastinal pleura.</p>\n<h4>The layers, from front to back</h4>\n<p>The structures are stacked in layers. Learning the order is the easiest way to remember the contents:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Thymus</b> (front-most; large in children, fatty remnant in adults)</li><li><b>Veins:</b> right and left brachiocephalic veins joining to form the superior vena cava (upper half)</li><li><b>Arteries:</b> the arch of the aorta and its three branches (brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, left subclavian artery)</li><li><b>Trachea</b></li><li><b>Esophagus</b> (back-most, directly in front of the vertebral column)</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Front to back, &quot;<b>T</b>hink <b>V</b>ery <b>A</b>natomically <b>T</b>o <b>E</b>xcel&quot;: <span class=\"hy\">Thymus, Veins, Arteries, Trachea, Esophagus</span>. The nerves (phrenic and vagus) run down through the layers, and the thoracic duct lies beside the esophagus on the left.</div>\n<h4>Superior vena cava</h4>\n<ul><li>Formed by the joining of the right and left brachiocephalic veins behind the lower border of the right first costal cartilage.</li><li>It runs straight down and <span class=\"hy\">enters the right atrium at the level of the right third costal cartilage</span>.</li><li>Just before it enters the pericardial sac, it receives the azygos vein, which arches over the root of the right lung at the T4 level.</li><li>Its upper half lies in the superior mediastinum and its lower half in the middle mediastinum.</li></ul>\n<h4>Arch of the aorta</h4>\n<ul><li>The arch lies entirely in the superior mediastinum.</li><li>It begins where the ascending aorta emerges from the pericardial sac (at the level of the right second costal cartilage, sternal angle) and ends at the level of the T4/T5 disc, where it continues as the descending thoracic aorta.</li><li>It arches backward and to the left, over the root of the left lung and the left main bronchus.</li></ul>\n<h4>Trachea and esophagus in the superior mediastinum</h4>\n<ul><li>The trachea is a midline structure; you can feel it in the jugular notch as it enters the superior mediastinum.</li><li>The esophagus lies directly behind the trachea and directly in front of the vertebral column.</li></ul>\n<h4>Left recurrent laryngeal nerve</h4>\n<ul><li>The left vagus nerve gives off the left recurrent laryngeal nerve as it crosses the arch of the aorta. <span class=\"hy\">The nerve hooks under the inferior margin of the arch, just lateral (to the left) of the ligamentum arteriosum.</span></li><li>It then climbs back up in the groove between the trachea and the esophagus, enters the neck and ends in the larynx, where it supplies most of the muscles that move the vocal cords.</li><li>The right recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks higher, <span class=\"hy\">under the right subclavian artery</span>, so it never enters the thorax proper.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key.</b> Because the left recurrent laryngeal nerve loops under the aortic arch, anything that enlarges or invades this area can <span class=\"hy\">paralyse the left vocal cord</span> and cause <b>hoarseness</b>: an aneurysm of the aortic arch, a tumour of the left lung or mediastinal lymph nodes, or a greatly enlarged left atrium or pulmonary artery (hoarseness from heart disease is called Ortner syndrome). The nerve can also be damaged during surgery on the ligamentum arteriosum (patent ductus arteriosus ligation) or the thyroid.</div>\n<h3>Anterior mediastinum</h3>\n<p><b>Boundaries:</b> in front, the body of the sternum; behind, the pericardium; above, the transverse thoracic plane; below, the diaphragm; on each side, the mediastinal pleura.</p>\n<p><b>Contents (small):</b> loose areolar tissue, the superior and inferior sternopericardial ligaments, lymph nodes, mediastinal branches of the internal thoracic (internal mammary) artery, and the lower part of the thymus in children.</p>\n<div class=\"box ep\"><b class=\"t\">Exam pearl.</b> Masses of the anterior mediastinum are classically remembered as the &quot;4 Ts&quot;: <span class=\"hy\">thymoma, thyroid (retrosternal goitre), teratoma (germ cell tumour) and &quot;terrible&quot; lymphoma</span>.</div>\n<h3>Middle mediastinum</h3>\n<p><b>Boundaries:</b> in front, the anterior mediastinum; behind, the posterior mediastinum; above, the superior mediastinum; below, the diaphragm.</p>\n<p><b>Contents:</b></p>\n<ul><li><b>Heart and pericardium.</b></li><li><b>Arteries:</b> ascending aorta, the pulmonary trunk dividing into right and left pulmonary arteries, and the pericardiacophrenic arteries.</li><li><b>Veins:</b> lower half of the superior vena cava, the terminal part of the azygos vein, and the pulmonary veins.</li><li><b>Nerves:</b> phrenic nerves and the deep cardiac plexus.</li><li><b>Lymph nodes:</b> tracheobronchial lymph nodes.</li><li><b>Airways:</b> the bifurcation of the trachea and the right and left main (principal) bronchi.</li></ul>\n<h3>Posterior mediastinum</h3>\n<p><b>Boundaries:</b> in front, the pericardium and its contents, the bifurcation of the trachea and the pulmonary vessels; behind, the bodies of the lower eight thoracic vertebrae (T5 to T12) and their discs; above, the transverse thoracic plane; below, the diaphragm; on each side, the mediastinal pleura.</p>\n<p><b>Contents:</b></p>\n<ul><li>Esophagus (with the vagus nerves forming the esophageal plexus on it).</li><li>Descending thoracic aorta and its branches.</li><li>Azygos, hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins.</li><li>Thoracic duct.</li><li>Sympathetic trunks and the thoracic splanchnic nerves.</li><li>Posterior mediastinal lymph nodes.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Posterior mediastinum, &quot;<b>DATES</b>&quot;: <b>D</b>escending aorta, <b>A</b>zygos system, <b>T</b>horacic duct, <b>E</b>sophagus (with the vagus nerves on it), <b>S</b>ympathetic trunks and splanchnic nerves.</div>\n<h3>Sympathetic trunk in the thorax</h3>\n<ul><li>The two sympathetic trunks are parallel chains of 12 ganglia, lying beside the vertebral bodies along the heads of the ribs.</li><li><b>Cardiac branches</b> (from the upper ganglia, T1 to T4) go to the cardiac plexus.</li><li><b>Pulmonary branches</b> (T1 to T4) go to the pulmonary plexus.</li><li><b>Esophageal branches</b> (T1 to T4) go to the esophagus.</li><li><b>Greater splanchnic nerve:</b> <span class=\"hy\">from ganglia T5 to T9</span>.</li><li><b>Lesser splanchnic nerve:</b> <span class=\"hy\">from ganglia T10 and T11</span>.</li><li><b>Least splanchnic nerve:</b> <span class=\"hy\">from ganglion T12</span>.</li><li>The splanchnic nerves pierce the crura of the diaphragm to reach abdominal ganglia (they supply abdominal organs, not thoracic ones).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Splanchnic nerves &quot;5-9, 10-11, 12&quot;: greater, lesser, least, in order down the trunk.</div>\n<h3>Vertebral levels in the thorax (surface and CT landmarks)</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Level</th><th>Landmark</th></tr><tr><td>T2</td><td>Jugular notch (upper border of manubrium, at the T2/T3 disc)</td></tr><tr><td>T3</td><td>Root of the spine of the scapula; top of the aortic arch is near this level on CT</td></tr><tr><td>T4/T5</td><td>Sternal angle: second costal cartilage, tracheal bifurcation, aortic arch starts and ends, azygos vein joins superior vena cava, right and left mediastinal pleurae meet in the midline</td></tr><tr><td>T7</td><td>Inferior angle of the scapula</td></tr><tr><td>T8</td><td>Caval opening of the diaphragm (inferior vena cava)</td></tr><tr><td>T9</td><td>Xiphisternal joint / xiphoid process</td></tr><tr><td>T10</td><td>Esophageal opening of the diaphragm</td></tr><tr><td>T12</td><td>Aortic opening of the diaphragm</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Diaphragm openings, &quot;<b>I 8 10 Eggs At 12</b>&quot;: Inferior vena cava at T8, Esophagus at T10, Aorta at T12.</div>\n<h3>Cardiovascular shadows on the chest X-ray</h3>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig04.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. What forms each border of the heart shadow on a posteroanterior chest X-ray.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>On a posteroanterior chest X-ray, the mediastinum and heart form a central white shadow with a right and a left border.</p>\n<p><b>Right border (from above downward):</b></p>\n<ul><li>Right brachiocephalic vessels</li><li>Superior vena cava (with the ascending aorta behind it)</li><li>Right atrium</li><li>Inferior vena cava (a short segment at the bottom)</li></ul>\n<p><b>Left border (from above downward):</b></p>\n<ul><li>Left subclavian artery and left brachiocephalic vein</li><li>Aortic arch (the &quot;aortic knob&quot; or knuckle)</li><li>Pulmonary trunk</li><li>Left auricle (left atrial appendage)</li><li>Left ventricle (down to the apex)</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">The right atrium forms the right heart border; the left ventricle forms most of the left heart border. The right ventricle forms most of the front of the heart</span> and so does not form either border on a frontal X-ray.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g4\">4. The Heart and Coronary Circulation</h2>\n<p>The heart lies in the middle mediastinum with the pericardium, the roots of the great vessels and several nerves. <span class=\"hy\">The heart is wrapped in the pericardium, which has two components: the fibrous pericardium and the serous pericardium.</span> (All pericardium detail is in Section 5.) The heart wall has three layers: epicardium (visceral serous pericardium, carrying the coronary vessels), myocardium (muscle) and endocardium (inner lining).</p>\n<h3>Position, surfaces and borders</h3>\n<p>The heart is shaped like a pyramid that has fallen over onto one side. <span class=\"hy\">The apex points forward, downward and to the left, while the base lies opposite the apex and faces backward (posteriorly).</span></p>\n<ul><li><b>Base:</b> faces backward toward T6 to T9, with the esophagus directly behind. Formed mostly by the <span class=\"hy\">left atrium</span>, plus a small part of the right atrium and the ends of the venae cavae and pulmonary veins.</li><li><b>Apex:</b> the tip of the left ventricle, <span class=\"hy\">behind the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">left 5th intercostal space</span>, about 8 to 9 cm from the midline</b>. The apex beat is felt here and moves down and outward when the left ventricle enlarges.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box wo\"><b class=\"t\">Watch out.</b> <span class=\"hy\">The &quot;base&quot; of the heart is its back, not its bottom.</span> The heart rests on its diaphragmatic surface.</div>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Surface or border</th><th>Formed by</th></tr><tr><td>Anterior (sternocostal) surface</td><td>Mostly right ventricle, plus some right atrium and left ventricle</td></tr><tr><td>Diaphragmatic (inferior) surface</td><td>Mostly left ventricle, small part of right ventricle, separated by the posterior interventricular groove</td></tr><tr><td>Left pulmonary surface</td><td>Left ventricle and part of the left atrium</td></tr><tr><td>Right pulmonary surface / right border</td><td>Right atrium</td></tr><tr><td>Inferior border (acute margin)</td><td>Mostly right ventricle</td></tr><tr><td>Left border (obtuse margin)</td><td>Mostly left ventricle, a little left auricle</td></tr></table>\n<h3>External grooves (sulci)</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Coronary sulcus:</b> circles the heart between atria and ventricles. Contains the right coronary artery, small cardiac vein, coronary sinus and circumflex artery.</li><li><b>Anterior interventricular sulcus:</b> anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery and great cardiac vein.</li><li><b>Posterior interventricular sulcus:</b> posterior interventricular artery and middle cardiac vein.</li></ul>\n<h3>Heart sounds</h3>\n<ul><li><b>S1 (&quot;lub&quot;):</b> closure of the mitral and tricuspid valves at the start of systole (ventricles emptying).</li><li><b>S2 (&quot;dub&quot;):</b> closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves at the start of diastole (ventricles filling).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> Between S1 and S2 is systole; after S2 is diastole. A murmur between S1 and S2 is systolic; a murmur after S2 is diastolic.</div>\n<h3>The chambers</h3>\n<p><b>Right atrium.</b> Receives the superior vena cava, inferior vena cava and coronary sinus. The <b>crista terminalis</b> inside (matching the sulcus terminalis outside) separates the smooth back part from the rough front part with <b>pectinate muscles</b>. The sinoatrial node sits at the top of the crista. The <b>fossa ovalis</b>, an oval dip in the septum above the inferior vena cava opening, marks the embryonic <b>foramen ovale</b>; its raised rim is the limbus.</p>\n<p><b>Right ventricle.</b> Rough walls (<b>trabeculae carneae</b>), three papillary muscles holding the tricuspid cusps through <b>chordae tendineae</b>, and the <b>septomarginal trabecula (moderator band)</b>, <span class=\"hy\">which carries the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">right bundle branch</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> to the anterior papillary muscle</span>. Its smooth outflow is the <b>conus arteriosus (infundibulum)</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Left atrium.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Forms most of the base of the heart.</span> Four valveless pulmonary veins bring oxygenated blood into its smooth-walled part. Its tubular <b>left auricle</b> has pectinate muscles and overlaps the pulmonary trunk. It lies directly in front of the esophagus.</p>\n<p><b>Left ventricle.</b> Forms the apex and most of the left border; its wall is two to three times thicker than the right&#x27;s. Two papillary muscles hold the mitral (bicuspid) valve; the smooth outflow below the aortic valve is the aortic vestibule.</p>\n<h3>Valves and the fibrous skeleton</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Pulmonary valve:</b> at the top of the conus arteriosus, level with the <b>left 3rd costal cartilage</b>; three semilunar cusps (anterior, right, left).</li><li><b>Aortic valve:</b> right, left and posterior cusps and sinuses. <b>The right coronary artery arises from the right aortic sinus and the left coronary from the left sinus</b>; the posterior sinus has no artery (non-coronary sinus). <span class=\"hy\">The coronaries fill during diastole.</span></li><li><b>Fibrous skeleton (annulus fibrosus):</b> fibrous rings around the four valves. It supports the openings, stops them over-stretching, anchors the cusps and <b><span class=\"hy\">electrically insulates atria from ventricles</span></b>, so the impulse can only pass through the atrioventricular bundle. The <b>right fibrous trigone</b> (between the aortic and tricuspid rings) is pierced by that bundle; the <b>left fibrous trigone</b> lies between the aortic and mitral rings.</li></ul>\n<h3>Auscultation areas</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Valve</th><th>Where to listen</th></tr><tr><td>Aortic</td><td>2nd intercostal space, right sternal border</td></tr><tr><td>Pulmonary</td><td>2nd intercostal space, left sternal border</td></tr><tr><td>Tricuspid</td><td>4th/5th intercostal space, lower left sternal border</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral</td><td>5th intercostal space, left midclavicular line (apex)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>All Physicians Take Money</b>&quot;: Aortic, Pulmonary, Tricuspid, Mitral, moving from the upper right chest to the apex.</div>\n<h3>Valve diseases</h3>\n<p><b>Stenosis</b> means a narrowed opening; <b>incompetence</b> (regurgitation) means the valve leaks backward.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Valve problem</th><th>Murmur timing</th><th>Key points</th></tr><tr><td>Tricuspid incompetence</td><td>Systolic (holosystolic)</td><td>Enlarged cusps; blood leaks back into right atrium</td></tr><tr><td>Tricuspid stenosis</td><td>Diastolic</td><td>Narrowed opening</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral valve prolapse</td><td>Systolic (mid-systolic click, late murmur)</td><td>Cusp bulges into left atrium; chest pain, fatigue</td></tr><tr><td>Mitral stenosis</td><td>Diastolic (opening snap, mid-diastolic rumble)</td><td>Usually after rheumatic fever; enlarged left atrium</td></tr><tr><td>Pulmonary incompetence</td><td>Diastolic</td><td>Thickened, stiff cusps</td></tr><tr><td>Pulmonary stenosis</td><td>Systolic ejection</td><td>Fused cusps; right ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic stenosis</td><td>Systolic ejection, radiates to carotids</td><td>Rheumatic fever or calcification; left ventricular hypertrophy</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic incompetence</td><td>Early diastolic</td><td>Collapsing (&quot;water-hammer&quot;) pulse</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> Leaking atrioventricular valves (mitral, tricuspid) and narrowed semilunar valves (aortic, pulmonary) are <b>systolic</b>. Narrowed atrioventricular valves and leaking semilunar valves are <b>diastolic</b>.</div>\n<h3>Coronary circulation</h3>\n<p>The <b>right and left coronary arteries</b> arise from the aortic sinuses at the start of the ascending aorta and run in the coronary and interventricular sulci.</p>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig05.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The coronary arteries (red) and cardiac veins (blue) from the front and from behind.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Right coronary artery</b> (right aortic sinus, right coronary sulcus):</p>\n<ul><li>Conus artery to the pulmonary outflow.</li><li><b>Sinoatrial nodal artery</b> in about <b>60 to 65%</b> of people.</li><li><b>Right marginal artery</b> along the inferior (acute) margin, supplying the right ventricle.</li><li><b>Atrioventricular nodal artery</b> in about <b>80%</b>, at the crux.</li><li><b>Posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery</b> in right-dominant hearts: both ventricles and the back third of the septum.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Left coronary artery</b> (left aortic sinus, passes between left auricle and pulmonary trunk), dividing into:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Anterior interventricular (left anterior descending) artery:</b> to the apex; front of both ventricles and the <b>front two-thirds of the septum</b>; gives diagonal and septal branches.</li><li><b>Circumflex artery:</b> around the left border in the coronary sulcus; gives the <b>left (obtuse) marginal artery</b>; supplies the sinoatrial node in about 40%.</li></ul>\n<p><b><span class=\"hy\">Dominance</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> is decided by </span><b><span class=\"hy\">which artery gives the</span> posterior interventricular artery</b>: right dominant 67 to 85%, left dominant (circumflex) 8 to 15%, codominant 7 to 18%.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Artery blocked</th><th>Territory affected</th><th>Clue</th></tr><tr><td>Anterior interventricular (most commonly blocked)</td><td>Front of left ventricle, front 2/3 of septum, apex</td><td>Anterior wall infarction</td></tr><tr><td>Right coronary</td><td>Right ventricle, inferior wall, back 1/3 of septum, sinoatrial and atrioventricular nodes</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Inferior infarction, often with slow heart rate or heart block</span></td></tr><tr><td>Circumflex</td><td>Lateral and back of left ventricle</td><td>Lateral wall infarction</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Order of occlusion frequency: </span><b><span class=\"hy\">anterior interventricular &gt; right coronary &gt; circumflex.</span></b></div>\n<h4>Cardiac veins</h4>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">Most venous blood drains into the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">coronary sinus</span></b> (back of the coronary sulcus), which opens into the right atrium.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Great cardiac vein:</b> with the anterior interventricular artery → coronary sinus.</li><li><b>Middle cardiac vein:</b> with the posterior interventricular artery → coronary sinus.</li><li><b>Small cardiac vein:</b> with the right marginal artery → coronary sinus.</li><li><b>Anterior cardiac veins</b> and <b>smallest cardiac veins</b> drain straight into the chambers.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nerve supply</h3>\n<p>The <b>cardiac plexus</b> carries both autonomic divisions; the heart beats on its own, and the nerves only change rate and force.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Sympathetic (T1 to T4):</b> increase heart rate and force; dilate the coronaries.</li><li><b>Parasympathetic (vagus, cranial nerve X):</b> decrease heart rate and force.</li><li><b>Superficial cardiac plexus:</b> below the aortic arch, in front of the right pulmonary artery. <b>Deep cardiac plexus:</b> in front of the tracheal bifurcation, behind the arch.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Pain fibres</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> travel with the sympathetics back to </span><b><span class=\"hy\">T1 to T4</span></b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Coronary artery disease.</b> Atherosclerotic plaque narrows a coronary artery → too little blood (ischemia) → complete blockage, often a clot on the plaque → death of muscle (<b>myocardial infarction</b>). Dying muscle releases <b>creatine kinase and troponin</b>, which are measured to confirm it.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Referred cardiac pain.</b> The heart does not feel touch, heat or cold. Ischemia → lactic acid and other metabolites build up → pain endings fire → signals travel with sympathetic fibres to <b>T1 to T4</b> → the brain reads them as coming from the skin of those segments. So <b>angina</b> is felt behind the sternum and in the left pectoral region, <span class=\"hy\">spreading to the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">left shoulder and the inner (medial) arm and forearm</span></b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Investigation and treatment. Coronary angiography</b> shows narrowed arteries with contrast dye. <b>Angioplasty</b> inflates a balloon in the narrowing, usually followed by a <b>stent</b>. <b>Coronary artery bypass grafting</b> reroutes blood using the great saphenous vein or the internal thoracic artery.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Coarctation of the aorta</b> is a birth defect in which part of the aorta is too narrow, usually near the ligamentum arteriosum (details in Sections 10 and 11).</div>\n<h2 id=\"g5\">5. Pericardium</h2>\n<h3>Where it is</h3>\n<p>The pericardium lies in the middle mediastinum, the central compartment of the chest, which contains the pericardium, the heart, the roots of the great vessels, nerves and smaller vessels. (The mediastinum&#x27;s boundaries are in Section 3: sternum in front, vertebral column behind, superior thoracic aperture above, diaphragm below, mediastinal pleura on each side.)</p>\n<h3>What it is</h3>\n<p>The pericardium is a <b>fibroserous sac</b> that encloses the heart and the roots of its great vessels. It has two components:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Fibrous pericardium:</b> the tough outer bag.</li><li><b>Serous pericardium:</b> a thin, slippery double membrane inside the fibrous bag.</li></ul>\n<h3>Fibrous pericardium</h3>\n<ul><li>A cone-shaped bag of dense connective tissue.</li><li><b>Base:</b> sits on the diaphragm, attached firmly to the <b>central tendon</b> (and a small muscular area of the diaphragm on the left side).</li><li><b>Apex (top):</b> blends with the outer coat (adventitia) of the great vessels.</li><li><b>In front:</b> attached to the back of the sternum by the <b>sternopericardial ligaments</b>.</li><li>Because it is tough and does not stretch, it stops the heart from over-filling, but it also means that <span class=\"hy\">fluid collecting quickly inside the sac squeezes the heart (tamponade)</span>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Serous pericardium</h3>\n<p>It has two layers that are continuous with each other:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Parietal layer:</b> lines the inner surface of the fibrous pericardium (fused to it).</li><li><b>Visceral layer (epicardium):</b> covers the surface of the heart itself.</li><li><b>Pericardial cavity (space):</b> the thin space between the two layers. It holds a small amount of <b>pericardial fluid</b>, which reduces friction as the heart beats.</li></ul>\n<h4>Where the two layers turn into each other (reflections)</h4>\n<p>The serous pericardium folds back on itself where the great vessels enter and leave the heart. There are two reflections:</p>\n<ul><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Superior (arterial) reflection</span>:</b> a single sleeve around the two great arteries, the <b>aorta and pulmonary trunk</b>.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Posterior (venous) reflection</span>:</b> a J-shaped sleeve around the six veins: the <b>superior and inferior venae cavae and the four pulmonary veins</b>.</li></ul>\n<p>These two reflections create the two pericardial sinuses.</p>\n<h3>Pericardial sinuses</h3>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig06.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. Back of the heart after it is lifted out of the pericardial sac: the two sleeves of serous pericardium and the two sinuses they create.</figcaption></figure>\n<h4>Transverse pericardial sinus</h4>\n<ul><li>A passage that runs side to side behind the <b>ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk</b>, and in front of the <b>superior vena cava and the superior pulmonary veins</b> (and the upper part of the left atrium).</li><li>It develops when the dorsal mesocardium (the embryonic tissue suspending the heart tube) breaks down.</li><li><b>Clinical use:</b> in open-heart surgery, the surgeon passes a finger, clamp or ligature through the transverse sinus, behind the aorta and pulmonary trunk. <span class=\"hy\">Tightening it stops blood flow through these arteries</span> while a heart-lung (cardiopulmonary bypass) machine takes over.</li></ul>\n<h4>Oblique pericardial sinus</h4>\n<ul><li>A narrow blind pouch (cul-de-sac) <b>behind the heart</b>, behind the base of the heart (the <b>left atrium</b>).</li><li>It lies inside the J-shaped venous reflection, bounded by the pulmonary veins on each side and the inferior vena cava below. It opens below (and to the right) into the rest of the pericardial cavity; it is closed above.</li><li><b>In front:</b> the left atrium. <b>Behind:</b> the parietal pericardium and, beyond it, the <b>esophagus</b>.</li><li><b>Function:</b> gives the left atrium room to expand with each beat.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> Transverse sinus = behind the <b>arteries</b> (aorta, pulmonary trunk), a passage you can put a finger <b>through</b>. Oblique sinus = behind the <b>left atrium</b>, a dead-end pouch you can only put a finger <b>into</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Dysphagia from an enlarged left atrium.</b> Since only the oblique sinus and pericardium separate the left atrium from the esophagus, <span class=\"hy\">a greatly enlarged left atrium (for example in mitral stenosis) can press on the esophagus</span> and cause difficulty swallowing (dysphagia). On a barium swallow, the esophagus is pushed back.</div>\n<h3>Blood supply</h3>\n<p><b>Fibrous pericardium and parietal serous pericardium:</b></p>\n<ul><li>Pericardiacophrenic artery (a branch of the internal thoracic artery, running with the phrenic nerve). This is the main one.</li><li>Musculophrenic artery (also from the internal thoracic).</li><li>Internal thoracic artery (small branches).</li><li>Branches of the descending thoracic aorta (bronchial, esophageal and superior phrenic arteries).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Visceral serous pericardium (epicardium):</b> the coronary arteries.</p>\n<p><b>Veins</b> follow the arteries: pericardiacophrenic veins into the brachiocephalic veins, and tributaries of the azygos system.</p>\n<h3>Nerve supply</h3>\n<p><b>Fibrous and parietal pericardium:</b></p>\n<ul><li>Somatic sensory supply from the <b>phrenic nerves (C3, C4, C5)</b>.</li><li><b>Sensitive to pain.</b> Pain from here is referred to the skin of the C3 to C5 dermatomes, which is the <b>shoulder (supraclavicular region) and the side of the neck</b>.</li></ul>\n<p><b>Visceral layer (epicardium):</b></p>\n<ul><li>Autonomic supply (from the cardiac plexus).</li><li><b>Insensitive to pain</b> in the usual sense (it does not feel touch, cutting or temperature).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Pericardial pain → phrenic nerve (C3 to C5) → felt at the tip of the shoulder. Heart muscle (ischemic) pain → sympathetic pain fibres (T1 to T4) → felt in the chest and medial arm.</span></div>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pericarditis.</b> Inflammation of the pericardium causes sharp chest pain (<span class=\"hy\">often better when sitting up and leaning forward</span>). Normally the serous layers glide silently. When they are inflamed and rough, they rub together, producing a <b>pericardial friction rub</b>, which sounds like the rustle of silk (or walking on fresh snow) through the stethoscope.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pericardial effusion.</b> Some inflammatory diseases make the pericardial capillaries leak fluid into the pericardial cavity. On chest X-ray, a large effusion makes the heart shadow big and globular, like a <b>water bottle</b> (&quot;water-bottle heart&quot;). CT or ultrasound shows fluid around the heart.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Cardiac tamponade.</b> Because the fibrous pericardium cannot stretch, fluid or blood that builds up quickly (for example after a stab wound or ruptured heart) compresses the heart, especially the thin-walled right side, so it cannot fill. <span class=\"hy\">Classic signs (Beck&#x27;s triad): </span><b><span class=\"hy\">low blood pressure, distended neck veins, and muffled heart sounds</span></b>. It is treated by draining the fluid.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pericardiocentesis (draining the pericardial sac).</b> The needle is usually inserted just below the xiphoid process, pointing up and back toward the left shoulder (<span class=\"hy\">subxiphoid approach</span>), or through the left 5th or 6th intercostal space close to the sternum, where the cardiac notch of the left lung leaves the pericardium uncovered by lung.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g6\">6. Lungs and Pleura</h2>\n<h3>The pleura</h3>\n<p>Each lung sits in its own <b>pleural sac</b>, a continuous membrane folded back on itself like a fist pushed into a balloon.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Visceral (pulmonary) pleura:</b> covers the lung, dips into the fissures and cannot be peeled off. It covers everything except the hilum and the pulmonary ligament.</li><li><b>Parietal pleura: thicker</b>; lines the pulmonary cavity. Its parts are <b>costal</b> (on the ribs, separated from them by the <b>endothoracic fascia</b>, so a surgeon can peel it away), <b>diaphragmatic</b>, <b>mediastinal</b> and <b>cervical</b> (the <b>cupola</b>, which rises 2 to 3 cm above the medial clavicle into the neck, reinforced by the suprapleural membrane).</li><li><b>Pleural cavity:</b> a potential space holding only about 5 to 10 mL of fluid, which lets the lung glide and holds it to the chest wall.</li><li>At the hilum the mediastinal pleura turns into the visceral pleura as a cuff around the root; its loose lower part hangs down as the <b>pulmonary ligament</b>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key.</b> Because the cupola and lung apex rise above the clavicle, a needle in the root of the neck (for example during central line placement) can cause a pneumothorax.</div>\n<h4>Pleural recesses</h4>\n<p>Where two layers of parietal pleura meet with no lung between them, there is spare space for the lung to fill in deep inspiration.</p>\n<ul><li><b>Costodiaphragmatic recess:</b> the gutter between costal and diaphragmatic pleura. <span class=\"hy\">It is the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">lowest part of the pleural cavity</span></b>, <span class=\"hy\">so fluid collects here first in an upright patient</span>.</li><li><b>Costomediastinal recess:</b> behind the sternum, largest on the left at the cardiac notch.</li></ul>\n<h4>Nerve, blood and lymph supply of the pleura</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Part</th><th>Nerve</th><th>Feels pain?</th></tr><tr><td>Costal pleura and peripheral diaphragmatic pleura</td><td>Intercostal nerves</td><td>Yes, felt locally in the chest (and abdominal) wall</td></tr><tr><td>Mediastinal pleura and central diaphragmatic pleura</td><td>Phrenic nerve (C3 to C5)</td><td>Yes, referred to the root of the neck and shoulder</td></tr><tr><td>Visceral pleura</td><td>Autonomic (pulmonary plexus, sympathetic T2 to T5)</td><td>No, only stretch</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Parietal pleura comes from somatic mesoderm → somatic nerves → sharp, well-localised pain. Visceral pleura comes from splanchnic mesoderm → autonomic nerves → no pain.</span></div>\n<ul><li><b>Parietal pleura</b> gets the chest wall&#x27;s arteries (intercostal, internal thoracic, musculophrenic) and drains to intercostal, parasternal and diaphragmatic lymph nodes.</li><li><b>Visceral pleura</b> gets the <b>bronchial arteries</b> and drains to the <b>bronchopulmonary (hilar) nodes</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Surface anatomy</h3>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">The pleura reaches </span><b><span class=\"hy\">two ribs lower</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> than the lung</span>; that extra space is the costodiaphragmatic recess.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Line</th><th>Lower border of lung</th><th>Lower border of pleura</th></tr><tr><td>Midclavicular</td><td>Rib 6</td><td>Rib 8</td></tr><tr><td>Midaxillary</td><td>Rib 8</td><td>Rib 10</td></tr><tr><td>Beside the spine</td><td>Rib 10</td><td>Rib 12</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>6-8-10 for the lung, 8-10-12 for the pleura.</b>&quot;</div>\n<ul><li><b>Oblique fissure (both lungs):</b> from about the T3 spinous process behind, around to the 6th costal cartilage in front.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Horizontal fissure (right only):</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> along the 4th rib and cartilage</span>, meeting the oblique fissure at the midaxillary line.</li><li><b>Listening points:</b> right upper lobe above the 4th rib in front (left upper lobe above the 6th); right middle lobe between the 4th and 6th ribs in front; lower lobes mainly from the back.</li><li><b>Triangle of auscultation:</b> bounded by the trapezius (medially), the medial border of the scapula (laterally) and the latissimus dorsi (below). It is the only part of the back without thick muscle, so lower lobe breath sounds are clearest here; folding the arms and leaning forward opens it up.</li></ul>\n<h3>The lungs</h3>\n<p>Each lung is a half-cone with an <b>apex</b> (above rib 1, in the neck), a <b>base</b> on the diaphragm, <b>costal</b> and <b>mediastinal</b> surfaces, and <b>anterior, posterior and inferior borders</b>. The <b>root</b> is the bundle of structures entering at the <b>hilum</b>.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Right lung</th><th>Left lung</th></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Larger, shorter, broader (about 700 g)</td><td>Smaller, longer, narrower (about 650 g)</td></tr><tr><td>Lobes and fissures</td><td>3 lobes, 2 fissures (oblique, horizontal)</td><td>2 lobes, 1 fissure (oblique)</td></tr><tr><td>Anterior border</td><td>Straight</td><td>Cardiac notch and lingula</td></tr><tr><td>Bronchi at hilum</td><td>2 (eparterial and hyparterial)</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Bronchial arteries</td><td>1</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td>Bronchopulmonary segments</td><td>10</td><td>8 to 10</td></tr></table>\n<p>Both roots also hold one pulmonary artery, two pulmonary veins, bronchial veins, the pulmonary nerve plexus, lymph vessels and bronchopulmonary lymph nodes.</p>\n<h4>Arrangement at the hilum</h4>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig07.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The hilum of each lung (mediastinal surface). The pulmonary artery is in front of the bronchus on the right and above it on the left; the veins are always lowest and most anterior.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>RALS</b>&quot;: pulmonary artery is <b>R</b>ight <b>A</b>nterior, <b>L</b>eft <b>S</b>uperior to the bronchus.</div>\n<p>On a posteroanterior chest X-ray, the roots cast a white <b>hilar shadow</b> in the medial third of each lung field, made mostly of pulmonary vessels; <span class=\"hy\">the left hilum is normally slightly higher</span>.</p>\n<h3>Airways and bronchopulmonary segments</h3>\n<p>The right main bronchus gives three lobar bronchi and the left gives two; each lobar bronchus gives one segmental bronchus per segment (details in Section 7).</p>\n<p>A <b>bronchopulmonary segment</b> is a wedge of lung with its apex at the hilum, aerated by <b>one segmental bronchus</b> and supplied by <b>one segmental artery</b> running in its centre.</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The pulmonary veins run between the segments (intersegmental), and each segment drains to more than one vein.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">So each segment has its own bronchus and artery but not its own vein.</span></li><li>Connective tissue septa separate segments, so a surgeon can remove one segment (segmentectomy) by following the intersegmental veins.</li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Lobe</th><th>Right lung (10)</th><th>Left lung (8 to 10)</th></tr><tr><td>Upper</td><td>Apical, posterior, anterior</td><td>Apicoposterior, anterior, superior lingular, inferior lingular</td></tr><tr><td>Middle</td><td>Lateral, medial</td><td>(lingula takes its place)</td></tr><tr><td>Lower</td><td>Superior, medial basal, anterior basal, lateral basal, posterior basal</td><td>Superior, anteromedial basal, lateral basal, posterior basal</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Right lung &quot;<b>A PALM Seed Makes Another Little Palm</b>&quot;: Apical, Posterior, Anterior; Lateral, Medial; Superior, Medial basal, Anterior basal, Lateral basal, Posterior basal.</div>\n<h3>Blood supply, lymph and nerves of the lungs</h3>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">The lungs have two sets of blood vessels:</span></p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">1. Pulmonary vessels, for gas exchange.</span> Pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle, branching with the bronchi. Veins inside segments drain to intersegmental veins and then to <b>two pulmonary veins per lung</b>, carrying oxygenated blood to the left atrium.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">2. Bronchial vessels, which feed the lung tissue itself, like the arteries of any other organ.</span> <b>Right:</b> one bronchial artery, <span class=\"hy\">usually from the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">right 3rd posterior intercostal artery</span></b>. <b>Left:</b> two, straight from the <b>descending aorta</b>. Bronchial veins drain on the right to the <b>azygos vein</b> and on the left to the <b>accessory hemiazygos or left superior intercostal vein</b>.</li><li><b>Lymph:</b> subpleural and deep plexuses → bronchopulmonary (hilar) nodes → tracheobronchial nodes → bronchomediastinal trunks.</li><li><b>Nerves (pulmonary plexuses, in front of and behind each root): parasympathetic (vagus)</b> → bronchoconstriction, pulmonary vasodilation, more gland secretion. <b>Sympathetic (T2 to T5)</b> → bronchodilation, vasoconstriction, less secretion.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> This is why vagus-blocking inhalers (ipratropium) and sympathetic beta-2 agonists (salbutamol) both open the airways in asthma.</div>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Auscultation and percussion.</b> Auscultation assesses airflow into each lobe. Percussion: air = <b>resonant</b>, fluid = <b>dull</b>, solid = <b>flat</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pleural effusion.</b> <span class=\"hy\">About </span><b><span class=\"hy\">300 mL</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> must collect in the costodiaphragmatic recess before an upright X-ray shows it</span> (normal is 5 to 10 mL). Causes: inflammation, tuberculosis, cancer, congestive heart failure. Fluid compresses the lung → <b>reduced breath sounds</b> and <b>dullness on percussion</b> over it.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pleurisy (pleuritis).</b> Inflamed parietal pleura → sharp pain, worse with breathing, spreading to the chest and abdominal walls. A classic cause is pulmonary tuberculosis. The roughened layers rub, giving a <b>pleural rub</b> on auscultation.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. What can fill the pleural cavity:</b> serous fluid = <b>hydrothorax</b>; air = <b>pneumothorax</b>; blood = <b>hemothorax</b>; pus = <b>pyothorax (empyema)</b>; chyle = <b>chylothorax</b>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Thoracocentesis.</b> To sample or drain fluid, blood, pus or air. Needle at the <b>midaxillary line</b>, one or two spaces below the top of the fluid, <span class=\"hy\">ideally the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">8th or 9th intercostal space</span></b> (avoiding lung, liver, spleen and diaphragm), <span class=\"hy\">passing </span><b><span class=\"hy\">just above the rib below</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> to miss the main vessels and nerve</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Chest tube:</b> to re-inflate a collapsed lung, drain fluid or blood, or give medication; usually 4th or 5th space just in front of the midaxillary line. <b>Needle decompression of a tension pneumothorax:</b> <span class=\"hy\">wide-bore cannula in the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">2nd intercostal space, midclavicular line, just above the 3rd rib</span></b> (classic Advanced Trauma Life Support teaching; newer adult guidance also accepts the 4th or 5th space at the midaxillary line).</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Lung cancer and the mediastinal nerves.</b> Invasion of the <b>phrenic nerve</b> paralyses the diaphragm on that side; invasion of the <b>recurrent laryngeal nerve</b> (left under the aortic arch, or right at the apex) causes <b>hoarseness</b>. An apical (Pancoast) tumour can also hit the lower brachial plexus (C8, T1: hand pain and weakness) and the cervical sympathetic trunk (<span class=\"hy\">Horner syndrome: drooping eyelid, small pupil, no sweating on that side</span>).</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Pneumonia.</b> Lung inflammation from infection (bacteria, viruses, fungi) or chemical injury; cough, chest pain, fever, breathlessness; X-ray shows an opacity in the lung tissue, sometimes with enlarged hilar nodes.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g7\">7. Trachea, Bronchi and Bronchioles</h2>\n<h3>The respiratory tract at a glance</h3>\n<p>The respiratory system is the set of organs used for breathing: the nasal cavity, pharynx, larynx, trachea, bronchi and lungs. Air passes through a <b>conducting zone</b> (nose down to the terminal bronchioles), which only moves and conditions the air, and then a <b>respiratory zone</b> (respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs and alveoli), where gas exchange happens.</p>\n<h3>The trachea</h3>\n<h4>Size and extent</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Length:</b> about 10 to 11 cm in adults (range given in lectures: 9 to 15 cm).</li><li><b>Width:</b> about 2 to 2.5 cm (smaller in children, roughly the width of the child&#x27;s little finger).</li><li><b>Begins:</b> in the neck, at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, at the level of <b>C6</b> (C6 to C7).</li><li><b>Ends (bifurcates):</b> at the <b>sternal angle</b>, at the level of the T4/T5 disc (lower border of T4), dividing into the right and left main bronchi. In a living person standing up and breathing in deeply, the bifurcation can drop to T6.</li><li>It has a <b>cervical part</b> in the neck and a <b>thoracic part</b> in the superior mediastinum.</li></ul>\n<h4>Structure of the wall</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Tracheal cartilages:</b> 16 to 20 <b>C-shaped (horseshoe-shaped) rings of hyaline cartilage</b>, open at the back. They keep the airway open.</li><li><b>Annular ligaments:</b> fibroelastic tissue joining one ring to the next, which lets the trachea stretch and bend.</li><li><b>Membranous part (posterior wall):</b> fills the gap at the back of each ring. It is made of smooth muscle (the <b>trachealis muscle</b>) and connective tissue. Because the back is soft, the esophagus behind can bulge forward when a food bolus passes, and coughing narrows the trachea to speed up airflow.</li></ul>\n<h4>Layers of the tracheal wall</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Mucosa (tunica mucosa):</b> lined by <b>pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells</b> (respiratory epithelium). It contains lymphoid nodules. Mucus traps inhaled particles and the cilia sweep it upward toward the pharynx, where it is coughed out or swallowed.</li><li><b>Submucosa:</b> loose connective tissue with blood vessels and <b>tracheal (seromucous) glands</b>, which produce sticky, lubricating mucus that catches anything entering with the air and triggers coughing.</li><li><b>Hyaline cartilage layer:</b> the C-shaped rings and the trachealis muscle.</li><li><b>Adventitia (tunica adventitia):</b> outer dense connective tissue that protects the trachea and binds it to nearby structures.</li></ul>\n<h3>The carina</h3>\n<ul><li>The <b>carina</b> is a keel-shaped ridge of cartilage inside the trachea at the bifurcation, between the openings of the two main bronchi.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Its mucous membrane is the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">most sensitive part of the airway for triggering the cough reflex</span></b>.</li><li>On bronchoscopy, the carina is normally a sharp ridge. If it becomes <b>widened, flattened or distorted</b>, this suggests enlargement of the tracheobronchial lymph nodes at the bifurcation, for example from spread of bronchogenic carcinoma, or a greatly enlarged left atrium.</li></ul>\n<h3>The bronchi</h3>\n<h4>Principal (main, primary) bronchi</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The trachea divides into the two principal bronchi at the lower border of T4.</span></li></ul>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Right principal bronchus</th><th>Left principal bronchus</th></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Shorter, about 2.5 cm</td><td>Longer, about 5 cm</td></tr><tr><td>Width</td><td>Wider</td><td>Narrower</td></tr><tr><td>Direction</td><td>More vertical, about 25 degrees from the midline, in line with the trachea</td><td>More horizontal, about 45 degrees from the midline</td></tr><tr><td>Relations</td><td>Azygos vein arches above it</td><td>Passes under the arch of the aorta and in front of the esophagus and descending aorta</td></tr><tr><td>Lobar branches</td><td>Three (upper, middle, lower); the upper lobe bronchus leaves before the bronchus enters the hilum</td><td>Two (upper, lower)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Aspiration of a foreign body.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Inhaled objects usually go into the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">right</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> principal bronchus because it is shorter, wider and more in line with the trachea.</span> For the same reason, <b>aspiration pneumonia and lung abscess are more common in the right lung.</b> Within the right lung, the segment affected depends on posture: lying on the back, material runs into the <b>superior segment of the right lower lobe</b> (or the posterior segment of the right upper lobe); standing or sitting, it runs into the <b>basal segments of the right lower lobe</b>.</div>\n<h4>Branching of the bronchial tree</h4>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig08.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. Branching of the airway from trachea to alveoli, with the point where cartilage disappears and gas exchange begins.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul><li><b>Principal (primary) bronchi</b> → <b>lobar (secondary) bronchi</b>, one for each lobe: three on the right, two on the left.</li><li><b>Lobar bronchi</b> → <b>segmental (tertiary) bronchi</b>, one for each bronchopulmonary segment (10 on the right, 8 to 10 on the left).</li><li><b>Segmental bronchi</b> → smaller bronchi → <b>bronchioles</b> (no cartilage) → <b>terminal bronchioles</b> (the last purely conducting airway) → <b>respiratory bronchioles</b> (with a few alveoli in their walls) → <b>alveolar ducts</b> → <b>alveolar sacs</b> → <b>alveoli</b>.</li><li>Each terminal bronchiole supplies a small unit of lung (an acinus) concerned with gas exchange.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comparing the walls of the airways</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Trachea</th><th>Bronchi</th><th>Bronchioles</th><th>Respiratory bronchioles</th></tr><tr><td>Cartilage</td><td>C-shaped rings</td><td>Irregular plates of cartilage</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Epithelium</td><td>Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells</td><td>Pseudostratified ciliated columnar with goblet cells</td><td>Simple ciliated columnar to cuboidal; <span class=\"hy\">club (Clara) cells replace goblet cells</span></td><td>Simple cuboidal, interrupted by alveoli</td></tr><tr><td>Glands</td><td>Seromucous glands in submucosa</td><td>Present</td><td>None</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Smooth muscle</td><td>Trachealis, only at the back</td><td>Spiral bands around the whole wall</td><td>Prominent, relative to wall thickness</td><td>Thin</td></tr><tr><td>Gas exchange</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, a little</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Cartilage and glands disappear at the level of the bronchioles.</span> Smooth muscle becomes relatively most prominent in the bronchioles, which is why bronchospasm in <b>asthma</b> narrows the small airways so much.</div>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Heimlich maneuver (abdominal thrusts).</b> For a choking person with an object blocking the larynx or trachea: stand behind them, place a fist just below the xiphoid process and thrust sharply inward and upward. This forces the diaphragm up, compresses the lungs and pushes a burst of air out of the trachea that expels the object.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Tracheostomy.</b> A surgical opening into the trachea through the front of the neck, for long-term ventilation or to bypass a blocked upper airway. The thyroid isthmus, which lies over tracheal rings 2 to 4, is divided or pulled out of the way. A vertical or horizontal skin incision is made, and the trachea is opened between the 1st and 2nd or the 2nd and 3rd tracheal rings.</div>\n<div class=\"box wo\"><b class=\"t\">Watch out. Tracheostomy is riskier in children.</b> In a child, the trachea is small, soft and mobile; <span class=\"hy\">the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">left brachiocephalic vein</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> and the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">top (summit) of the aortic arch</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> may rise above the suprasternal (jugular) notch</span> into the neck, along with the thymus; so they can be cut during the operation.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Tracheal stenosis (narrowing).</b> Can be caused by external pressure from an enlarged thyroid gland, an aortic aneurysm, or scarring (fibrosis) of the tracheal wall after a tracheostomy or long-term intubation.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Mediastinal shift.</b> The trachea normally lies in the midline at the jugular notch. <span class=\"hy\">It is pushed </span><b><span class=\"hy\">away</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> from a side with a large tension pneumothorax or large pleural effusion, and pulled </span><b><span class=\"hy\">toward</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> a side with lung collapse (atelectasis) or after removal of a lung.</span></div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Tracheal tug.</b> In an <b>aneurysm of the aortic arch</b>, the enlarged arch sits right on top of the left main bronchus and close to the trachea. With each heartbeat it pulls the trachea downward, which the examiner can feel as a rhythmic downward tug while gently holding the cricoid cartilage up.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g8\">8. Diaphragm</h2>\n<h3>What it is</h3>\n<p>The diaphragm is a <b>dome-shaped partition made of muscle and tendon</b> (musculoaponeurotic) between the thoracic and abdominal cavities.</p>\n<ul><li>It forms the <b>floor of the thoracic cavity</b> and the <b>roof of the abdominal cavity</b>.</li><li><b>Thoracic (upper) surface:</b> convex, rising into two domes (cupolae), right and left, with a slight dip in the middle where the heart sits.</li><li><b>Abdominal (lower) surface:</b> concave.</li><li><b>Peripheral part:</b> skeletal (striated) muscle.</li><li><b>Central part:</b> a tendon, the <b>central tendon</b>, toward which all the muscle fibres converge.</li></ul>\n<h3>Origin (where the muscle fibres arise)</h3>\n<p>The fibres arise all around the inner edge of the inferior thoracic aperture, in three parts:</p>\n<ul><li><b>Sternal part:</b> two small fleshy slips from the back of the xiphoid process.</li><li><b>Costal part:</b> from the inner surfaces of the lower six ribs and their costal cartilages, interlocking (interdigitating) with the slips of the transversus abdominis muscle.</li><li><b>Vertebral (lumbar) part:</b> from the two crura and the arcuate ligaments.</li></ul>\n<h4>The crura</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Right crus:</b> longer, larger and more muscular. It arises from the bodies and discs of the <b>upper three lumbar vertebrae (L1 to L3)</b>. It is larger because it has to hold up the heavy liver on the right. Its fibres split to loop around the <b>esophageal opening</b>, acting as a sling (sphincter) around the lower esophagus.</li><li><b>Left crus:</b> arises from the bodies of <b>L1 and L2</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>The arcuate ligaments (tendinous arches)</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Median arcuate ligament:</b> joins the two crura in the midline, arching <b>in front of the aorta</b> (at about T12).</li><li><b>Medial arcuate ligament:</b> a thickening of the fascia over the <b>psoas major</b>, running from the body of L1 (or L2) to the transverse process of L1.</li><li><b>Lateral arcuate ligament:</b> a thickening of the fascia over the <b>quadratus lumborum</b>, running from the transverse process of L1 to the <b>12th rib</b>.</li></ul>\n<h3>Insertion</h3>\n<ul><li>All muscle fibres insert into the <b>central tendon</b>, which is the flat, depressed middle part.</li><li>The central tendon is shaped like a <b>three-leaf clover (trefoil)</b>, with a middle (anterior) leaflet and right and left leaflets.</li><li>The <b>caval opening</b> lies at the junction of the middle and right leaflets.</li><li>To the left of the caval opening the fibres cross (the central point of decussation).</li><li>The fibrous pericardium is fused to the upper surface of the central tendon.</li></ul>\n<h4>Height of the diaphragm (in full expiration)</h4>\n<ul><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Right dome:</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> reaches the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">upper border of the 5th rib</span></b> (higher, because the liver is below).</li><li><b>Left dome:</b> reaches the <b>lower border of the 5th rib</b> (5th intercostal space).</li><li><b>Central tendon:</b> at the level of the <b>xiphisternal joint</b>.</li><li>The level changes with breathing, posture (higher when lying down) and the size of the abdominal organs.</li></ul>\n<h3>Openings in the diaphragm</h3>\n<h4>The three major openings</h4>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig09.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The three major openings of the diaphragm, their vertebral levels and what passes through each.</figcaption></figure>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Opening</th><th>Vertebral level</th><th>Shape and position</th><th>Structures passing through</th><th>Effect of diaphragm contraction</th></tr><tr><td>Caval (vena caval) opening</td><td><b>T8</b></td><td>Quadrilateral; in the central tendon, about 2.5 cm right of the midline</td><td>Inferior vena cava (its wall is fixed to the edge of the opening), branches of the right phrenic nerve, lymph vessels from the liver</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Opening is pulled </span><b><span class=\"hy\">wider</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> during inspiration, so more blood flows into the right atrium</span></td></tr><tr><td>Esophageal opening</td><td><b>T10</b></td><td>Elliptical; in the muscular right crus, about 2.5 cm left of the midline</td><td>Esophagus, anterior and posterior vagal trunks, esophageal branches of the left gastric artery and vein, lymph vessels</td><td><span class=\"hy\">Opening is </span><b><span class=\"hy\">squeezed shut</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> during inspiration</span>, helping to prevent stomach contents from flowing back (reflux)</td></tr><tr><td>Aortic opening</td><td><b>T12</b></td><td>Round; not really through the diaphragm but behind it, under the median arcuate ligament</td><td>Aorta, thoracic duct, azygos vein (often)</td><td><b>Not affected</b>, so aortic blood flow is not squeezed during breathing</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>I 8 10 Eggs At 12</b>&quot;: <b>I</b>nferior vena cava T<b>8</b>, <b>E</b>sophagus T<b>10</b>, <b>A</b>orta T<b>12</b>. Or count the letters: &quot;vena cava&quot; (8), &quot;oesophagus&quot; (10), &quot;aortic hiatus&quot; (12).</div>\n<div class=\"box ep\"><b class=\"t\">Exam pearl.</b> Because the esophageal opening is squeezed closed when the diaphragm contracts, you cannot swallow and breathe in at exactly the same moment.</div>\n<h4>Other smaller openings</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Greater, lesser and least splanchnic nerves:</b> pierce the crura.</li><li><b>Sympathetic trunk:</b> passes behind the medial arcuate ligament.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Left phrenic nerve:</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> pierces the left dome.</span></li><li><b>Superior epigastric vessels:</b> pass between the sternal and costal origins (the sternocostal triangle, also called the foramen of Morgagni).</li><li><b>Hemiazygos vein:</b> pierces the left crus.</li></ul>\n<h3>Blood supply</h3>\n<p><b>Upper (thoracic) surface:</b></p>\n<ul><li>Pericardiacophrenic and musculophrenic arteries (both from the internal thoracic artery).</li><li>Superior phrenic arteries (from the thoracic aorta).</li></ul>\n<p><b>Lower (abdominal) surface:</b></p>\n<ul><li>Inferior phrenic arteries (the first branches of the abdominal aorta). These are the largest arteries to the diaphragm.</li></ul>\n<h3>Nerve supply</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Motor:</b> entirely by the <b>phrenic nerves (C3, C4, C5)</b>. Each phrenic nerve supplies its own half of the diaphragm.</li><li><b>Sensory, central part</b> (including the central tendon and the pleura and peritoneum covering it): <b>phrenic nerve</b>. <span class=\"hy\">Pain is referred to the shoulder (C4 dermatome).</span></li><li><b>Sensory, peripheral part:</b> the <b>lower five or six intercostal nerves and the subcostal nerve</b>. Pain is felt in the lower chest and abdominal wall.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive.</b>&quot; <span class=\"hy\">A spinal cord injury above C3 stops breathing</span>; an injury below C5 leaves the diaphragm working.</div>\n<h3>Functions</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Main muscle of inspiration.</b> When it contracts, the domes flatten and move down, enlarging the vertical size of the chest. Pressure inside the chest falls and air is drawn into the lungs.</li><li><b>Expiration (quiet):</b> the diaphragm relaxes and air is pushed out by the elastic recoil of the lungs.</li><li><b>Abdominal straining:</b> with the glottis closed and the abdominal muscles tightening, the contracted diaphragm raises intra-abdominal pressure to help <b>urination (micturition), defecation and childbirth (parturition)</b>.</li><li><b>Weight lifting:</b> a deep breath held against a closed glottis plus a contracted diaphragm raises pressure inside the abdomen, which supports the vertebral column and prevents it bending forward (flexion).</li><li><b>Thoracoabdominal pump:</b> when the diaphragm descends, pressure falls in the chest and rises in the abdomen, which pushes blood from the inferior vena cava up into the right atrium (and the caval opening widens).</li><li><b>Esophageal sphincter:</b> the right crus fibres around the esophagus act as an extra sphincter that helps prevent reflux.</li></ul>\n<h3>Clinical keys</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Diaphragmatic paralysis.</b> Usually caused by a lesion of the <b>phrenic nerve</b>: mechanical trauma (cut or crushed during surgery or injury), or compression by a tumour in the chest (for example lung cancer). The paralysed half does not descend during inspiration. Instead it <b>moves up (paradoxical movement)</b>, because it is passively pushed up by abdominal pressure and pulled up by the negative pressure in the chest. On X-ray, the paralysed dome sits high, and on fluoroscopy it rises when the patient sniffs (the &quot;sniff test&quot;).</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Referred pain from the diaphragm.</b> Irritation of the central diaphragm (for example by blood or pus under the diaphragm, or gallbladder disease) is felt in the <b>shoulder</b> (C3 to C5, through the phrenic nerve). Irritation of the peripheral diaphragm is felt in the lower chest wall and abdomen (lower intercostal nerves).</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Hiccups (hiccough).</b> Involuntary spasmodic contractions of the diaphragm, followed by sudden closure of the glottis that makes the &quot;hic&quot; sound. Caused by irritation of the phrenic or vagus nerve endings, for example by a full stomach.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Congenital diaphragmatic hernia.</b> A defect in the diaphragm present at birth, <span class=\"hy\">most commonly </span><b><span class=\"hy\">posterolateral on the left side</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> (Bochdalek hernia)</span>, through a gap in the lumbocostal region. Abdominal organs move into the chest, compress the developing lung and cause <b><span class=\"hy\">pulmonary hypoplasia</span></b> (an underdeveloped lung), leading to severe breathing difficulty at birth with a sunken (scaphoid) abdomen. A less common <b>anterior parasternal hernia</b> (Morgagni hernia) passes through the sternocostal triangle.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Hiatal (hiatus) hernia.</b> Part of the stomach slides up through the <b>esophageal opening</b> into the chest. <span class=\"hy\">In the more common </span><b><span class=\"hy\">sliding</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> type</span>, the junction of the esophagus and stomach moves up into the thorax (often with acid reflux). In the <b>paraesophageal (rolling)</b> type, the junction stays in place and part of the stomach rolls up beside the esophagus.</div>\n<h2 id=\"g9\">9. Esophagus and Thoracic Duct</h2>\n<h3>Esophagus: introduction</h3>\n<ul><li>A muscular tube that carries food from the pharynx to the stomach.</li><li>It is the <b>narrowest part of the digestive tract</b>, apart from the vermiform appendix.</li><li><b>Length:</b> about 25 cm. <b>Width:</b> about 2 cm.</li><li><b>Three parts:</b> cervical (about 4 cm), thoracic (about 20 cm) and abdominal (about 1.25 cm).</li><li><b>Muscle:</b> <span class=\"hy\">upper third skeletal (voluntary) muscle, middle third mixed, lower third smooth muscle</span>.</li><li><b>Lining:</b> non-keratinised stratified squamous epithelium (built to resist friction from swallowed food).</li></ul>\n<h3>Course</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Begins</b> in the neck at the lower border of the <b>cricoid cartilage, opposite C6</b>, as the continuation of the pharynx.</li><li>Runs down in front of the vertebral column, through the <b>superior</b> and then the <b>posterior</b> mediastinum.</li><li><b>In the superior mediastinum:</b> lies behind the trachea and the arch of the aorta (the arch is to its left).</li><li><b>In the posterior mediastinum:</b> lies in front of and to the right of the descending aorta at first.</li><li>Lower down (about <b>T7 to T8</b>), it moves forward and to the left, <b>crossing in front of the descending aorta</b>.</li><li><b>Pierces the diaphragm at T10</b> through the esophageal opening.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Ends at T11</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> by entering the cardiac orifice of the stomach</span>, about 2.5 cm to the left of the midline.</li></ul>\n<h4>Curvatures</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Front-to-back (anteroposterior) curves:</b> follow the cervical and thoracic curves of the vertebral column.</li><li><b>Side-to-side (lateral) curves:</b> the esophagus starts in the midline (C6), <b>deviates to the left</b> as it passes into the root of the neck, <b>returns to the midline at about T5</b>, then <b>deviates to the left again from about T7</b> as it moves forward to reach the esophageal opening.</li></ul>\n<h4>The four constrictions</h4>\n<figure class=\"gfig\"><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"figs/guide/thx-fig10.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Figure. The four narrowings of the esophagus, their vertebral levels and their distance from the upper incisor teeth.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>The esophagus is narrowed at four places where something presses on it. These are measured from the upper incisor teeth, which is how an endoscopist knows where the scope is:</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Constriction</th><th>Vertebral level</th><th>Distance from incisor teeth</th></tr><tr><td>1. Where it begins (cricopharyngeus, at the cricoid cartilage)</td><td>C6</td><td>About 15 cm</td></tr><tr><td>2. Where it is crossed by the arch of the aorta</td><td>T4</td><td>About 22.5 cm</td></tr><tr><td>3. Where it is crossed by the left main bronchus</td><td>T5 to T6</td><td>About 27.5 cm</td></tr><tr><td>4. Where it passes through the diaphragm (esophageal opening)</td><td>T10</td><td>About 40 cm</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> &quot;<b>CAB D</b>river&quot; from the top down: <b>C</b>ricoid, <b>A</b>ortic arch, <b>B</b>ronchus (left main), <b>D</b>iaphragm. Distances 15, 22.5, 27.5 and 40 cm (add 7.5, then 5, then 12.5).</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Why the constrictions matter:</b> a swallowed foreign body tends to <b>get stuck</b> there; corrosive (caustic) liquids cause the worst <b>strictures</b> there because they slow down; and these are favoured sites for <b>carcinoma</b>. They are also the places where an endoscope must be passed with care.</div>\n<h3>Relations</h3>\n<h4>Cervical part (about 4 cm)</h4>\n<ul><li><b>In front:</b> the trachea, with the <b>recurrent laryngeal nerves</b> in the groove between the trachea and esophagus on each side.</li><li><b>Behind:</b> the cervical vertebrae (with the longus colli muscles and prevertebral fascia).</li><li><b>On each side:</b> the common carotid artery and the lateral lobe of the thyroid gland. On the <b>left side</b>, the <b>thoracic duct</b> also lies beside it.</li></ul>\n<h4>Thoracic part (about 20 cm)</h4>\n<ul><li><b>In front (top to bottom):</b> the trachea, the right pulmonary artery, the <b>left main bronchus</b>, the <b>fibrous pericardium</b> over the <b>left atrium</b>, and the diaphragm.</li><li><b>Behind:</b> the thoracic vertebrae, the <b>right posterior intercostal arteries</b>, the <b>thoracic duct</b>, the <b>azygos vein</b>, the terminal parts of the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins, and (lower down) the descending aorta.</li><li><b>On the left:</b> the arch of the aorta, the left subclavian artery, the thoracic duct (upper part), the left recurrent laryngeal nerve and the descending aorta.</li><li><b>On the right:</b> the right mediastinal pleura and the arch of the azygos vein.</li><li>The <b>vagus nerves</b> form the <b>esophageal plexus</b> on its surface. Below, they gather into the <b>anterior vagal trunk</b> (mostly from the left vagus) and the <b>posterior vagal trunk</b> (mostly from the right vagus), which pass through the esophageal opening.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ep\"><b class=\"t\">Exam pearl.</b> Remember &quot;<b>LARP</b>&quot;: <b><span class=\"hy\">L</span></b><span class=\"hy\">eft vagus becomes</span> <b>A</b>nterior, <b>R</b>ight vagus becomes <b>P</b>osterior, because the gut rotates during development.</div>\n<h3>Blood supply</h3>\n<h4>Arteries</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Part</th><th>Artery</th></tr><tr><td>Cervical</td><td>Inferior thyroid artery</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic</td><td>Esophageal branches of the descending thoracic aorta and the bronchial arteries</td></tr><tr><td>Abdominal</td><td>Left gastric artery and the left inferior phrenic artery</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Veins</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Part</th><th>Drains into</th></tr><tr><td>Cervical</td><td>Inferior thyroid veins</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic</td><td>Azygos and hemiazygos veins (systemic system)</td></tr><tr><td>Abdominal</td><td>Partly into the <b>left gastric vein</b> (portal system) and partly into the <b>azygos/hemiazygos veins</b> (systemic, caval system)</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Esophageal varices.</b> At the lower esophagus, veins of the <b>portal system</b> (left gastric vein) connect with veins of the <b>systemic system</b> (esophageal veins of the azygos system). <span class=\"hy\">This is a </span><b><span class=\"hy\">portacaval (portosystemic) anastomosis</span></b>. In <b>portal hypertension</b> (most often from liver cirrhosis), portal blood is forced back through these connections, so the submucosal veins of the lower esophagus become dilated and twisted (<b>varices</b>). They can rupture and cause life-threatening vomiting of blood (hematemesis).</div>\n<h3>Lymph drainage</h3>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Part</th><th>Lymph nodes</th></tr><tr><td>Cervical</td><td>Deep cervical nodes (jugulo-omohyoid group)</td></tr><tr><td>Thoracic</td><td>Paratracheal, tracheobronchial and posterior mediastinal nodes</td></tr><tr><td>Abdominal</td><td>Left gastric nodes, then the coeliac (celiac) nodes of the preaortic group</td></tr></table>\n<h3>Nerve supply</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Sympathetic:</b> from the sympathetic trunks (T5 to T9 segments) through the esophageal plexus. It constricts blood vessels and relaxes the muscle, except at the sphincter.</li><li><b>Parasympathetic:</b> from the <b>vagus nerves</b> (directly, and through the recurrent laryngeal nerves for the upper part). It produces <b>peristalsis</b> and gland secretion.</li><li><b>Intrinsic (enteric) nervous system:</b> nerve cell bodies in the wall form the <b>myenteric (Auerbach) plexus</b> between the muscle layers and the <b>submucous (Meissner) plexus</b>. These neurons act as the postganglionic neurons for the parasympathetic fibres and coordinate peristalsis and relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Achalasia cardia.</b> The neurons of the <b>myenteric plexus</b> in the lower esophagus are lost (usually an acquired degeneration in adults; occasionally present from birth). Without them, peristalsis fails and the <b>lower esophageal sphincter cannot relax</b>. Food piles up and the esophagus above dilates. Symptoms: difficulty swallowing both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food. <b>Barium swallow:</b> a dilated esophagus tapering smoothly to a narrow end like a <b>&quot;bird&#x27;s beak&quot;</b>.</div>\n<h3>Clinical correlations</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Barium swallow</b> (the patient swallows barium, which shows up white on X-ray) is used to examine the esophagus. It can show:</div>\n<ul><li><b>Achalasia:</b> dilated esophagus with a smooth &quot;bird&#x27;s beak&quot; narrowing at the bottom.</li><li><b>Enlarged left atrium (for example in mitral stenosis):</b> the esophagus is pushed backward and indented from in front, causing difficulty swallowing.</li><li><b>Esophageal strictures:</b> smooth narrowings, for example after swallowing caustic (corrosive) substances or from long-standing acid reflux.</li><li><b>Carcinoma:</b> an irregular, ragged narrowing with &quot;shouldered&quot; edges (an apple-core appearance).</li></ul>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.</b> These are common congenital anomalies caused by <b>failure of the tracheoesophageal septum</b> to separate the trachea from the esophagus properly. <b>Atresia</b> means a passage is closed or absent, so the upper esophagus ends blindly. A <b>fistula</b> is an abnormal connection between two hollow organs, here the trachea and esophagus. The <b>most common type</b> (about 85%) is a blind upper esophageal pouch with a <b>fistula between the trachea and the lower esophagus</b>. Clues: <b>polyhydramnios</b> before birth (the fetus cannot swallow amniotic fluid), drooling and frothing at the mouth, choking, coughing and cyanosis with the first feed, a feeding tube that cannot be passed into the stomach, and air in the stomach on X-ray (from the lower fistula).</div>\n<h3>Thoracic duct</h3>\n<h4>Overview</h4>\n<ul><li>The <b>largest lymph vessel</b> in the body. It carries <b>chyle</b> (milky lymph full of fat absorbed from the intestine) and most of the body&#x27;s lymph back to the blood.</li><li><b>Length:</b> about 45 cm. <b>Width:</b> about 0.5 cm.</li><li>It looks <b>beaded</b> because it has many valves inside it.</li><li>It runs from the upper abdomen (lower border of T12) to the root of the neck, passing through the posterior and superior mediastinum.</li></ul>\n<h4>Area drained</h4>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">The thoracic duct drains lymph from the whole body except:</span></p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The right side of the head and neck</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The right upper limb</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The right lung and right side of the chest wall</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The right side of the heart and the upper (convex) surface of the liver</span></li></ul>\n<p>These areas drain instead into the <b>right lymphatic duct</b>, which empties into the junction of the right internal jugular and right subclavian veins.</p>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> <span class=\"hy\">Thoracic duct = </span><b><span class=\"hy\">everything below the diaphragm</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> (both sides) </span><b><span class=\"hy\">plus the left half above the diaphragm</span></b>. Right lymphatic duct = <b>right upper quarter of the body</b> (right head, neck, arm and chest).</div>\n<h4>Course</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Begins</b> in the abdomen at the lower border of T12 as the upward continuation of the <b>cisterna chyli</b> (a sac collecting lymph from the abdomen and lower limbs, lying at about L1 to L2, beside the aorta).</li><li><b>Enters the thorax</b> through the <b>aortic opening of the diaphragm (T12)</b>, between the aorta (on its left) and the azygos vein (on its right).</li><li><b>Ascends in the posterior mediastinum</b> on the right of the midline, behind the esophagus, between the descending aorta and the azygos vein.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">At T5</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> (about T4 to T6), it </span><b><span class=\"hy\">crosses to the left</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> behind the esophagus</span> and continues up in the superior mediastinum, along the left side of the esophagus.</li><li><b>At C7</b> (root of the neck), it arches laterally (outward) and then downward, in front of the left subclavian artery.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Ends</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> by opening into the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">angle formed by the union of the left internal jugular vein and the left subclavian vein</span></b> (the left venous angle). A pair of valves at its end stops blood flowing back into it.</li></ul>\n<h4>Tributaries</h4>\n<p>Below the diaphragm, both halves of the body drain into it through the cisterna chyli (intestines, lower limbs, upper lumbar nodes). Above the diaphragm it receives the posterior intercostal and posterior mediastinal nodes, then the <b>left subclavian trunk</b> (left arm and axilla), <b>left jugular trunk</b> (left head and neck) and <b>left bronchomediastinal trunk</b> (left lung and left side of the heart).</p>\n<h3>Clinical keys: thoracic duct</h3>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Chylothorax.</b> The thoracic duct can be torn during chest surgery (for example lung or esophageal surgery) or by trauma. Milky, fat-rich chyle then leaks into the pleural cavity. Because the duct crosses from right to left at T5, <span class=\"hy\">injury </span><b><span class=\"hy\">below T5</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> usually causes a </span><b><span class=\"hy\">right</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> chylothorax</span> and injury <b>above T5</b> a <b>left</b> chylothorax.</div>\n<div class=\"box ck\"><b class=\"t\">Clinical key. Obstruction by filariasis.</b> The parasitic worm <b>Wuchereria bancrofti</b> blocks lymph vessels. Blocked chyle can leak into the pleural cavity (<b>chylothorax</b>), the peritoneal cavity (<b>chyloperitoneum / chylous ascites</b>), the urinary tract (<b>chyluria</b>, in which lymph leaks into the urine and turns it milky white), or the tunica vaginalis around the testis (<b>chylocele</b>).</div>\n<h2 id=\"g10\">10. Radiology of the Thorax</h2>\n<h3>How a plain X-ray works</h3>\n<p>Think of a lamp shining on your finger held over a desk. The lamp is the X-ray tube, your finger is the patient and the desk is the film (detector).</p>\n<ul><li>Wherever X-rays <b>reach the film</b>, the film turns <b>dark (black)</b>.</li><li>A <b>dense object</b> (bone, metal) stops most X-rays, so little reaches the film, and the object appears <b>white</b> (radiopaque).</li><li>A <b>less dense object</b> (air) lets most X-rays through, so it appears <b>black</b> (radiolucent).</li></ul>\n<h3>The five radiographic densities</h3>\n<p>From darkest to brightest:</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Density</th><th>Appearance</th><th>Examples in the chest</th></tr><tr><td>Air (gas)</td><td>Black</td><td>Lungs, trachea and main bronchi, gastric air bubble under the left diaphragm</td></tr><tr><td>Fat</td><td>Dark grey</td><td>Fat around the heart and under the skin, breast tissue</td></tr><tr><td>Soft tissue / fluid (water)</td><td>Grey to light grey</td><td>Heart, great vessels, diaphragm, muscles, blood</td></tr><tr><td>Bone (calcium)</td><td>White</td><td>Ribs, clavicles, vertebrae, scapulae</td></tr><tr><td>Metal (man-made)</td><td>Bright white</td><td>Pacemakers, surgical clips, wires, jewellery, contrast media</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"box kc\"><b class=\"t\">Key concept.</b> You can only identify a structure on an X-ray if it touches something of a <b>different density</b>. The heart border is visible because the heart (soft tissue density) lies against the lung (air density).</div>\n<ul><li>A film should be neither <b>too dark</b> (overexposed) nor <b>too white</b> (underexposed).</li><li><b>Breasts</b> (fat and soft tissue) cast shadows over the lower lung fields and can make them look whiter; non-pendulous breasts can produce impressions on the lung fields.</li><li><b>Blood:</b> the heart is the most opaque soft tissue structure; the <b>hilar vessels</b> are less opaque, and their opacity fades toward the periphery of the lungs (vessels get smaller).</li></ul>\n<h3>Posteroanterior (PA) vs anteroposterior (AP) views</h3>\n<p>The name describes the direction the X-ray beam travels through the patient.</p>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Posteroanterior (PA) view</th><th>Anteroposterior (AP) view</th></tr><tr><td>Beam direction</td><td>Back to front</td><td>Front to back</td></tr><tr><td>Patient position</td><td>Standing, chest against the detector</td><td>Sitting or lying on the back (supine)</td></tr><tr><td>Heart position</td><td>Close to the detector, so little magnification</td><td>Far from the detector, so magnified</td></tr><tr><td>Image quality</td><td>High: clear, sharp, standardised</td><td>Lower: more blurred and distorted</td></tr><tr><td>Scapulae</td><td>Rotated out of the lung fields</td><td>Often overlap the lung fields</td></tr><tr><td>Ribs</td><td>Posterior ribs seen more clearly</td><td>Anterior ribs clearer; posterior ribs look more horizontal</td></tr><tr><td>Distance from tube to detector</td><td>180 cm (standard)</td><td>About 100 cm (portable machine)</td></tr></table>\n<p><span class=\"hy\">The PA view is the gold standard</span>: it shows the true heart size, the patient can take a full breath, the scapulae are moved out of the lung fields and the breasts and thyroid get a lower radiation dose.</p>\n<p>The AP view is used when the patient cannot stand (in bed, after trauma, in intensive care), for quick bedside checks of tubes, central lines and pacemakers, and in emergencies with a portable machine.</p>\n<h4>Pitfalls of the AP view</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">An AP film enlarges the shadow of the heart and makes the posterior ribs look more horizontal. This can cause a </span><b><span class=\"hy\">false diagnosis of</span> cardiomegaly</b>.</li><li>Fluid and air behave differently when the patient is lying down: the PA (upright) view shows pleural effusion and pneumothorax clearly, but on a supine AP view fluid layers at the back and air rises to the front, so they can be missed.</li><li>Lower image quality and poor inspiration mean pneumonia or nodules may be missed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Checking that a film is technically good</h3>\n<h4>Inspiration</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The patient should be examined in full inspiration.</span> This lets the radiologist judge the lungs properly. A film taken in expiration can make a normal chest look very abnormal (crowded vessels and a large-looking heart).</li><li><span class=\"hy\">On a good inspiration, the dome of the diaphragm lies at about the level of the 8th to 10th posterior rib, or the 5th to 6th anterior rib.</span></li><li>Count the ribs: posterior ribs are the more horizontal ones, clearly seen near the spine; anterior ribs slope downward toward the sternum.</li></ul>\n<h4>Penetration (exposure)</h4>\n<ul><li>On a good PA film, the <b>intervertebral disc spaces of the thoracic spine should be just visible through the heart shadow</b>, and the lung vessels behind the heart should be visible.</li><li><b>Underpenetrated</b> (too white): the spine cannot be seen behind the heart, and the lungs look falsely white.</li><li><b>Overpenetrated</b> (too dark): lung markings fade and the lungs look falsely black.</li></ul>\n<h4>Rotation</h4>\n<ul><li>If the patient is turned, the mediastinum can look very unusual.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">You can assess rotation by checking that the medial ends (heads) of the two clavicles are the same distance from the spinous processes of the thoracic vertebrae.</span></li></ul>\n<div class=\"box mnb\"><b class=\"t\">Mnemonic.</b> Quality check &quot;<b>RIPE</b>&quot;: <b>R</b>otation (clavicles equidistant from spinous processes), <b>I</b>nspiration (5 to 6 anterior ribs, 8 to 10 posterior ribs), <b>P</b>rojection (PA or AP), <b>E</b>xposure / penetration (disc spaces just visible behind the heart).</div>\n<h3>Other projections</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Lateral view:</b> the film is placed against the side of the chest. It shows structures behind the heart and in front of the spine, and locates lesions to the correct lobe.</li><li><b>Lateral decubitus view:</b> the patient lies on one side with a horizontal beam. A <b>mobile (free-flowing) pleural effusion</b> moves to the dependent side and forms a layer along the lateral chest wall. It can show much smaller effusions than an upright PA film.</li></ul>\n<h3>Normal anatomy on the chest X-ray</h3>\n<h4>Lobes on the X-ray</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">On the front (PA) view, most of the left lung field is formed by the left upper lobe.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">On the lateral view, the upper lobe occupies the upper and front part of the chest.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">Toward the back, most of the lung is the lower lobe.</span></li></ul>\n<h4>Position of the fissures</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Right lung:</b> the <b>horizontal (minor) fissure</b> runs horizontally at the level of the right 4th rib anteriorly; the <b>oblique (major) fissure</b> is seen on the lateral view running diagonally from about T4 behind to the diaphragm in front.</li><li><b>Left lung:</b> only an <b>oblique (major) fissure</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Silhouette sign</h4>\n<p>When a lung opacity (fluid, pus, tumour) touches a soft tissue border, that border disappears because the two densities are the same.</p>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Right heart border lost → disease in the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">right middle lobe</span></b>.</li><li><span class=\"hy\">Left heart border lost → disease in the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">lingula</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> (left upper lobe).</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">A dome of the diaphragm lost → disease in the </span><b><span class=\"hy\">lower lobe</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> on that side.</span></li></ul>\n<h4>Hilum</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">Made of: 1. the pulmonary arteries and veins, 2. the bronchi.</span></li><li><span class=\"hy\">The left hilum normally lies higher than the right (by up to about 1 to 2.5 cm).</span> The densities should be symmetrical.</li></ul>\n<h4>Diaphragm</h4>\n<ul><li><span class=\"hy\">The left dome of the diaphragm normally lies about 2.5 cm lower than the right one</span> (because the liver pushes the right dome up).</li><li>The costophrenic angles should be sharp and clear. <span class=\"hy\">Blunting suggests a pleural effusion.</span></li><li>A <b>gastric air bubble</b> is normally seen under the left dome.</li></ul>\n<h3>Pathology on the chest X-ray</h3>\n<h4>Heart size and chambers</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Cardiomegaly:</b> the <b>cardiothoracic ratio</b> (widest diameter of the heart divided by the widest inner diameter of the chest) <span class=\"hy\">is </span><b><span class=\"hy\">more than 0.5 on a PA film</span></b>. An AP film can falsely suggest it.</li><li><b>Left ventricular enlargement:</b> the heart enlarges down and to the left, with the apex displaced outward and downward.</li><li><b>Prominent pulmonary arteries:</b> enlarged pulmonary trunk and hilar arteries suggest <b>pulmonary arterial hypertension</b>.</li></ul>\n<h4>Classic congenital heart disease shapes</h4>\n<table class=\"gt\"><tr><th>X-ray sign</th><th>Condition</th><th>Key idea</th></tr><tr><td>Boot-shaped heart (coeur en sabot)</td><td><b><span class=\"hy\">Tetralogy of Fallot</span></b></td><td>Uplifted apex from right ventricular hypertrophy plus a small pulmonary trunk (concave left heart border)</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Egg on a string&quot;</td><td><b><span class=\"hy\">Transposition of the great arteries</span></b></td><td>The aorta and pulmonary trunk are switched in position, so the superior mediastinum (the &quot;string&quot;) looks narrow</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Snowman&quot; or &quot;figure of 8&quot; heart</td><td><b>Total anomalous pulmonary venous return</b> (supracardiac type)</td><td>Oxygen-rich blood from the lungs returns to the right side of the heart instead of the left atrium; the dilated vertical vein and superior vena cava form the &quot;head&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Rib notching and the &quot;3&quot; sign</td><td><b>Coarctation of the aorta</b></td><td>Enlarged posterior intercostal collaterals notch the lower borders of the ribs; the narrowed aorta makes a &quot;3&quot; shape on the left mediastinal border</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Box-shaped&quot; heart with a very large right atrium</td><td><b><span class=\"hy\">Ebstein anomaly</span></b></td><td>The tricuspid valve is displaced downward into the right ventricle and its leaflets are malformed, so blood leaks back into the right atrium</td></tr><tr><td>Scimitar sign</td><td><b>Scimitar syndrome (congenital pulmonary venolobar syndrome)</b></td><td>An abnormal pulmonary vein drains all or part of the right lung into the inferior vena cava (or portal vein, hepatic vein or right atrium); the vein curves along the right heart border like a curved sword (scimitar)</td></tr><tr><td>Large heart with increased lung vessels</td><td><b>Endocardial cushion defect (atrioventricular septal defect)</b></td><td>The walls separating the four chambers are poorly formed and the atrioventricular valves are malformed; it is present from birth and common in Down syndrome</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Water-bottle&quot; heart</td><td><b>Pericardial effusion</b></td><td>Too much fluid in the pericardial sac makes the heart shadow large and globular</td></tr></table>\n<h4>Pleura and mediastinum</h4>\n<ul><li><b>Pleural effusion:</b> a collection of fluid between the visceral and parietal pleura (the pleural space). It may be serous fluid (pleural effusion), blood (hemothorax) or pus (empyema); in empyema, small bubbles of air may be seen in the pus. On an upright film, fluid blunts the costophrenic angle and forms a curved upper edge (meniscus) rising up the side wall.</li><li><b><span class=\"hy\">Pneumothorax:</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> a black area with no lung markings</span>, beyond a thin white line (the visceral pleural edge).</li><li><b>Pneumomediastinum:</b> air in the mediastinum outlines the mediastinal structures and the mediastinal pleura with a thin dark line; air may track under the heart, making the diaphragm look continuous across the midline.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"g11\">11. Clinicals of the Thorax (Integrated Review)</h2>\n<p>This lecture gathers the clinical points from the whole module. Topics already explained in full earlier are summarised in the master table so you can review them quickly; topics that appear only in this lecture are explained in full below it.</p>\n<h3>Where the other clinicals are</h3>\n<p>Rib fractures, flail chest, cervical rib, thoracic outlet syndrome, thoracotomy, rib dislocation, sternal fracture and diaphragm paralysis are in Section 1; herpes zoster and intercostal nerve block in Section 2; pericarditis, effusion, tamponade and the pericardial sinuses in Section 5; valve disease and coronary artery disease in Section 4; pleural effusion, pleurisy, thoracocentesis, chest tubes, lung cancer and pneumonia in Section 6; foreign bodies and tracheostomy in Section 7; esophageal and thoracic duct problems in Section 9.</p>\n<h3>Pulmonary collapse (atelectasis)</h3>\n<ul><li>If air enters the pleural cavity (for example from a penetrating wound or a ruptured lung), the <b>potential space becomes a real space</b>. The surface tension holding the lung to the chest wall is lost and the lung&#x27;s own elastic recoil makes it <b>collapse</b>.</li><li>Lung collapse can also happen when a bronchus is blocked (by a mucus plug, tumour or foreign body): the air beyond the block is absorbed and that part of the lung shrinks (atelectasis).</li><li>On X-ray, the affected lung or lobe is smaller and denser. The mediastinum shifts: <b><span class=\"hy\">toward</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> the side of a collapse from a blocked bronchus (volume loss), but </span><b><span class=\"hy\">away</span></b><span class=\"hy\"> from the side of a tension pneumothorax</span> (the air is under pressure).</li><li>Each lung lies in its own separate pleural sac, so one lung can collapse while the other keeps working.</li></ul>\n<h3>Lung resection</h3>\n<p>Treatment of lung cancer may require removal of:</p>\n<ul><li>A <b>whole lung:</b> pneumonectomy.</li><li>A <b>lobe:</b> lobectomy.</li><li>A <b>bronchopulmonary segment:</b> segmentectomy. This is possible because each segment has its own bronchus and artery and is separated from its neighbours by connective tissue, with the intersegmental veins marking the boundary.</li></ul>\n<h3>Injury to the pleura and pain</h3>\n<ul><li>The <b>visceral pleura is insensitive to pain</b> (autonomic supply).</li><li>The <b>parietal pleura</b> produces both <b>local and referred pain</b>:</li></ul>\n<ul><li><b>Costal and peripheral diaphragmatic pleura</b> (intercostal nerves): pain felt locally over the chest wall, or referred to the abdominal wall.</li><li><b>Mediastinal and central diaphragmatic pleura</b> (phrenic nerve): pain referred to the <b>root of the neck and over the shoulder</b> (C3 to C5 dermatomes).</li></ul>\n<h3>Pulmonary embolism</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Obstruction of a pulmonary artery by a blood clot</b> (embolus), usually a clot that has broken off from a deep vein in the leg or pelvis and travelled through the right side of the heart into the lungs. It is an important cause of illness and death.</li><li>A large embolus can block the pulmonary trunk or a main pulmonary artery, causing <b>acute respiratory distress</b>, sudden collapse and death.</li><li>A <b>medium-sized embolus</b> may block the artery to a <b>bronchopulmonary segment</b>, causing a <b>pulmonary infarct</b> (death of that wedge of lung tissue, <span class=\"hy\">seen as a wedge-shaped opacity with its base on the pleura</span>).</li><li>Because the lung also has a bronchial blood supply, small emboli do not always cause infarction.</li></ul>\n<h3>Bronchogenic carcinoma</h3>\n<ul><li>The <b>common type of lung cancer</b>, arising from the <b>epithelium of the bronchial tree</b>.</li><li><b>Main cause:</b> cigarette smoking.</li><li><b>Spreads (metastasises) early</b>, because of the rich lymph drainage of the lung (to bronchopulmonary, tracheobronchial and mediastinal nodes).</li><li>Tumour cells can also enter small veins or capillaries in the lung, travel through the <b>pulmonary veins</b> to the left side of the heart and then out through the systemic arteries to the rest of the body, <b>especially the skull (cranium) and brain</b>. This is why lung cancer often presents with brain metastases.</li><li>Invasion of nearby nerves: phrenic nerve (diaphragm paralysis), recurrent laryngeal nerve (hoarseness), cervical sympathetic trunk and brachial plexus at the apex (Pancoast tumour).</li></ul>\n<h3>Atrial and ventricular septal defects</h3>\n<ul><li><b>Atrial septal defect:</b> a small, probe-sized opening in the upper part of the fossa ovalis (a patent foramen ovale) is present in about a quarter of adults and is usually of <b>no clinical importance</b>. A <b>large atrial septal defect</b> lets oxygenated blood shunt from the <b>left atrium to the right atrium</b> (left-to-right shunt), so the right atrium, right ventricle and pulmonary trunk enlarge from the extra volume.</li><li><b>Ventricular septal defect:</b> <span class=\"hy\">the most common congenital heart defect (usually in the membranous part of the septum)</span>. Blood shunts <b>left to right</b>. A large shunt overloads the lungs, causing <b>pulmonary hypertension</b> and eventually <b>heart failure</b>. It gives a harsh holosystolic murmur at the lower left sternal border.</li></ul>\n<h3>Thrombi in the left atrium</h3>\n<p>In some heart diseases (for example atrial fibrillation or mitral stenosis), blood stagnates in the left atrium, especially in the left auricle, and <b>clots (thrombi) form</b>. A piece can break off and travel through the <b>systemic circulation</b>. If it reaches the brain, it causes a <b>stroke (cerebrovascular accident)</b>.</p>\n<h3>Coarctation of the aorta</h3>\n<ul><li>An abnormal narrowing (<b>stenosis</b>) of the aorta.</li><li><b>Usual site:</b> near the <b>ligamentum arteriosum</b> (the remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus).</li><li>The most common adult form is <b>postductal</b> (the narrowing is just <b>below</b> the ligamentum arteriosum).</li><li>To bypass the narrowing, a <b>collateral circulation</b> develops between the parts of the aorta above and below it, through the <b>internal thoracic arteries and the intercostal arteries</b>: subclavian artery → internal thoracic artery → anterior intercostal arteries → posterior intercostal arteries → descending aorta.</li><li><b>Signs:</b> high blood pressure in the arms, <span class=\"hy\">weak and delayed femoral pulses (radiofemoral delay)</span>, <b>rib notching</b> (from enlarged posterior intercostal arteries eroding the lower borders of ribs 3 to 8) and the <b>&quot;3&quot; sign</b> on X-ray.</li></ul>"};
